When using `navigate()` from `astro:transitions/client` in Astro, focus is not automatically moved to a new location after the page swaps. To ensure proper keyboard navigation, you must manually manage focus after navigation.

After the view transition completes, use the `astro:after-swap` event to move focus to an appropriate element — typically the main heading of the new page or the first interactive element in the new view.

Example:

```js
document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', () => {
  const mainHeading = document.querySelector('h1');
  if (mainHeading) {
    mainHeading.focus();
  }
});
```

This ensures keyboard users are not left stranded on the triggering control after navigation. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/view-transitions/

Type the layout with the `MarkdownLayoutProps` helper, giving your frontmatter shape as its type parameter; `frontmatter`, `url`, `headings`, and the rest of the layout props then become type-safe:

```astro
---
import type { MarkdownLayoutProps } from 'astro';
type Props = MarkdownLayoutProps<{
  title: string;
  author: string;
  date: string;
}>;
const { frontmatter, url } = Astro.props;
---
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <link rel="canonical" href={new URL(url, Astro.site).pathname}>
  <title>{frontmatter.title}</title>
</head>
```

Building the canonical link: pass the layout `url` (e.g. `/en/guides/markdown-content`) as the path and your configured `Astro.site` as the base, then take `.pathname`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/basics/layouts/

Destructure the props from the `Astro.props` global in the component's frontmatter; Astro puts all passed attributes on `Astro.props`:

```astro
---
// Usage: <GreetingHeadline greeting="Hi" name={name} />
const { greeting, name } = Astro.props;
---
<h2>{greeting}, {name}!</h2>
```

That renders `<h2>Hi, Astro!</h2>`. For editor tooling and type errors, declare a `Props` interface in the frontmatter — Astro picks it up automatically — and you can give defaults while destructuring:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  name: string;
  greeting?: string;
}
const { greeting = "Hello", name } = Astro.props;
---
<h2>{greeting}, {name}!</h2>
```

A `PokemonList.astro` component should use a frontmatter fence `---` to contain its script, destructure `title` from `Astro.props`, define the `pokemon` array, then close the fence before the template. In the template, use either HTML comments (`<!-- ... -->`) or JS-style comments (`{/* ... */}`) directly inside the template. Build classes with `class:list` combining an array of strings and an object, and map over `pokemon` to emit `<li>` elements. 

```astro
---
const { title } = Astro.props;
const pokemon = [/* ... */];
---
<!-- HTML comment -->
{/* JS comment */}
<p class:list={["class1", "class2", { active: true }]}>{title}</p>
<ul>
  {pokemon.map((data) => <li>{data.name}</li>)}
</ul>
```

Treat the `error` prop as `unknown` and narrow it before reading any property, falling back to generic content otherwise — this both prevents the blow-up and avoids disclosure:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  error: unknown;
}
const { error } = Astro.props;
---
<div>{error instanceof Error ? error.message : "Unknown error"}</div>
```

Do not display the stack; evaluate the error first and render only appropriate, non-sensitive content based on what was thrown.

Add a TypeScript `Props` interface in the layout's frontmatter, listing every prop with its type and no optional markers:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  publishDate: string;
  viewCount: number;
}
const { title, description, publishDate, viewCount } = Astro.props;
---
```

The Astro VS Code Extension automatically looks for the `Props` interface, giving you type safety and autocompletion both inside the component and wherever it is used (an `export` statement is allowed but not required). Declaring the type changes nothing about how the consuming page passes the props — the syntax stays identical; a page that forgets `viewCount` is now flagged by the type checker/editor instead of silently rendering.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/typescript/

You would choose a built-in session driver like `sessionDrivers.redis()` when you want to use a proven, well-tested storage solution with minimal setup, especially for production environments. Built-in drivers are optimized for performance and reliability, leveraging established libraries such as Unstorage drivers.

The trade-off involves flexibility: built-in drivers offer less customization than a custom implementation, which might be needed if you require specific logic or behavior not supported by the driver’s default configuration. For example, adding TTL overrides or custom error handling may necessitate building your own driver.

Example usage:
```ts
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL })
  }
})
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

To configure a file-system-based session driver in Astro using an Unstorage driver, install the `unstorage` package and use the `sessionDrivers.fs()` helper with the `entrypoint` set to `"unstorage/drivers/fs"`.

Example configuration:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config'
import { sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'

export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.fs({
      base: './sessions',
    }),
  },
})
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

Add the two Trusted Types directives to `security.csp.directives` in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    csp: {
      directives: [
        "require-trusted-types-for 'script'",
        "trusted-types escape"
      ]
    }
  }
});
```

`security.csp.directives` (added in `astro@6.0.0`) takes an `Array<string>` of directives beyond the default `script-src` and `style-src`, and they are added to all pages; after the build they appear in a `<meta http-equiv="content-security-policy">` element alongside Astro's defaults. The `trusted-types` value must list the policy names your page creates (here `escape`). Integrations can read the same list via `SSRManifest.csp.directives`.

Because with `require-trusted-types-for 'script'` enforced, assigning a plain string to `innerHTML` throws a `TypeError` before the value reaches the parser — a `TrustedHTML` value is the only thing the sink accepts. That forces every write through a policy you control, so untrusted input (e.g. a query string echoed into the page) is sanitised rather than parsed as markup:

```js
const policy = trustedTypes.createPolicy("escape", {
  createHTML: (s) => DOMPurify.sanitize(s),
});
results.innerHTML = policy.createHTML(`<h2>Results for ${q}</h2>`);
```

This blocks DOM-based XSS that a nonce-based CSP never sees, since it abuses code already on the page instead of injecting a new script. `TrustedHTML` is non-spoofable, so the guarantee is structural rather than dependent on every developer remembering to sanitise.

Create `src/env.d.ts` and add top-level `declare` statements:

```ts
// Custom types declarations
declare var myString: string;
declare function myFunction(): boolean;

// Astro types, not necessary if you already have a tsconfig.json
/// <reference path="../.astro/types.d.ts" />
```

This types `globalThis.myString` and `window.myString` (the `window` alias only exists in client-side code). To type only `window`, declare an `interface Window` instead. For custom JSX attributes or CSS properties, redeclare the `astroHTML.JSX` namespace (`HTMLAttributes`, `CSSProperties`) in the same file; in plain `.ts` files add `/// <reference types="astro/astro-jsx" />`. Reuse types from elsewhere with dynamic imports (`type X = import("./lib/x").X`). Note a `.d.ts` file must not contain top-level `import`/`export`, or TypeScript treats it as a module augmentation and your global types break.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/typescript/

Pass `skewProtection: true` to the `vercel()` adapter in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vercel from '@astrojs/vercel';
export default defineConfig({
  // ...
  adapter: vercel({
    skewProtection: true
  }),
});
```

The option is a `boolean`, is available for the Serverless output only, and was added in `@astrojs/vercel@7.6.0`. It enables Vercel Skew protection, which requires a Vercel Pro or Enterprise account. (For comparison, the Netlify adapter configures its own skew protection automatically since `@astrojs/netlify@6.6.0`.)

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/vercel/

For Starlight sites, create a file exporting `onRequest` built with `defineRouteMiddleware()` and register it via the `routeMiddleware` option (a string or array of paths) in the `starlight()` integration config. The handler receives Astro's `context` object; mutate `context.locals.starlightRoute` to change route data, and optionally `await next()` (second argument) to run after later middleware. This is separate from Astro's own middleware, and the two must not conflict:

```ts
import { defineRouteMiddleware } from '@astrojs/starlight/route-data';
export const onRequest = defineRouteMiddleware((context) => {
  const { entry } = context.locals.starlightRoute;
  entry.data.title = entry.data.title + '!';
});
```

Docs: https://starlight.astro.build/guides/route-data/

Astro's built-in session cookie already defaults to `{ name: "astro-session", sameSite: "lax", httpOnly: true, secure: true }` (added in `astro@5.7.0`), so no configuration is required for those attributes. To set them explicitly (or rename the cookie), pass an object to `session.cookie` in `astro.config.mjs`; options are merged with the defaults:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    cookie: {
      name: "my-session-cookie",
      sameSite: "lax",
      httpOnly: true,
      secure: true
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

To serve a bare HTML fragment from a route in `src/pages/` under SSR mode, create an endpoint file like `src/pages/api/fragment.html.ts` that returns a `Response` with `Content-Type: text/html`. Export `prerender = false` to disable prerendering for this route. The route will be rendered on-demand during server-side rendering.

Example:
```ts
// src/pages/api/fragment.html.ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro';

export const prerender = false;

export const GET: APIRoute = async () => {
  return new Response('<h1>Hello</h1>', {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' },
  });
};
```

Constraints:
- The route must be in `server` mode or have `prerender = false`.
- Use `export const prerender = false` to prevent prerendering.
- Return a `Response` object with appropriate `Content-Type` header for HTML content.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/endpoints/

Include the `X-Netlify-Deploy-ID` header manually in your fetch, using the `DEPLOY_ID` environment variable as its value. The Netlify adapter (`@astrojs/netlify@6.6.0` and later) automatically injects the current deploy ID for Astro's built-in features (actions, server islands, view transitions, prefetch), but your own fetch requests to your site must set the header explicitly:

```js
const response = await fetch('/api/endpoint', {
  headers: {
    'X-Netlify-Deploy-ID': import.meta.env.DEPLOY_ID,
  },
});
```

No, calling `paginate()` from `getStaticPaths()` is not necessary for a collection of 8 items that fits on a single page. You can directly return the paths without pagination.

Example:
```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await getCollection('blog');
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { slug: post.id },
    props: { post }
  }));
}
```

This approach generates one page per item, avoiding unnecessary complexity. Use `paginate()` only when you need to split data across multiple pages.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

Configure `security.allowedDomains` in `astro.config.mjs` (added in `astro@5.14.2`) as an array of `RemotePattern` objects; each pattern may specify `protocol`, `hostname`, and `port`, and all three are validated when provided. Wildcards work as follows: `*.example.com` matches exactly one subdomain level, while `**.example.com` matches any subdomain depth. When configured, Astro validates the `X-Forwarded-Host` header against these patterns and ignores the header (using the request's original host) if it matches nothing, which blocks host header injection that would otherwise manipulate `Astro.url`. The default is `[]`, meaning `X-Forwarded-Host` is never trusted; `[{}]` allows all domains and should only be used behind trusted reverse proxies.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    allowedDomains: [
      { hostname: '**.example.com', protocol: 'https' },
      { hostname: 'staging.myapp.com', protocol: 'https', port: '443' }
    ]
  }
});
```

Enable it with the top-level `security.csp` option (added in Astro 6, default `false`) in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: { csp: true },
});
```

Astro then injects a `<meta http-equiv="content-security-policy">` element into each page's `<head>` whose `content` supplies `script-src` and `style-src` hashes based on the scripts and styles that page uses. Pass an object instead of `true` to set `algorithm` (`'SHA-256'` default, `'SHA-384'`, `'SHA-512'`) or add extra directives via `directives`. CSP is not supported in `dev` mode (Vite dev server); test with `build` and `preview`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Pass an array of directive strings as `security.csp.directives`; they are added to every page beyond the default `script-src` and `style-src`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    csp: {
      directives: [
        "default-src 'self'",
        "img-src 'self' https://images.cdn.example.com"
      ]
    }
  }
});
```

After the build, the `<meta http-equiv="content-security-policy">` element merges your directives into the `content` value alongside Astro's hashed defaults. You can also set `security.csp.algorithm` for the hash function, and note that Astro emits no hashes for a directive containing `'unsafe-inline'`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Create an Astro endpoint that exports a POST handler and return a `Response` object; browsers POST Reporting API JSON to the named endpoints declared in `Reporting-Endpoints`.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro'

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request }) => {
  const report = await request.json()
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ ok: true }), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  })
}
```

This requires SSR (`output: 'server'`) with an adapter, since endpoints only run on demand. Returning a simple object is deprecated since Astro v3; return `Response` directly. Point the header at your collector (e.g. `Reporting-Endpoints: csp-endpoint="/reports", default="/reports"`), typically configured via platform headers such as `public/_headers`. CSP references the endpoint name with `report-to`, not `report-uri`.

Set `security.csp.scriptDirective.strictDynamic` to `true` to enable the `strict-dynamic` keyword (boolean, default `false`, added in `astro@6.0.0`):

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    csp: {
      scriptDirective: {
        strictDynamic: true
      }
    }
  }
});
```

This applies to `script-src`. When you also scope `script` resources or hashes to `script-src-elem` (using `kind: "element"`), `strict-dynamic` is inherited by `script-src-elem` so dynamically injected `<script>` elements continue to work.

Call `getActionContext()` from `astro:actions` in your middleware handler. It returns an `action` object with `calledFrom` (`"rpc"` or `"form"`), `name`, and `handler()`. Gate RPC calls by checking `action?.calledFrom === 'rpc'`, then verify the session cookie before proceeding.

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';
import { getActionContext } from 'astro:actions';

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const { action } = getActionContext(context);
  if (action?.calledFrom === 'rpc' && !context.cookies.has('session')) {
    // reject or handle unauthorized
  }
  return next();
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/

Pass `actions.createProduct` to `Astro.getActionResult()`, check that the result has no error, then use `result.data.id` with `Astro.redirect()`:

```astro
---
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
const result = Astro.getActionResult(actions.createProduct);
if (result && !result.error) {
  return Astro.redirect(`/products/${result.data.id}`);
}
---
```

With `data.id` equal to `'123'`, this redirects to `/products/123`. `getActionResult()` returns `undefined` when the action was not called during the request.

Throwing an `ActionError` with a code like `"UNAUTHORIZED"` has two benefits over returning `undefined`: you can set a status code (e.g. `401 - Unauthorized`) for better debugging in development and production, and all errors flow to the `error` object on the action result. That avoids `undefined` checks on data and lets you show targeted user feedback based on `error.code`.

```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from "astro:actions";
// in handler:
throw new ActionError({ code: "UNAUTHORIZED", message: "User must be logged in." });
```

Yes. Install the Sentry SDK with `npx astro add @sentry/astro` (or the pnpm/yarn equivalent). This adds the `@sentry/astro` integration to `astro.config.mjs`. Configure it with your DSN, and optionally `sourceMapsUploadOptions` (project name and auth token). Once configured, the SDK automatically captures and sends errors and performance events.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sentry from '@sentry/astro';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    sentry({
      dsn: 'https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0',
      sourceMapsUploadOptions: {
        project: 'example-project',
        authToken: process.env.SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN,
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/sentry/

Generate all tag pages from one dynamic route, `src/pages/tags/[tag].astro`, that exports `getStaticPaths()`. Inside that function, load every post with `import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', { eager: true })`, derive the tags from post frontmatter, and return one entry per tag with `params: { tag }` plus `props` (e.g. the full post list). Because routes are computed from the posts at build time, a tag added to any post produces a new page automatically — no route edits. Outside the function, read `Astro.params`/`Astro.props` and filter:

```astro
---
const { tag } = Astro.params;
const { posts } = Astro.props;
const filteredPosts = posts.filter((post: any) => post.frontmatter.tags?.includes(tag));
---
```

`getStaticPaths()` must return an array of route objects: each object **requires** a `params` object whose keys match the bracket parameters in the file path (values are URL-encoded, so strings only), and may **optionally** include a `props` object whose data is passed to the page template via `Astro.props`.

```js
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { post: '1' } },
    { params: { post: '2' }, props: { /* extra data */ } },
  ];
}
```

In static/SSG mode omitting the export throws `GetStaticPathsRequired`; in `server` output mode dynamic routes don't need it unless you opt into prerendering with `export const prerender = true`. The function runs once in an isolated scope, so only file imports from the parent scope are accessible.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

Set `accept: 'form'` in `defineAction()` and validate the field with `z.coerce.date()` — form inputs arrive as strings, so the validator must coerce them. Import `z` from `astro/zod`, and call the action from an on-demand rendered page (prerendering disabled).

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  event: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({ date: z.coerce.date() }),
    handler: async ({ date }) => {
      // date is a Date
    },
  }),
};
```

`.refine()`, `.transform()`, and `.pipe()` are supported on the `z.object()` for extra date rules. Empty submitted inputs are converted to `null`, so the output type may not match your validator — use native `required` on the HTML input.

Use `z.instanceof(File)` for the file field with `accept: 'form'` on the action, and set `enctype="multipart/form-data"` on the form so files arrive in a format `z.instanceof(File)` recognizes.

```astro
<form method="POST" action={actions.upload} enctype="multipart/form-data">
  <label for="file">Upload File</label>
  <input type="file" id="file" name="file" />
  <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>
```

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  upload: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({ file: z.instanceof(File) }),
    handler: async ({ file }) => { /* ... */ },
  }),
};
```

Multiple inputs sharing a `name` can be validated with `z.array(z.instanceof(File))`, and the page using the form must be on-demand rendered.

To integrate a deploy host that doesn’t use the standard `Request`/`Response` API, create a server entrypoint that converts your host’s request format into a `Request` object and wraps the adapter’s response in your host’s expected format.

Use `astro/app/entrypoint` to build an app instance with `createApp()`, then handle conversion logic inside your handler function. For example:

```js
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';

const app = createApp();

export async function handler(event, context) {
  // Convert host request format to standard Request object
  const request = new Request(`https://${context.domain}${event.path}`, {
    method: event.method,
    headers: event.headers,
    body: event.body,
  });

  // Render with Astro app
  const response = await app.render(request);

  // Wrap Astro's Response in host-specific format
  return context.respond(response);
}
```

This approach allows your adapter to handle non-standard APIs while leveraging Astro’s routing and rendering.

Add an `overrides` section targeting `*.astro` files with `"parser": "astro"`:

```json
{
  "plugins": ["prettier-plugin-astro"],
  "overrides": [
    { "files": "*.astro", "options": { "parser": "astro" } }
  ]
}
```

A single adapter entrypoint using `createApp()` handles both development-mode and prebuilt pages because it works with standard Web API Request/Response objects, abstracting away the rendering method. During development, Astro's server injects pages directly; in production, prebuilt HTML is served via the same interface.

```
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';
const app = createApp();
export async function handler(event, context) {
  return app.render(event.request);
}
```

Import `actions` from `astro:actions` inside the page's `<script>` tag, await the call, and check `error` before using `data`:

```astro
<script>
  import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
  const { data, error } = await actions.getGreeting({ name: 'Ada' });
  if (error) {
    // handle validation or backend errors
  } else {
    console.log(data.greeting);
  }
</script>
```

The result always contains either type-safe `data` from the `handler()` or an `error` (input validation or a thrown error), so checking `error` first guarantees `data` is defined. To skip the check while prototyping, use `actions.getGreeting.orThrow({ name: 'Ada' })`, which throws instead of returning `error`.

In the component frontmatter, import `actions` from `astro:actions`, await `Astro.callAction()` with the action and its input, then branch on `error?.code`:

```astro
---
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
const { data, error } = await Astro.callAction(actions.likePost, { postId });
const showLogin = error?.code === 'UNAUTHORIZED';
---
{showLogin && <a href="/signin">Log in to like a post.</a>}
```

`Astro.callAction()` (added in `astro@4.15.0`) returns the same `data`/`error` objects as a client call; `error` is an `ActionError` carrying `code` and `message`. Calling an action directly from server code without this wrapper throws `ActionCalledFromServerError`; in a server endpoint, use `context.callAction()` instead.

Define the related actions as a nested object in `src/actions/user.ts`, then import that object as a top-level key of `server` in `src/actions/index.ts`:

```ts
// src/actions/user.ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
export const user = {
  getUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
  createUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
}
```

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { user } from './user';
export const server = {
  myAction: defineAction({ /* ... */ }),
  user,
}
```

All actions must still be exported from the `server` object in `src/actions/index.ts`. The impact on calling code: components still `import { actions } from 'astro:actions'`, but invoke them as `actions.user.getUser()` and `actions.user.createUser()` (via `Astro.callAction(actions.user.getUser, ...)` on the server, or directly on the client).

Use the `session.cookie` option (added in `astro@5.7.0`) in `astro.config.mjs`, passing an object that is merged with the defaults:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    cookie: {
      name: "my-session-cookie",
      sameSite: "lax",
      httpOnly: true,
      secure: true,
      partitioned: true,
    },
  },
});
```

`secure: true` is required for partitioned cookies (`AstroCookieSetOptions.partitioned`, added in `astro@5.17.0`) and is already part of the default cookie config. Passing a string instead only renames the cookie. For individual cookies, `Astro.cookies.set()` accepts the same `AstroCookieSetOptions`, including `partitioned`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

The `security.allowedDomains` configuration option defines a list of permitted host patterns for incoming requests during on-demand rendering. The `app.getAllowedDomains()` method returns this same list from the App instance.

An adapter needs to call `app.getAllowedDomains()` when it must validate or filter incoming requests based on these allowed domains, particularly in serverless or edge environments where request handling occurs outside of Astro's standard rendering pipeline.

Example:
```ts
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";
const app = createApp();
export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  const allowedDomains = app.getAllowedDomains();
  // Validate request origin against allowed domains
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/#securityalloweddomains

To set the `Permissions-Policy` header in an Astro site, add it to your server-side response headers using a middleware or adapter-specific configuration.

Example using a custom middleware:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro';

export default defineMiddleware(({ request, locals }, next) => {
  const response = next();
  response.headers.set('Permissions-Policy', 'camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()');
  return response;
});
```

For more control over which features are enabled per origin or third-party embeds, configure the header to allow only necessary permissions:

```http
Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), fullscreen=(self "https://player.vimeo.com")
```

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/security/permissions-policy/

Set `compressHTML: true` in `astro.config.mjs` — this removes whitespace from `.astro` components in a lossless manner, preserving some whitespace where needed for visual rendering:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  compressHTML: true
});
```

Note that since v7.0 the default changed from `true` to `'jsx'`, which applies React-style whitespace rules instead; `compressHTML: false` disables compression entirely.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Call `isInputError(error)` (from `astro:actions`) to check whether an `ActionError` came from failed input validation; when the `input` validator is a `z.object()`, the error exposes a `fields` object with messages grouped by input name:

```js
import { actions, isInputError } from 'astro:actions';
const { error } = await actions.newsletter(formData);
if (isInputError(error) && error.fields.email) {
  const message = error.fields.email.join(', ');
}
```

On the server, combine it with `Astro.getActionResult()` and render `{inputErrors.email && <p>{inputErrors.email.join(',')}</p>}` under the field.

Register fonts with the `fonts` option in `astro.config.mjs`; each entry requires a `provider` from the `fontProviders` helper, a `name`, and a `cssVariable` that exposes the font to your CSS. For a local file, add it under `src/` (e.g. `src/assets/fonts/`) and configure it with the local provider:

```astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: fontProviders.local(),
    name: "DistantGalaxy",
    cssVariable: "--font-distant-galaxy",
    options: {
      variants: [{
        src: ['./src/assets/fonts/DistantGalaxy.woff2'],
        weight: 'normal',
        style: 'normal'
      }]
    }
  }]
});
```

Built-in providers cover Adobe, Bunny, Fontshare, Fontsource, Google, Google Icons, and NPM. For variable fonts, pass the weight range instead of individual weights (e.g. `weights: ["300 700"]`, or `weight: "100 900"` for a local variant). Once configured, add the font to your page head to use it.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/fonts/

Your string check compares against the wrong string. The public pathname a middleware sees is not guaranteed to match the route Astro matches internally: a configured `base` (your `/app`), URL encoding, and duplicate slashes can all make them differ, so a pathname like `/app/dashboard` never satisfies `startsWith("/dashboard")` — the guard simply doesn’t fire, and an attacker can deliberately exploit that gap to reach a protected route unrecognized.

Stop authorizing by matching `context.url.pathname` against a string. Instead, restrict access on a router that matches routes for you:

```src/fetch.ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { astro } from 'astro/hono';
const app = new Hono();
app.use('/dashboard', requireAuth);
app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth);
app.use(astro());
export default app;
```

Pass the action to `Astro.getActionResult()` in your page frontmatter, then render from the returned object. It returns `undefined` until a submission is received; after a submission it contains either `data` (the action's return value) or `error`.

```
---
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
const result = Astro.getActionResult(actions.addToCart);
---
{result && !result.error && (
  <p class="success">Added {result.data.productName} to cart</p>
)}
```

Added in `astro@4.15.0`. Because the result is tied to the POST, it resets to `undefined` on refresh unless you persist it with middleware (`getActionContext()` / `setActionResult()`).

Create a dynamic route file `src/pages/authors/[author].astro` and export `getStaticPaths()` returning one object per author with a `params` property; read the value from `Astro.params`.

```
---
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { author: "ada" }, props: { bio: "..." } },
    { params: { author: "grace" }, props: { bio: "..." } },
  ];
}
const { author } = Astro.params;
const { bio } = Astro.props;
---
<h1>{author}</h1>
<p>{bio}</p>
```

In default static output this generates `/authors/ada`, `/authors/grace`, etc. at build time, so every author must be predetermined. In SSR mode the page is generated on request for any matching route and `getStaticPaths()` is not required. Extra data (bio text) can be passed via `props`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

You can organize related actions by exporting them from a separate file, like `src/actions/user.ts`, and then importing that file into `src/actions/index.ts` to nest the actions under a top-level key in the `server` object.

In `src/actions/user.ts`, define your actions using `defineAction()` and export them as properties of an object:

```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
export const user = {
  getUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
  createUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
};
```

Then, in `src/actions/index.ts`, import the `user` object and include it as a key under the `server` object:

```ts
import { user } from './user';
export const server = {
  myAction: defineAction({ /* ... */ }),
  user,
};
```

This allows you to call the actions as `actions.user.getUser()` or `actions.user.createUser()`.

To configure an Astro endpoint to handle a specific HTTP method like `POST`, export a function named after that method (e.g., `POST`) from your `.ts` or `.js` file in the `src/pages/` directory. The exported function must be typed as `APIRoute`.

Example:
```ts
// src/pages/form-submit.ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro';

export const POST: APIRoute = ({ request }) => {
  // Handle form submission logic here
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Form submitted!' }));
};
```

This approach aligns with Astro’s requirement that HTTP methods be uppercase, as of v3.0 and enforced in v4.0.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/endpoints/

In Astro, dynamic redirects are handled using the `Astro.redirect()` method on the `Astro` global within a page component. The redirect must point to a valid route pattern that exists in your project; otherwise, it will throw an `InvalidRedirectDestination` error.

To ensure destinations match existing routes:

1. Define all dynamic parameters in both source and destination paths.
2. Use configured redirects for static mappings (via `astro.config.mjs`) with the `redirects` key.
3. For dynamic behavior inside components, use `Astro.redirect()` at the page level only, not within child components.

Example of a dynamic redirect:

```ts
// src/pages/account.astro
---
import { isLoggedIn } from "../utils";
const cookie = Astro.request.headers.get("cookie");
if (!isLoggedIn(cookie)) {
  return Astro.redirect("/login");
}
---
```

If using configured redirects with dynamic parameters, ensure both paths have matching parameter names and counts.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/#dynamic-redirects

You can implement the `Deprecation` and `Sunset` headers in an Astro middleware function by setting them on the response object. These headers signal to clients when a resource is deprecated, when it will stop working, and where to migrate.

Example:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(({ locals }, next) => {
  // Set deprecation and sunset dates (example values)
  const deprecationDate = new Date('2024-06-01T00:00:00Z');
  const sunsetDate = new Date('2025-06-01T23:59:59Z');

  // Set headers
  locals.response.headers.set('Deprecation', `@${Math.floor(deprecationDate.getTime() / 1000)}`);
  locals.response.headers.set('Sunset', sunsetDate.toUTCString());

  // Add links for migration and deprecation info
  locals.response.headers.append(
    'Link',
    `<https://api.example.com/v2/orders>; rel="successor-version", ` +
      `<https://developer.example.com/deprecation/orders-v1>; rel="deprecation"`
  );

  return next();
});
```

This middleware applies to all routes and sets the headers on each response. Ensure that `Sunset` is not earlier than `Deprecation`, and provide appropriate links for migration guidance.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/resilience/deprecation-and-sunset/

The Hono guard is a routing-layer pattern meant for protecting groups of routes (e.g. everything under `/dashboard`); it's overkill for a single page, especially if you're only pulling in `astro/hono` (added in `astro@7.0.0`) for that one check. The Astro-native alternative is to fetch the session directly in the page's frontmatter, where it both gates the page and supplies the data the template needs:

```
---
import { auth } from "../lib/auth";
const session = await auth.api.getSession({ headers: Astro.request.headers });
if (!session) { /* deny or redirect */ }
---
```

Since the page already needs the session, one call in frontmatter does both jobs.

Listen for `pageshow` in a client-side `<script>` in your page and branch on `event.persisted`, which is `true` only when the page was restored from BFCache:

```
<script>
  window.addEventListener("pageshow", (event) => {
    if (event.persisted) {
      // restore state: reopen connections, refresh data
    }
  });
</script>
```

Pair it with `pagehide` for cleanup (never `unload`, which blocks BFCache), and reopen any WebSockets or IndexedDB transactions you closed when the page was hidden.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/bfcache/

The `upgrade-insecure-requests` directive in a Content Security Policy (CSP) tells the browser to automatically upgrade insecure HTTP requests to HTTPS, helping prevent mixed content issues on pages served over HTTPS. This is especially useful for legacy or large sites where not all resources are immediately updated to use HTTPS.

In Astro projects, you can configure this by adding the `upgrade-insecure-requests` directive to your CSP header via the `contentSecurityPolicy` option in `astro.config.mjs`. However, note that Astro does not directly support setting CSP headers through its config; instead, you must implement them at the server or edge level (e.g., using middleware or a hosting provider's configuration).

Example:
```mjs
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';

export default defineConfig({
  // Other config options...
});
```

To apply `upgrade-insecure-requests`, configure your server or deployment platform to send the CSP header with this directive.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/security/mixed-content/

To define a simple action, create `src/actions/index.ts` with:

```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  getGreeting: defineAction({
    input: z.object({
      name: z.string(),
    }),
    handler: async (input) => {
      return `Hello, ${input.name}!`;
    }
  })
};
```

Then in an Astro page, call it from a script tag:

```astro
<script>
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';

const button = document.querySelector('button');
button?.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  const { data, error } = await actions.getGreeting({ name: "Houston" });
  if (!error) alert(data);
});
</script>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

You must add an authentication check inside an action handler because Astro's action system runs on the server, but actions themselves are not automatically protected by middleware. While middleware can authenticate users at the request level (e.g., via `locals.auth()`), actions require explicit validation of the authenticated state to ensure only authorized users can execute them.

The subset of APIContext exposed in an action handler includes `request`, `params`, and `locals`. The `locals` object contains the authentication data set by middleware, such as `locals.auth()`, which provides methods like `isAuthenticated()` or `userId`.

Example:
```ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro';

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ locals }) => {
  const { isAuthenticated } = locals.auth();
  if (!isAuthenticated) {
    return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 });
  }
  // Proceed with action logic
};
```

An action that accepts JSON data by default handles structured data sent via JavaScript fetch requests, while an action configured with `accept: 'form'` processes data submitted directly from an HTML `<form>` element. The form-based action can validate form-specific input types like `checkbox`, `file`, and `number` using Zod coercions and supports features such as multipart file uploads when `enctype="multipart/form-data"` is set.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  upload: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      file: z.instanceof(File),
    }),
    handler: async (input) => {
      // Handle file upload
    },
  }),
};
```

When an action handler throws an error, the client receives it through the `error` property of the `{ data, error }` return value. The `ActionError` class from `astro:actions` should be used to standardize backend errors.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from "astro:actions";

export const server = {
  likePost: defineAction({
    handler: async (input, ctx) => {
      if (!ctx.cookies.has('user-session')) {
        throw new ActionError({
          code: "UNAUTHORIZED",
          message: "User must be logged in.",
        });
      }
      // Otherwise, like the post
    },
  }),
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/#actionerror

Enable route caching (added in `astro@7.0.0`) by setting a cache provider in your Astro config, then control caching per request with `Astro.cache` in `.astro` pages or `context.cache` in API routes and middleware. Steps: (1) choose a provider — the built-in `memoryCache()`, or an experimental adapter CDN provider (`cacheNetlify()`, `cacheVercel()`, `cacheCloudflare()`) for Netlify/Vercel/Cloudflare; (2) set it in `defineConfig`; (3) set cache directives on your on-demand rendered pages and endpoints (`max-age`, `stale-while-revalidate`, tags), optionally declaring defaults for route groups via `routeRules`; (4) invalidate with `cache.invalidate({ path })`.

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
import node from '@astrojs/node';
export default defineConfig({
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

By default, `app.match()` does not match prerendered routes, even if they exist. To include prerendered routes in matching, pass `true` as the second argument to `app.match(request, true)`.

Example:
```ts
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";
const app = createApp();
export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  if (app.match(request, true)) {
    const response = await app.render(request);
    return response;
  } else {
    return new Response("Not Found", { status: 404 });
  }
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-app/#appmatch

To build a minimal server entrypoint for a custom Astro adapter, import `createApp` from `"astro/app/entrypoint"`, use it to create an app instance, and handle incoming requests by calling `app.render(request)` to get a `Response`. Return that response in your platform's fetch handler.

Example:

```ts
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";

const app = createApp();

export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  return await app.render(request);
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-app/

Implement a custom live loader by exporting a `LiveLoader` from `astro/loaders` with `loadCollection` and `loadEntry` methods that return `cacheHint` objects containing `tags` and `lastModified`. In your pages, retrieve the cache hint via `getLiveEntry` and pass it to `Astro.cache.set(cacheHint)` if using a cache provider, or manually set response headers like `Cache-Tag` and `Last-Modified` from the hint.

```ts
// my-loader.ts
import type { LiveLoader } from "astro/loaders";
export function myLoader(config: { apiKey: string }): LiveLoader {
  return {
    name: "cached-loader",
    loadCollection: async ({ filter }) => {
      const products = await loadStoreProducts(filter);
      return {
        entries: products.map(item => ({
          id: item.id,
          data: item,
          cacheHint: { tags: [`product-${item.id}`, `category-${item.category}`] }
        })),
        cacheHint: { lastModified: getLastModifiedDate(products), tags: ["products"] }
      };
    },
    loadEntry: async ({ filter }) => {
      const item = await loadStoreProduct(filter);
      return { id: item.id, data: item, cacheHint: { lastModified: new Date(item.lastModified), tags: [`product-${item.id}`, `category-${item.category}`] } };
    }
  };
}
```

```astro
// src/pages/store/[id].astro
---
export const prerender = false;
import { getLiveEntry } from 'astro:content';
const { entry, error, cacheHint } = await getLiveEntry('products', Astro.params.id);
if (error) return Astro.redirect('/404');
if (cacheHint) Astro.cache.set(cacheHint);
---
<h1>{entry.data.name}</h1>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

The `astro.config.mjs` file configures your Astro project—integrations, build options, server settings, and more. It supports `.js`, `.mjs`, and `.ts` formats. To customize the development environment, define environment variables in `.env` files (e.g., `.env.development`, `.env.production`) and load them in the config using Vite's `loadEnv` helper, since `import.meta.env` isn't available at config evaluation time.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { loadEnv } from "vite";
const { SECRET_PASSWORD } = loadEnv(process.env.NODE_ENV, process.cwd(), "");
export default defineConfig({
  // integrations, build, server, etc.
});
```

Run with different modes via `--mode` flag: `npm run astro dev -- --mode staging`. CLI-injected vars (e.g., `PUBLIC_POKEAPI=... npm run dev`) are accessible via `process.env` in the config.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/basics/project-structure/

Configure caching rules declaratively in `astro.config.mjs` using `routeRules` with a cache provider. Patterns match Astro's file-based routing (static, dynamic `[param]`, rest `[...path]`). More specific patterns take precedence. Per-route `Astro.cache.set()` calls merge with and can override config defaults.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
import node from '@astrojs/node';
export default defineConfig({
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
  routeRules: {
    '/api/[...path]': { swr: 600 },
    '/products/[...slug]': { maxAge: 3600, tags: ['products'] },
    '/blog/[...slug]': { maxAge: 300, swr: 60 },
  },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

Enable client-side prerendering by setting `experimental.clientPrerender: true` in `astro.config.mjs` alongside `prefetch` configuration. This uses the Speculation Rules API to prerender prefetched links in supported browsers (Chrome). Links still require the `data-astro-prefetch` attribute to opt in. If the API isn't supported, prefetch falls back to the configured strategy.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  prefetch: { prefetchAll: true, defaultStrategy: 'viewport' },
  experimental: { clientPrerender: true },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/experimental-flags/client-prerender/

Set `build.inlineStylesheets: 'always'` in `astro.config.mjs` to inline all project stylesheets into `<style>` tags instead of external files. The default `'auto'` only inlines stylesheets under 4kb; `'never'` uses external stylesheets exclusively.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  build: { inlineStylesheets: 'always' },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Gate every scroll-driven animation behind a `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` media query that turns it off, because the motion is yours rather than the browser's:

```css
.reveal {
  animation: fade-in linear both;
  animation-timeline: view();
  animation-range: entry 0% cover 30%;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .reveal { animation: none; }
}
```

With the animation removed under `reduce`, no viewport-driven transform or opacity motion plays for users susceptible to vestibular issues; content renders in its static state. Since scroll-driven animations are an `optional` feature — nothing breaks if omitted — this opt-in gating is required whenever the motion ships.

The `values` property in `envField.enum()` defines the allowed string values for the environment variable. It is required and must be an array of strings.

If omitted, Astro throws an unknown configuration error because the schema validation cannot determine the valid options for the enum field.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      DEBUG_MODE: envField.enum({
        context: "server",
        access: "public",
        values: ['info', 'warnings', 'errors'], // required
      }),
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-config/#envfieldenum

Create the catch-all endpoint `src/pages/api/auth/[...all].ts` to mount the Better Auth handler:

```ts
import { auth } from "../../../lib/auth"; // import your Better Auth instance
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";

export const prerender = false; // Not needed in 'server' mode

export const ALL: APIRoute = async (ctx) => {
  return auth.handler(ctx.request);
};
```

`ALL` handles every HTTP method, and `prerender = false` opts the route out of prerendering (unnecessary if your adapter runs in `server` mode). Configure your database table and authentication methods first, per the Better Auth installation guide.

Astro treats CSS Modules like any other stylesheet — importing a `[name].module.css` file automatically applies its CSS to the page, while the default `styles` object maps original classnames to unique scoped identifiers — and inlining is decided per built chunk at build time, not per component. In production Astro minifies and combines CSS into per-page chunks (plus shared chunks split off for reuse), inlining any chunk under 4 kB into `<style type="text/css">` and linking larger ones as `<link rel="stylesheet">`. Tune the threshold with Vite's `assetsInlineLimit`, or force the behavior with `build.inlineStylesheets`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    inlineStylesheets: 'never'
  }
});
```

Use `'always'` to inline every stylesheet.

Destructure `isAuthenticated` and `userId` from `locals.auth()` and return a 401 `Response` when the user isn't authenticated — this requires `clerkMiddleware()` to be the `onRequest` handler in `src/middleware.ts`:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";

export const GET: APIRoute = ({ locals }) => {
  const { isAuthenticated, userId } = locals.auth();
  if (!isAuthenticated) {
    return new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 });
  }
  return Response.json({ userId });
};
```

`createAuthClient()` is imported from framework-specific subpaths: `better-auth/react`, `better-auth/vue`, `better-auth/svelte`, and `better-auth/solid` (a helper also exists for Vanilla JS). A typical client file just creates the client and re-exports the auth actions:

```ts
// src/lib/auth-client.ts
import { createAuthClient } from 'better-auth/react';
export const authClient = createAuthClient();
export const { signIn, signOut } = authClient;
```

Use it in an Astro component from a `<script>` tag with a dynamic import, then call e.g. `signIn.social({ provider: "github", callbackURL: "/dashboard" })` or `signOut()`.

Yes. Export both functions from the same endpoint file — each HTTP method is a separate named export (uppercase `POST`), and the file path defines the route, so one endpoint can respond differently per method:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";

export const GET: APIRoute = () => {
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ method: "GET" }));
};

export const POST = (async ({ request }) => {
  const body = await request.json();
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(body), { status: 200 });
}) satisfies APIRoute;
```

For entirely different URLs, use separate files. In `static` mode, custom endpoints require `export const prerender = false`; in `server` mode they render on demand by default.

The `envField.number()` helper supports number-specific validation properties like `gt`, `lt`, `int`, which are not available on `envField.string()`. The `gt` property ensures a number is greater than a specified value, while `min` ensures it's greater than or equal to a value. Use `gt` when you need strict inequality (e.g., port > 1024), and `min` when the minimum value is inclusive (e.g., a count >= 0).

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      PORT: envField.number({
        context: "server",
        access: "public",
        gt: 1024,
        lt: 65536,
      }),
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-config/

To define a type-safe environment variable for a feature flag using `envField.boolean()`, set the `context` to `"client"` and `access` to `"public"`, and provide a `default` value of `true`.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      FEATURE_FLAG: envField.boolean({ context: "client", access: "public", default: true })
    }
  }
});
```

This variable will be available in both client and server bundles and can be imported from `astro:env/client`.

The `astro:before-swap` lifecycle event provides access to the new page’s document via the `newDocument` attribute, allowing you to modify its DOM before it replaces the current page.

Example:
```js
document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', (event) => {
  const elementToRemove = event.newDocument.querySelector('#element-to-remove');
  if (elementToRemove) {
    elementToRemove.remove();
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-transitions/#astrobefore-swap-event

Yes, the `event.viewTransition` property exists even when the browser doesn't support the View Transitions API. In that case, Astro provides a polyfill object that implements the same API surface as the native `ViewTransition`, but without DOM integration.

You can interact with this object using its methods like `.updateCallbackDone`, `.ready`, and `.finished` promises, which are useful for managing lifecycle events in your custom swap logic or view transition handling. However, since there's no actual DOM integration, calling `.skipTransition()` or other transition-specific APIs won't have any visual effect.

Example:
```js
document.addEventListener("astro:before-swap", (event) => {
  // Access the polyfilled ViewTransition object
  const vt = event.viewTransition;

  // Use its promises for control flow
  vt.ready.then(() => console.log('View transition is ready'));
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/view-transitions/

Creating the `authClient` in a separate file ensures it’s instantiated once and reused across components, avoiding issues with reactivity and hydration. Directly importing or initializing it within a component's frontmatter could lead to inconsistent state or errors during client-side rendering.

Example:
```ts
// src/lib/auth-client.ts
import { createAuthClient } from 'better-auth/react';
export const authClient = createAuthClient();
export const { signIn, signOut } = authClient;
```

Then in your Astro component:
```astro
---
import { signIn, signOut } from '../lib/auth-client';
---
<button onClick={() => signIn.social({ provider: "github" })}>Login</button>
```

To set up Clerk middleware in Astro for route protection, create `src/middleware.ts` and export `clerkMiddleware()` as the `onRequest` handler:

```ts
import { clerkMiddleware } from "@clerk/astro/server";
export const onRequest = clerkMiddleware({ /* options */ });
```

In pages or API routes, access the authentication state via `Astro.locals.auth()`. For example, in a page:

```astro
---
const { isAuthenticated, redirectToSignIn } = Astro.locals.auth();
if (!isAuthenticated) return redirectToSignIn();
---
<h1>Dashboard</h1>
```

For API routes:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const GET: APIRoute = ({ locals }) => {
  const { isAuthenticated, userId } = locals.auth();
  if (!isAuthenticated) {
    return new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 });
  }
  return Response.json({ userId });
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

To optimize fallback fonts in Astro and prevent layout shifts, use a generic font family like `sans-serif` or `monospace` as the fallback in your font configuration. Astro automatically generates optimized fallbacks from the last defined fallback if it's a generic font family. You can configure this behavior using the `fonts` option in `astro.config.mjs`, specifying `fallbacks` for each font.

Example:

```js
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [
    {
      provider: fontProviders.fontsource(),
      name: "Cousine",
      cssVariable: "--font-cousine",
      fallbacks: ["monospace"]
    }
  ]
});
```

If you want to disable automatic optimization, set `font.optimizedFallbacks` to `false`. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/fonts/

Astro does not automatically compress text resources; you must configure compression at the server or CDN level.

To compress text responses like HTML, CSS, JavaScript, JSON, SVG, XML, and plain text, use your hosting provider's settings:

- **Cloudflare**: Enable "Auto Minify" in the dashboard.
- **Fastly**: Configure compression rules via the Fastly UI or API.
- **AWS CloudFront**: Use Lambda@Edge functions to add `Content-Encoding` headers.

For self-hosted deployments (e.g., with a Node.js server), configure your server:

```js
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { brotliCompressSync, gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  const acceptEncoding = req.headers['accept-encoding'] || '';
  const encoding = acceptEncoding.includes('br') ? 'br' : 'gzip';
  const compress = encoding === 'br' ? brotliCompressSync : gzipSync;
  // ... serve compressed assets
});
```

Set `Vary: Accept-Encoding` to ensure proper caching by CDNs.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/compression/

Call `render(entry)` from `astro:content` to get `remarkPluginFrontmatter`, then read `lastModified` from it and format it before rendering:

```astro
---
import { getCollection, render } from "astro:content";
import dayjs from "dayjs";
import utc from "dayjs/plugin/utc";

dayjs.extend(utc);

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const blog = await getCollection("blog");
  return blog.map((entry) => ({ params: { slug: entry.id }, props: { entry } }));
}

const { entry } = Astro.props;
const { Content, remarkPluginFrontmatter } = await render(entry);
const lastModified = dayjs(remarkPluginFrontmatter.lastModified)
  .utc()
  .format("HH:mm:ss DD MMMM YYYY UTC");
---
<p>Last Modified: {lastModified}</p>
<Content />
```

The property only exists if a remark plugin (e.g. `remarkModifiedTime`) is registered via `markdown.processor` in `astro.config.mjs`. With a Markdown layout instead, read `Astro.props.frontmatter.lastModified`. Git-based times may be inaccurate on hosts that do shallow clones.

Cache hints are declarative metadata returned by live loaders (`lastModified`, `tags`) that do not cause any caching by themselves; manually configured HTTP headers are the actual caching strategy applied to the response. Without a cache provider, you translate hints into headers yourself:

```astro
if (cacheHint?.tags) {
  Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Tag', cacheHint.tags.join(','));
}
if (cacheHint?.lastModified) {
  Astro.response.headers.set('Last-Modified', cacheHint.lastModified.toUTCString());
}
```

With a cache provider configured, you instead pass hints straight to route caching via `Astro.cache.set(cacheHint)` (collection hints merge: tags combine, `lastModified` takes the most recent value).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

When the user presses the browser's back button, the `navigationType` attribute in lifecycle events like `astro:before-preparation` reports `"popstate"`.

This differs from a navigation that creates a new history entry (such as clicking a link with `data-astro-history="push"`) which reports `"pushstate"` or `"replacestate"` depending on how the navigation was initiated. The `"popstate"` type indicates that the navigation was triggered by the browser's history stack, not by user interaction with links.

Example:
```ts
window.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', (event) => {
  console.log(event.navigationType); // "popstate" when using back button
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-transitions/#navigationtype

In Astro v7, the default `compressHTML` behavior changed from `true` (HTML-aware compression) to `'jsx'` (whitespace stripping using JSX rules), which may affect how inline elements are rendered. To prevent visual issues, inspect your site and explicitly add spaces between inline elements using `{" "}` if needed.

To restore the previous behavior, set `compressHTML: true` in your Astro configuration:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  compressHTML: true,
});
```

Alternatively, disable compression entirely with `compressHTML: false`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v7/

In an Astro project configured with `output: 'server'`, `export const prerender = false` is recommended for the Better Auth API route because it ensures the route is handled dynamically at runtime, not during static generation. This is required for authentication flows that depend on request context like headers or cookies.

The Better Auth handler needs access to incoming requests and their metadata (like Authorization headers) which are only available during server-side rendering or on-demand rendering. Prerendering would prevent this dynamic behavior, breaking the auth flow.

Example:
```ts
// src/pages/api/auth/[...all].ts
import { auth } from "../../../lib/auth";
export const prerender = false; // Required for runtime request handling
export const ALL: APIRoute = async (ctx) => {
  return auth.handler(ctx.request);
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

You can implement the `No-Vary-Search` response header in Astro by setting it at the edge or via a server-side middleware, telling caches and browsers which query parameters don't affect the response. This improves cache hit rates and prefetch accuracy.

Example implementation using a custom server middleware (Node.js runtime):

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro';

export default defineMiddleware(({ request, locals }, next) => {
  const url = new URL(request.url);
  if (url.pathname === '/products') {
    // Tell the cache that UTM params don't vary the response
    return new Response(next(), {
      headers: {
        'No-Vary-Search': 'params=("utm_source" "utm_medium" "utm_campaign")',
      },
    });
  }
  return next();
});
```

This header should be set in your CDN or reverse proxy configuration for best effect, as it applies to all requests handled by that layer.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/no-vary-search/

To set `No-Vary-Search` headers for a specific route in Astro using middleware, create a middleware function that checks the request URL and adds the header using `context.locals` or by modifying the response directly.

Example:

```js
// src/middleware.js
export function onRequest(context, next) {
  if (context.url.pathname.startsWith('/products')) {
    const response = next();
    response.headers.set('No-Vary-Search', 'params=("utm_source" "utm_medium" "utm_campaign")');
    return response;
  }
  return next();
}
```

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/no-vary-search/

Set `image.endpoint` as an object with `route` and `entrypoint` (since Astro 5; in Astro 4.x this was a plain string path):

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    endpoint: {
      route: '/custom_endpoint',
      entrypoint: 'src/my_endpoint.ts',
    },
  },
});
```

The default is `{route: '/_image', entrypoint: undefined}`. The endpoint is used for image optimization in dev and SSR; set `entrypoint` to `undefined` to restore the default. A custom route is useful when `/_image` conflicts with an existing route.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

To apply the `No-Vary-Search` header to all pages in your Astro site automatically, configure it at the edge or via a reverse proxy, since Astro itself does not directly set this header. You can define the header in your CDN or server configuration (e.g., Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare) to apply it globally.

Alternatively, if using a custom server setup with middleware, you can use `Astro.response.headers.set()` inside a middleware function that runs on all routes:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro';

export default defineMiddleware(({ request, locals }, next) => {
  const response = next();
  response.headers.set('No-Vary-Search', 'params=("utm_source" "utm_medium" "utm_campaign" "utm_term" "utm_content"), key-order');
  return response;
});
```

This ensures consistent application without manual edits per page. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/middleware/

The `Astro.locals.auth()` API requires the Clerk middleware to be configured in your project’s `src/middleware.ts` file. Without this middleware, `Astro.locals.auth()` will not populate authentication state, causing `isAuthenticated` to always return `false`.

You must set `clerkMiddleware()` as the `onRequest` handler in your middleware:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import { clerkMiddleware } from "@clerk/astro/server";
export const onRequest = clerkMiddleware();
```

This middleware is required because it parses the incoming request, extracts authentication tokens (like cookies or headers), and populates `Astro.locals.auth()` with user session data. Without it, Astro has no way to verify a user’s login status.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/#clerk

Import `prefetch()` from the `astro:prefetch` module in a client-side script and call it from the click handler:

```html
<button id="btn">Click me</button>
<script>
  import { prefetch } from 'astro:prefetch';
  const btn = document.getElementById('btn');
  btn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    prefetch('/about');
  });
</script>
```

`prefetch()` includes data saver mode (`saveData`) and slow connection (`effectiveType`) detection, so it only prefetches when needed. To bypass that detection, pass `prefetch('/about', { ignoreSlowConnection: true })`. Import `prefetch()` only in client-side scripts, since it relies on browser APIs.

`eagerness` accepts `'immediate' | 'eager' | 'moderate' | 'conservative'`, defaulting to `'immediate'` (added in `astro@5.6.0`), mirroring the Speculation Rules API from most to least eager. It is available when the experimental `clientPrerender` flag is enabled, which upgrades prefetching to client-side prerendering of link targets in supported browsers:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  prefetch: {
    prefetchAll: true,
    defaultStrategy: 'viewport',
  },
  experimental: {
    clientPrerender: true,
  },
});
```

Use `conservative` for resource-heavy pages and `moderate` for large link sets so the browser schedules them (FIFO); where the Speculation Rules API is unsupported, `prefetch` falls back to the supported strategy.

Use `i18n.domains` to map locales to custom URLs: a `Record<string, string>` that assigns each listed locale its own domain or subdomain, in a `server`-rendered project using the `@astrojs/node` or `@astrojs/vercel` adapter with `site` configured and no prerendered pages.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config"
import node from "@astrojs/node"
export default defineConfig({
  site: "https://example.com",
  output: "server",
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  i18n: {
    locales: ["es", "en", "fr", "ja"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
    routing: { prefixDefaultLocale: false },
    domains: { fr: "https://fr.example.com", es: "https://example.es" }
  }
})
```

Mapped locales get no `/[locale]/` prefix (e.g. `/fr/about.astro` → `https://fr.example.com/about`); unmapped locales follow your `prefixDefaultLocale` strategy. Localized folders in `src/pages/` are still required, including for `defaultLocale`, and `getAbsoluteLocaleUrl()`/`getAbsoluteLocaleUrlList()` return these domain URLs.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Configure `image.domains` for an exact allowlist and/or `image.remotePatterns` for pattern-based allowlists; remote URLs matching neither are not optimized (and raise the "Remote image is not allowed" error when used).

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ['astro.build'],
    remotePatterns: [{ protocol: 'https', hostname: '**.amazonaws.com' }],
  },
});
```

`domains` takes exact domain strings only — no wildcards; use `remotePatterns` (with `protocol`, `hostname`, `port`, `pathname`) when you need them. Hostname wildcards: `**.` allows all subdomains, `*.` one level; pathname `/**` allows all sub-routes, `/*` one level. Redirects are followed, so the final destination must also be allowed. Unauthorized images still render via `<Image />`, avoiding CLS, just without optimization.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Instantiate `Intl.PluralRules` in the component script and index a category-keyed message map in the template; Astro templates evaluate JavaScript expressions, so this works like any other frontmatter value.

```astro
---
const count = 5;
const rules = new Intl.PluralRules("ru");
const messages = {
  one: `${count} файл`,
  few: `${count} файла`,
  many: `${count} файлов`,
  other: `${count} файла`,
};
const label = messages[rules.select(count)];
---
<p>{label}</p>
```

`select()` returns a CLDR category (`zero`, `one`, `two`, `few`, `many`, `other`), so store strings by category rather than hard-coding `count === 1` logic, which breaks for most locales (Japanese and Chinese use only `other`). Pass `{ type: "ordinal" }` for "1st/2nd/3rd", and pair with `Intl.NumberFormat` for locale-appropriate digits.

You can set `Cache-Control` headers in Astro middleware by modifying the response object returned by `next()`. Use `response.headers.set()` to apply directives like `immutable`, `max-age`, or `no-cache`.

Example:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(({ request, next }) => {
  const response = next();
  response.headers.set("Cache-Control", "public, max-age=31536000, immutable");
  return response;
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/middleware/

To generate Cache-Control headers for fingerprinted assets using `buildCacheControlDirectives`, set `immutable` to `true` and `maxAge` to `31536000` (1 year). This tells browsers and CDNs to cache the asset indefinitely, since its URL includes a content hash.

Example:
```ts
import { buildCacheControlDirectives } from 'astro';

const cacheControl = buildCacheControlDirectives({
  immutable: true,
  maxAge: 31536000
});
// Result: "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
```

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/cache-control/

To configure Astro to use Vercel's CDN cache provider with correct headers, you need to set up a custom cache provider that applies `Cache-Control` directives via Vercel-specific internal headers.

Use the `astro/cache/provider-utils` helpers in your adapter or middleware:

```ts
import { buildCacheControlDirectives } from 'astro/cache/provider-utils';

export default {
  cache: {
    provider: {
      setHeaders(headers, options) {
        const cacheControl = buildCacheControlDirectives(options);
        if (cacheControl) {
          headers.set('Vercel-CDN-Cache-Control', cacheControl);
        }
      },
    },
  },
};
```

This configures Astro to use Vercel's CDN provider with appropriate `Cache-Control` directives, such as `public, max-age=31536000, immutable` for fingerprinted assets and `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` for HTML.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

To add the last modified time as a frontmatter property in Markdown documents using Astro, use a markdown processor plugin to read Git history and inject the `lastModified` field into each document’s frontmatter.

For **unified**, configure your `astro.config.mjs` with:

```js
import { unified } from "@astrojs/markdown-remark";
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import { remarkModifiedTime } from "./remark-modified-time.mjs";

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      remarkPlugins: [remarkModifiedTime],
    }),
  },
});
```

Then, in your page or layout template, access `lastModified` via `remarkPluginFrontmatter` (for content collections) or `Astro.props.frontmatter` (for Markdown layouts). Example:

```astro
---
import dayjs from "dayjs";
import utc from "dayjs/plugin/utc";
dayjs.extend(utc);

const lastModified = dayjs(Astro.props.frontmatter.lastModified)
  .utc()
  .format("HH:mm:ss DD MMMM YYYY UTC");
---

<p>Last Modified: {lastModified}</p>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/modified-time/

Use a `root` locale in the `starlight()` integration's `locales` config: add a `root` key with a `label` and a `lang` (`lang` is required for root locales), and optionally set `defaultLocale: 'root'` (or remove `defaultLocale`) when English is your default. English routes then have no prefix (`/about` instead of `/en/about`), and its content files live directly in `src/content/docs/` rather than a language folder.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import starlight from '@astrojs/starlight';
export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    starlight({
      title: 'My Docs',
      defaultLocale: 'root', // optional
      locales: {
        root: { label: 'English', lang: 'en' },
        'zh-cn': { label: '简体中文', lang: 'zh-CN' },
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

Docs: https://starlight.astro.build/guides/i18n/

Apply `writing-mode: vertical-rl` with `text-orientation: mixed` on the paragraph, and set `lang="ja"` on the same element so the browser picks the Japanese line-break dictionary — CSS alone is not enough:

```astro
<p lang="ja" class="vertical">日本の段落</p>

<style>
  .vertical {
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
    text-orientation: mixed;
  }
  :lang(ja) {
    line-break: strict;
    word-break: keep-all;
  }
</style>
```

`line-break: strict` enforces Japanese publisher conventions (JIS X 4051), and `word-break: keep-all` ensures wraps fall between characters rather than inside compounds.

Map each route per language and translate paths through helpers. Add route mappings in `src/i18n/ui.ts`:
```ts
export const routes = {
  de: { services: "leistungen" },
  fr: { services: "prestations-de-service" },
};
```
Then in `src/i18n/utils.ts` create `useTranslatedPath(lang)`, which looks up `routes[l][pathName]` for non-default languages and prefixes `/${l}` (omitting it for the default language when `showDefaultLang` is false). Use it in links: `const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang); <a href={translatePath("/services/")}>`, and pair it with `getRouteFromUrl(Astro.url)` in a `LanguagePicker` so switching languages keeps users on the equivalent translated route.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/i18n/

Target the language with CSS's `:lang()` pseudo-class, which matches elements whose `lang` attribute (or an ancestor's) equals the given language—useful for typography and quotation marks. Declare `lang` on the elements (e.g. `<span lang="fr">` or `<html lang={lang}>` from `getLangFromUrl(Astro.url)`), then style them in a component `<style>` block:
```css
:lang(fr) { font-style: italic; }
:lang(de) { hyphens: auto; }
```
Scoped styles work normally here; use `is:global` or a global stylesheet if the language styling must span components.

Set `attrs` in the `autogenerate` configuration to apply attributes to all autogenerated links, then override per page with the `sidebar.attrs` frontmatter field, which is merged with `autogenerate.attrs`:
```js
starlight({
  sidebar: [{
    autogenerate: {
      directory: 'constellations',
      attrs: { style: 'font-style: italic' },
    },
  }],
});
```
Individual pages add `sidebar: { attrs: { target: '_blank' } }` in frontmatter, so only that page's link gets the extra attribute. Note `sidebar` frontmatter only applies to autogenerated links and `slug`-defined links, not `link`-defined ones; manually defined links use `attrs` on the item itself.
Docs: https://starlight.astro.build/guides/sidebar/

Set both attributes on each switcher link to the locale that link points to: `lang` declares the language of the link's own text, so a screen reader switches voice and announces "Deutsch" correctly even on a page in another language, while `hreflang` tells search engines and browsers which language the target URL serves. Combine this with a native-language label and a translated path:

```astro
---
import { languages } from "../i18n/ui";
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from "../i18n/utils";
const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);
---
<ul>
  {Object.entries(languages).map(([lang, label]) => (
    <li>
      <a href={translatePath("/", lang)} lang={lang} hreflang={lang}>{label}</a>
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>
```

Add a region to the label only where it disambiguates, e.g. "English (UK)" vs "English (US)".

Follow the i18n recipe's "Hide default language in the URL" steps:

1. Keep default-language pages directly in `src/pages/` and put each other language in its own directory (e.g. `src/pages/fr/about.astro`).
2. In `src/i18n/ui.ts`, add `export const showDefaultLang = false;`
3. In `src/i18n/utils.ts`, add a path-translating helper:

```ts
import { ui, defaultLang, showDefaultLang } from "./ui";
export function useTranslatedPath(lang: keyof typeof ui) {
  return function translatePath(path: string, l: string = lang) {
    return !showDefaultLang && l === defaultLang ? path : `/${l}${path}`;
  };
}
```

4. Import `useTranslatedPath` in components and link with `translatePath("/home/")`.
5. Use it in `LanguagePicker.astro` with `translatePath("/", lang)` so the default language links to an unprefixed path.

Astro v4.0+ also ships built-in i18n routing as an alternative to this hand-rolled recipe.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/internationalization/

Use `Intl.PluralRules` with `{ type: "ordinal" }` — ordinals follow a different CLDR rule set from cardinals — and store strings per plural category (`one`, `two`, `few`, `other`, …) rather than per number, letting `select()` pick the form. Pair it with `Intl.NumberFormat` so digits render for the locale:

```js
const ord = new Intl.PluralRules("en", { type: "ordinal" });
const forms = { one: "{n}st", two: "{n}nd", few: "{n}rd", other: "{n}th" };
const text = forms[ord.select(n)].replace("{n}", new Intl.NumberFormat("en").format(n));
```

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/i18n/plural-rules/

Call `auth.api.getSession({ headers: Astro.request.headers })` from the page frontmatter, importing your Better Auth instance, and mark the page dynamic with `export const prerender = false` (not needed in `'server'` output mode) since the session is read per request:

```astro
---
import { auth } from "../lib/auth"; // import your Better Auth instance
export const prerender = false;
const session = await auth.api.getSession({
  headers: Astro.request.headers,
});
---
<p>{session.user?.name}</p>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

Export a Hono app from `src/fetch.ts` that registers `requireAuth` on `/dashboard` and `/dashboard/*` before Astro's handlers, so only those paths are guarded while every other request falls through to Astro's pipeline:

```ts
// src/fetch.ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';
import { isLoggedIn } from './lib/auth';

const app = new Hono();

// Guard every route under /dashboard.
app.use('/dashboard', requireAuth);
app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth);

app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());

export default app;

async function requireAuth(c, next) {
  if (!(await isLoggedIn(c.req.raw))) {
    return c.redirect('/login');
  }
  return next();
}
```

`requireAuth` checks `c.req.raw`, redirects unauthenticated visitors, and otherwise calls `next()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

Call `Astro.redirect()` from the page's frontmatter and `return` it so the redirect fires before any HTML streams:

```astro
---
import { isLoggedIn } from "../utils";
const cookie = Astro.request.headers.get("cookie");
if (!isLoggedIn(cookie)) {
  return Astro.redirect("/login");
}
---
```

Because Astro streams HTML in on-demand rendering, the redirect must happen at the page level, not inside child components — a component-level redirect arrives too late. `Astro.redirect` is for request-dependent runtime decisions (auth, sessions) and requires on-demand rendering; in static mode routes are fixed at build time via `getStaticPaths()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

Pass an `i18n` option to the `@astrojs/sitemap` integration (with `site` set) and it emits `<xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="…">` entries per URL. `i18n` takes a required `defaultLocale` (which must be a key of `locales`) and `locales` mapping path segments to language attributes (English alphabet and hyphen only):

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';
export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  integrations: [
    sitemap({
      i18n: {
        defaultLocale: 'en', // All urls that don't contain `es` or `fr` after `https://example.com/` will be treated as default locale, i.e. `en`
        locales: {
          en: 'en-US', // The `defaultLocale` value must present in `locales` keys
          es: 'es-ES',
          fr: 'fr-CA',
        },
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

URLs without a locale segment are treated as the default locale. The integration cannot generate entries for dynamic routes in SSR mode.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

Pass an `i18n` object with the two required properties — `defaultLocale` and `locales` — to the `sitemap()` integration. Keys of `locales` are matched against the locale part of a page path, values are the language attribute (English alphabet and hyphen only), and `defaultLocale` must also be a key of `locales`.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';
export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  integrations: [
    sitemap({
      i18n: {
        defaultLocale: 'en',
        locales: { en: 'en-US', es: 'es-ES', fr: 'fr-CA' },
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

The generated sitemap emits `<xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="...">` entries for every locale on each URL; paths without `es` or `fr` are treated as the default locale.

Use an explicit language picker instead of header-based negotiation: render a link to each supported locale and let the visitor choose, deriving the current language from the URL rather than `Accept-Language`:

```astro
---
import { languages } from "../i18n/ui";
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from "../i18n/utils";
const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);
---
<ul>
  {Object.entries(languages).map(([lang, label]) => (
    <li><a href={translatePath("/", lang)}>{label}</a></li>
  ))}
</ul>
```

Show it on every page (e.g. in the base layout footer). `Astro.currentLocale`, computed from the URL, is available on all pages including prerendered ones — unlike `Astro.preferredLocale`, which depends on `Accept-Language` and only exists on routes rendered on demand.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/i18n/

Create `src/pages/dashboard.astro`, query Firestore in the frontmatter with the Admin SDK, then map the docs to render each record with a details link and an edit button:

```astro
---
import { app } from "../firebase/server";
import { getFirestore } from "firebase-admin/firestore";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

interface Friend {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  age: number;
  isBestFriend: boolean;
}

const db = getFirestore(app);
const friendsRef = db.collection("friends");
const friendsSnapshot = await friendsRef.get();
const friends = friendsSnapshot.docs.map((doc) => ({
  id: doc.id,
  ...doc.data(),
})) as Friend[];
---

<Layout title="My friends">
  <h1>Friends</h1>
  <ul>
    {
      friends.map((friend) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/friend/${friend.id}`}>{friend.name}</a>
          <span>({friend.age})</span>
          <strong>{friend.isBestFriend ? "Bestie" : "Friend"}</strong>
          <a href={`/edit/${friend.id}`}>Edit</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</Layout>
```

The edit button is a plain link to `/edit/${friend.id}`, which resolves to `src/pages/edit/[id].astro`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/firebase/

Add a dynamic route at `src/pages/friend/[id].astro` that reads the `id` param, fetches that one Firestore document, and redirects to `/404` when the id is missing or the document doesn't exist:

```astro
---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { app } from "../../firebase/server";
import { getFirestore } from "firebase-admin/firestore";

interface Friend {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  isBestFriend: boolean;
}
const { id } = Astro.params;
if (!id) {
  return Astro.redirect("/404");
}
const db = getFirestore(app);
const friendsRef = db.collection("friends");
const friendSnapshot = await friendsRef.doc(id).get();
if (!friendSnapshot.exists) {
  return Astro.redirect("/404");
}
const friend = friendSnapshot.data() as Friend;
---

<Layout title={friend.name}>
  <h1>{friend.name}</h1>
  <p>Age: {friend.age}</p>
  <p>Is best friend: {friend.isBestFriend ? "Yes" : "No"}</p>
</Layout>
```

This requires the Firebase server setup (`src/firebase/server.ts`) used by the other pages.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/firebase/

Mark the parent wrapper as a query container, then write `@container` rules that style its descendants — set `container-type` on the wrapper, not on the element you're styling, since an element cannot query itself:

```css
.card-list {
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: cards;
}

@container cards (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card {
    grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr;
  }
}
```

`inline-size` is the common choice; name containers so nested queries target the intended ancestor. Style queries test custom properties instead: `@container style(--density: compact) { ... }`. Treat it as progressive enhancement — unsupported browsers ignore the `@container` block, so provide a sensible single-column default first.

Pass values from your component frontmatter to the `<style>` block with the `define:vars` directive; they become CSS custom properties named `--<variableName>` you can reference with `var()`.

```astro
---
const foregroundColor = "rgb(221 243 228)";
const backgroundColor = "rgb(24 121 78)";
---
<style define:vars={{ foregroundColor, backgroundColor }}>
  h1 {
    background-color: var(--backgroundColor);
    color: var(--foregroundColor);
  }
</style>
<h1>Hello</h1>
```

The `<style>` block can also reference any CSS variable already available on the page.

With an SSR adapter configured, an RSS endpoint route renders on demand: create `src/pages/rss.xml.js` that exports an async `GET(context)` function returning the `rss()` result, instead of a prerendered build-time file.

```js
import rss from '@astrojs/rss';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
export async function GET(context) {
  const blog = await getCollection('blog');
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz’s Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut’s guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    items: blog.map((post) => ({
      title: post.data.title,
      pubDate: post.data.pubDate,
      description: post.data.description,
      link: `/blog/${post.id}/`,
    })),
  });
}
```

Optionally apply `rssSchema` to your collection schema to enforce valid item properties.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

In Starlight, keep the `favicon` option pointing at your default SVG and add the Safari ICO fallback as an extra tag through the `head` option:

```js
starlight({
  favicon: '/images/favicon.svg',
  head: [
    // Add ICO favicon fallback for Safari.
    {
      tag: 'link',
      attrs: {
        rel: 'icon',
        href: '/images/favicon.ico',
        sizes: '32x32',
      },
    },
  ],
});
```

The `favicon` option (default `'/favicon.svg'`) sets the primary icon from your `public/` directory, while `head` appends additional `<link>` tags without replacing it.

Docs: https://starlight.astro.build/reference/configuration/

Install `@astrojs/rss`, configure `site` in `astro.config` (used to generate links to your articles), and add a file in `src/pages/` with the `.xml.js` extension — `src/pages/rss.xml.js` serves at `site/rss.xml`:

```js
import rss from '@astrojs/rss';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

export async function GET(context) {
  const blog = await getCollection('blog');
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz’s Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut’s guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    items: blog.map((post) => ({
      title: post.data.title,
      pubDate: post.data.pubDate,
      description: post.data.description,
      link: `/blog/${post.id}/`,
    })),
  });
}
```

It works for static builds and on-demand generation with an SSR adapter. Optionally use `rssSchema` as your collection schema, or `pagesGlobToRssItems(import.meta.glob('./blog/*.{md,mdx}'))` for page files. For auto-discovery, add to `<head>`: `<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="Your Site's Title" href={new URL("rss.xml", Astro.site)} />`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

Install `rollup-plugin-visualizer` as a dev dependency, then add it under `vite.plugins` in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { visualizer } from "rollup-plugin-visualizer";
export default defineConfig({vite: {    plugins: [visualizer({        emitFile: true,        filename: "stats.html",    })]}});
```

Run `npm run build` (or `pnpm build`/`yarn build`) and open `stats.html` at the root of `dist/`. With on-demand rendering there are two files: one in `dist/client/` and one in `dist/server/`. Caveats: the visualizer shows all dependencies used across the site but not per-page sizes, and due to Astro's hydration model the build isn't necessarily what the client receives.

After retrieving a `cacheHint` from `getLiveCollection()`, pass it to `Astro.cache.set()` to apply the caching strategy defined by the live loader.

```astro
---import { getLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const { entries, cacheHint } = await getLiveCollection('products');
if (cacheHint) {
  Astro.cache.set(cacheHint);
}
---
<ul>
  {entries.map((p) => <li>{p.data.name}</li>)}
</ul>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/#getlivecollection

`import.meta.glob()` only supports static string literals; dynamic variables and string interpolation are not allowed in the pattern. The common workaround is to glob a larger static set containing all the files you need, then filter the results using the dynamic value:

```astro
---
const { postSlug } = Astro.props;
const pathToMyFeaturedPost = `src/pages/blog/${postSlug}.md`;
const posts = Object.values(
  import.meta.glob("../pages/blog/*.md", { eager: true }),
);
const myFeaturedPost: any = posts.find((post: any) =>
  post.file.includes(pathToMyFeaturedPost),
);
---
```

Pass `{ eager: true }` as the second argument to load all matched modules up front, and use `Object.values()` on the result.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/imports/

Content collections provide performant, content-focused APIs for loading multiple files instead of `import.meta.glob()`, so prefer `getCollection()` for structured content like blog posts, and reserve `import.meta.glob()` for importing arbitrary sets of local files (e.g. components) that aren't managed as a collection. Query with `const allPosts = await getCollection("blog");` (imported from `astro:content`). The returned shape differs: frontmatter values live on `data` instead of `frontmatter`, and each entry has an `id`/slug rather than a full `url`, so you construct paths yourself:

```astro
---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const allPosts = await getCollection("blog");
---
{allPosts.map((post) => <a href={`/posts/${post.id}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>)}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/imports/

You cannot: `import.meta.glob()` only accepts static string literals — dynamic variables and string interpolation are unsupported. The workaround is to glob a broader static pattern with `{ eager: true }`, then filter the matched modules by comparing each one's `file` property to a path built from the variable:

```astro
---
const { postSlug } = Astro.props;
const pathToMyFeaturedPost = `src/pages/blog/${postSlug}.md`;
const posts = Object.values(
  import.meta.glob("../pages/blog/*.md", { eager: true }),
);
const myFeaturedPost: any = posts.find((post: any) =>
  post.file.includes(pathToMyFeaturedPost),
);
---
```

For content files, prefer `getCollection()` instead.

Set `preserveBuildClientDir: true` (added in astro@6.0.0) and/or `preserveBuildServerDir: true` (added in astro@6.4.0) in `adapterFeatures` when calling `setAdapter()`; both default to `false`. A use case is a static site containing server islands or image endpoints that still need a server entry, requiring a consistent `dist/client/` and `dist/server/` layout:

```js
setAdapter({
  name: '@example/my-adapter',
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
  adapterFeatures: {
    preserveBuildServerDir: true,
  },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Use `passthroughImageService()` when your adapter does not support Astro's built-in Sharp optimization (e.g. Cloudflare), so you can still use `<Image />` and `<Picture />`. It is a no-op: Astro performs no image transformation, but you keep `astro:assets` benefits—no Cumulative Layout Shift, enforced `alt`, and a consistent authoring experience.

```js
import { defineConfig, passthroughImageService } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    service: passthroughImageService()
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/images/

Install the official RSS package (`npm install @astrojs/rss`) and create an endpoint at `src/pages/rss.xml.js` exporting a `GET` function:

```js
import rss, { pagesGlobToRssItems } from '@astrojs/rss';
export async function GET(context) {
  return rss({
    title: 'Astro Learner | Blog',
    description: 'My journey learning Astro',
    site: context.site,
    items: await pagesGlobToRssItems(import.meta.glob('./**/*.md')),
    customData: `<language>en-us</language>`,
  });
}
```

Set `site` in `astro.config.mjs` so `context.site` resolves, then enable auto-discovery in your `<head>`:

```html
<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="Your Site's Title" href={new URL("rss.xml", Astro.site)}/>
```

The feed regenerates on every build and is served at `/rss.xml`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

Declare configured redirects in `astro.config.mjs` with the `redirects` option, pointing each old route directly at its final destination (A → C in one hop, never A → B → C), and keep them indefinitely:

```js
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  redirects: {
    "/old-page": "/new-page",
    "/blog/[...slug]": "/articles/[...slug]"
  }
});
```

The default status is `301`; pass `{ status: 302, destination: "/new-page" }` only for temporary moves. Static builds emit HTML meta refreshes; supported adapters write the host's redirect config instead. Dynamic destinations must match an existing route with the same parameters or `InvalidRedirectDestination` is thrown. For runtime checks, use `Astro.redirect()` at the page level, not in child components.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

Set Clerk's `clerkMiddleware()` as the `onRequest` handler in `src/middleware.ts`, then gate each route via `Astro.locals.auth()`:

```ts
import { clerkMiddleware } from "@clerk/astro/server";
export const onRequest = clerkMiddleware({
  /* options */
});
```

```astro
---
const { isAuthenticated, redirectToSignIn } = Astro.locals.auth();
if (!isAuthenticated) return redirectToSignIn();
---
<h1>Dashboard</h1>
```

API routes read the same state and can return `new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 })`. Requests must render on demand (`export const prerender = false` when not in `server` mode). Astro has no official auth solution; Better Auth is the other common choice.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

Create a catch-all API route at `src/pages/api/auth/[...all].ts` that exports an `ALL` handler delegating to your auth library's request handler:

```ts
import { auth } from "../../../lib/auth"; // import your Better Auth instance
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const prerender = false; // Not needed in 'server' mode
export const ALL: APIRoute = async (ctx) => {
  return auth.handler(ctx.request);
};
```

This mounts Better Auth at `/api/auth/`, handling sign-in and OAuth flows. To protect your own endpoints instead, check `locals.auth()` in the handler and return a 401 response when unauthenticated.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

Install `better-auth` via npm/pnpm/yarn. Configure your database tables and authentication methods per the Better Auth Installation Guide. Mount the Better Auth handler in an Astro API route at `src/pages/api/auth/[...all].ts`:

```ts
import { auth } from "../../../lib/auth";
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const prerender = false;
export const ALL: APIRoute = async (ctx) => {
  return auth.handler(ctx.request);
};
