You must define build-time content collections (like Markdown blogs) in `src/content.config.ts` and live collections (like real-time products) in `src/live.config.ts`. Each config file exports its own `collections` object. Do not mix them in the same file or export.

Example structure:

**src/content.config.ts**
```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
const blog = defineCollection({ /* ... */ });
export const collections = { blog };
```

**src/live.config.ts**
```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const products = defineLiveCollection({ /* ... */ });
export const collections = { products };
```

Use `getCollection()`/`getEntry()` for content and `getLiveCollection()`/`getLiveEntry()` for live data.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#live-content-collections

Export a `GET` function from a file in `src/pages` that returns a `Response` (or a `Promise` resolving to one). Returning anything else throws **EndpointDidNotReturnAResponse**.

```ts
import type { APIContext } from 'astro';
export async function GET({ request, url, cookies }: APIContext): Promise<Response> {
  return Response.json({
    success: true,
    result: 'Data from Astro Endpoint!'
  })
}
```

The `APIContext` also exposes `params`, `request`, and `cookies` for building the payload.

Endpoints support the same dynamic routing as pages: name the file with a bracketed parameter and export `getStaticPaths()`, then read the value from `params` inside `GET`.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const GET = (({ params, request }) => {
  const id = Number(params.id);
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ name: usernames[id] }));
}) satisfies APIRoute;
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [{ params: { id: "0" } }, { params: { id: "1" } }];
}
```

In static mode each entry is generated at build time (`/api/0.json`, `/api/1.json`); with on-demand rendering, passing `props` is not supported since an endpoint is a function, not a component. Trailing slashes: endpoints whose URLs include a file extension (e.g. `/sitemap.xml`) can only be accessed without a trailing slash, regardless of your `build.trailingSlash` configuration.

Add `src/pages/llms-full.txt.ts` exporting an `GET` that returns the concatenated markdown as a `Response`; in static mode Astro calls it at build time and writes `/llms-full.txt`, in SSR it runs on request.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const GET = (async () => {
  const body = "# Example Corp — full content\n\n> All public documentation, concatenated.\n\n---\n\n# Getting started\n...";
  return new Response(body, {
    headers: { "Content-Type": "text/markdown; charset=utf-8" },
  });
}) satisfies APIRoute;
```

Curate the same pages as `llms.txt`, concatenate them in the same order, separate sections with `---` and `#` headings (include each page URL near its heading), strip front-matter, and set cache headers since the file changes on every doc update.

Install `rollup-plugin-visualizer` as a dev dependency, add it to the `vite.plugins` array in `astro.config.mjs`, then run `npm run build` and open the generated `stats.html`.

```js
// @ts-check
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { visualizer } from "rollup-plugin-visualizer";
export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    plugins: [visualizer({ emitFile: true, filename: "stats.html" })]
  }
});
```

For fully static sites, `stats.html` sits at the root of `dist/`. With on-demand rendering you get two files: one for the client and one for the server, at the roots of `dist/client/` and `dist/server/`. When interpreting it, remember two caveats: the visualizer shows all dependencies used across the site but does not break down size per page, and because of Astro's hydration approach the build isn't necessarily representative of the bundle the client actually receives. Use it to spot heavy dependencies worth trimming or code-splitting.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/analyze-bundle-size/

Use `getLiveEntry()` from `astro:content` in a page rendered on demand, which refetches data from your source at each request instead of once at build time. This requires an adapter configured for on-demand rendering, `export const prerender = false` (unneeded in `'server'` mode), and the collection defined in `src/live.config.ts` with `defineLiveCollection()` and a custom loader implementing `loadCollection`/`loadEntry`:

```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { storeLoader from '@mystore/astro-loader';
const products = defineLiveCollection({ loader: storeLoader({ apiKey: process.env.STORE_API_KEY, endpoint: 'https://api.mystore.com/v1' }) });
export const collections = { products };
```

Inspect `Astro.params` for the slug, call `getLiveEntry()`, redirect if missing, then render. This differs from `getEntry()`, which fetches build-time collection data only once, on first request.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

When querying a live collection, the Zod schema takes precedence over the loader’s loose typing. The schema defines the final TypeScript type and validation rules for the entry data.

Example:
```ts
const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: apiLoader({ endpoint: process.env.API_URL }),
  schema: z.object({
    price: z.number(),
  }),
});
```

If `price` is a string from the API, Zod will coerce it to a number during validation. If coercion fails, an error is thrown.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#using-zod-schemas-with-live-collections

To handle both OAuth and email/password login in a single `/api/auth/signin` endpoint, check the `provider` field from `formData`. If a valid provider (e.g., `github`, `google`, `discord`) is present, call `supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth()` with the provider and a `redirectTo` pointing to your callback endpoint (`/api/auth/callback`), then return `redirect(data.url)`. Otherwise, fall back to `supabase.auth.signInWithPassword()` with email/password, set the returned `access_token` and `refresh_token` as cookies (`sb-access-token`, `sb-refresh-token`), and redirect to `/dashboard`.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { supabase } from "../../../lib/supabase";
import type { Provider } from "@supabase/supabase-js";

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request, cookies, redirect }) => {
  const formData = await request.formData();
  const email = formData.get("email")?.toString();
  const password = formData.get("password")?.toString();
  const provider = formData.get("provider")?.toString();

  const validProviders = ["google", "github", "discord"];
  if (provider && validProviders.includes(provider)) {
    const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth({
      provider: provider as Provider,
      options: { redirectTo: "http://localhost:4321/api/auth/callback" },
    });
    if (error) return new Response(error.message, { status: 500 });
    return redirect(data.url);
  }

  if (!email || !password) {
    return new Response("Email and password are required", { status: 400 });
  }
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithPassword({ email, password });
  if (error) return new Response(error.message, { status: 500 });

  const { access_token, refresh_token } = data.session;
  cookies.set("sb-access-token", access_token, { path: "/" });
  cookies.set("sb-refresh-token", refresh_token, { path: "/" });
  return redirect("/dashboard");
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/supabase/

Use `redirect()` for navigation responses (3xx) — it sets the `Location` header and appropriate status automatically. Return `new Response()` when you need full control over status, headers, or body (e.g., 400/500 errors, JSON payloads, binary data, custom headers). In API routes, `redirect()` is a helper on the context object; `new Response()` is the standard Web API. Both satisfy the `APIRoute` return type.

```ts
// Redirect after successful login
return redirect("/dashboard");

// Error response with custom status/message
return new Response("Email and password are required", { status: 400 });

// JSON response with headers
return new Response(JSON.stringify(product), {
  status: 200,
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
});
```

Astro v3+ requires returning a `Response` (or `redirect()` which returns one); plain objects are deprecated.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/endpoints/

Enabling `nodejs_compat` in Wrangler lets you use `node:*` imports (e.g., `node:crypto`, `node:path`) in Cloudflare Workers, but pitfalls include: (1) incomplete Node API coverage — some modules are polyfilled, others unavailable; (2) increased Worker size and cold-start latency from polyfills; (3) packages that use non-`node:*` imports (e.g., `require('crypto')`) will fail unless they support the `node:*` syntax. Mitigate by: using `node:*` imports exclusively, checking Cloudflare's supported API list, avoiding dependencies that don't use `node:*`, and testing locally with `wrangler dev`. If a package fails, find an alternative or vendor the dependency.

```jsonc
// wrangler.jsonc
{
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"]
}
```

```js
// Server-side code
import { Buffer } from 'node:buffer';
import { randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/cloudflare/

Configure i18n routing in `astro.config.mjs`, create localized folders under `src/pages/`, and build links with `getRelativeLocaleUrl()` from `astro:i18n`:

```
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config"
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    locales: ["es", "en", "pt-br"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
  }
})
```

Touch target size is not handled by routing — apply CSS to your language switcher and other controls. WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 requires at least 24×24 CSS px (44×44 for the enhanced criterion):

```css
button,
a.button,
[role="button"] {
  min-height: 44px;
  min-width: 44px;
}
```

