Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/internationalization/

Define each collection with `defineCollection()` in `src/content.config.ts`, passing a `schema` built from Zod types imported from `astro/zod` (a re-export supporting all of Zod 4):

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { z } from "astro/zod";
import { glob } from "astro/loaders";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
    updatedDate: z.coerce.date().optional(),
  }),
});

export const collections = { blog };
```

Every frontmatter property must then exist in the declared form; violations produce a helpful error, and the schema powers generated TypeScript types and autocompletion when querying. Zod methods such as `.parse()` and `.transform()` work, except `image().refine()`, which is unsupported. Restart the dev server or sync the content layer (`s + enter`) so Astro picks up schema changes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

Because any environment variable with the `PUBLIC_` prefix is intentionally exposed to the browser bundle — Astro inlines it for client-side code, so your database URL and auth token shipped to every visitor. Remove the prefix so they stay server-only: name them `TURSO_DATABASE_URL` and `TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN` in `.env`, and read them only from server-side code, e.g. in `src/turso.ts`:

```ts
import { createClient } from "@libsql/client/web";
export const turso = createClient({
  url: import.meta.env.TURSO_DATABASE_URL,
  authToken: import.meta.env.TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN,
});
```

Then rotate the leaked token, since it has already been public.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/turso/

The file `src/turso.ts` must import and instantiate the Turso client using:
```ts
import { createClient } from "@libsql/client/web";
export const turso = createClient({
  url: import.meta.env.TURSO_DATABASE_URL,
  authToken: import.meta.env.TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN,
});
```

The `loader` property in `defineCollection()` is for build-time collections, where data is fetched and processed during the build phase. The `loader` property in `defineLiveCollection()` is for live collections, where data is fetched at runtime.

For example, a build-time collection might use the `file()` loader to read local JSON files:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { file } from 'astro/loaders';

const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file('src/data/dogs.json'),
});

export const collections = { dogs };
```

A live collection uses a custom `loader` function that fetches data at runtime:

```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { storeLoader } from '@example/astro-loader';

const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: storeLoader({ apiKey: process.env.STORE_API_KEY }),
});

export const collections = { products };
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/

Install the SDK with `npx astro add @sentry/astro` (or the `pnpm`/`yarn` equivalents) — the CLI installs the package and adds the Sentry integration to `astro.config.mjs`. Then configure it there with your DSN, project name, and auth token:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sentry from '@sentry/astro';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    sentry({
      dsn: 'https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0',
      sourceMapsUploadOptions: {
        project: 'example-project',
        authToken: process.env.SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN,
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

Once `dsn` and `sourceMapsUploadOptions` are set, the SDK automatically captures and sends errors and performance events, and uploads source maps. Get the DSN from *Client keys (DSN)*, the project name from *General settings*, and create the auth token under your organization's *Auth tokens*. Verify with `<button onclick="throw new Error('This is a test error')">Throw test error</button>` on a page, then open the error at sentry.io.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/sentry/

Astro has built-in TypeScript support: you can import `.ts`/`.tsx` files, write TypeScript directly in component frontmatter, and use `astro.config.ts`. Essentials:

- Keep `tsconfig.json` and extend a template — `"extends": "astro/tsconfigs/base"` (use `strict` or `strictest` if you write TS), plus `"include": [".astro/types.d.ts", "**/*"]` and `"exclude": ["dist"]`.
- Type component props with a `Props` interface in frontmatter; the Astro VS Code extension picks it up automatically:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  name: string;
  greeting?: string;
}
const { greeting = "Hello", name } = Astro.props;
---
<h2>{greeting}, {name}!</h2>
```

- No props: `type Props = Record<string, never>`; required children: `type Props = { children: any; }`.
- The dev server does no type checking — run `astro check` to check `.astro` and `.ts` files (`tsc` ignores `.astro` files entirely).

Add top-level declarations with the `declare` keyword in `src/env.d.ts`:

```ts
declare var myString: string;
declare function myFunction(): boolean;
```

This types both `globalThis.myString`/`globalThis.myFunction` and `window.myString`/`window.myFunction`, so the properties are typed in server and client code — prefer `declare var` over `interface Window`, which only types the `window` object. Environment caveats: `window` exists only in client-side code, while `globalThis` is available both server-side and client-side, but its server-side value is not shared with the client. You can also include Astro's own types with `/// <reference path="../.astro/types.d.ts" />`.

A `.d.ts` file is an ambient module declaration and does not allow top-level `import`/`export` statements: if TypeScript encounters one, the file is treated as a module augmentation instead, which breaks your global types. Avoid it by reusing types with dynamic `import()` type syntax inside the declaration rather than top-level imports:

```ts
type Product = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
};
declare namespace App {
  interface Locals {
    orders: Map<string, Product[]>
    session: import("./lib/server/session").Session | null;
    user: import("my-external-library").User;
  }
}
```

Local `type` aliases in the same file are fine — only top-level import/export statements turn the file into a module.

In Astro v3.0, automatic flattening of `getStaticPaths()` return values was removed. If you return an array of arrays, you must now use `.flatMap()` or `.flat()` to ensure a flat array of objects is returned.

Example fix:
```js
// Before (v2.x) - auto-flattened
return posts.map(post => [{ params: { slug: post.slug } }]);

// After (v3.0+) - must flatten manually
return posts.flatMap(post => [{ params: { slug: post.slug } }]);
// or
return posts.map(post => [{ params: { slug: post.slug } }]).flat();
```

Create `src/pages/api/auth/register.ts` with a POST handler that accepts form data (email/password), validates input, calls `supabase.auth.signUp()`, and redirects on success. For static output, add `export const prerender = false`.

```ts
// With `output: 'static'` configured:
// export const prerender = false;
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { supabase } from "../../../lib/supabase";

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request, redirect }) => {
  const formData = await request.formData();
  const email = formData.get("email")?.toString();
  const password = formData.get("password")?.toString();

  if (!email || !password) {
    return new Response("Email and password are required", { status: 400 });
  }

  const { error } = await supabase.auth.signUp({
    email,
    password,
  });

  if (error) {
    return new Response(error.message, { status: 500 });
  }

  return redirect("/signin");
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/supabase/

Edit or create `src/env.d.ts` and declare `ImportMetaEnv` with `XATA_API_KEY` as a required string and `XATA_BRANCH` as optional, then extend `ImportMeta` so `import.meta.env` is typed:

