To drain a buffer at the end of each request, implement the optional `flush()` method in your custom logger's `AstroLoggerDestination`. This is called by Astro after each request completes. For cleanup before server shutdown, implement the optional `close()` method.

These are called by Astro’s logging infrastructure:
- `flush()` is invoked per-request (on-demand)
- `close()` is invoked when the server shuts down (Node adapter)

Example:

```js
export default function customLogger({ buffer = [] }) {
  return {
    write(message) {
      buffer.push(message);
    },
    flush() {
      // Send buffered messages to log aggregation service
      console.log('Flushing:', buffer);
      buffer.length = 0;
    },
    close() {
      // Final flush before shutdown
      console.log('Closing:', buffer);
    }
  };
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/logger-reference/

Import `cacheVercel()` from `@astrojs/vercel/cache` and set it as the provider in `astro.config.mjs` alongside the Vercel adapter. The provider sets `Vercel-CDN-Cache-Control` and `Vercel-Cache-Tag` headers, pushing directives to Vercel's edge CDN. Tag invalidation is soft (stale-while-revalidate background revalidation). Both tag- and path-based invalidation work.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vercel from '@astrojs/vercel';
import { cacheVercel } from '@astrojs/vercel/cache';

export default defineConfig({
  adapter: vercel(),
  cache: { provider: cacheVercel() },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

`cache.invalidate()` throws an error when no cache provider is configured (or in dev mode where `cache.enabled` is `false`). Before calling any cache method, guard with `if (context.cache.enabled)` (or `Astro.cache.enabled` in pages). This check returns `false` when no provider exists or in development, preventing the error.

```ts
// In middleware or API route
if (context.cache.enabled) {
  await context.cache.invalidate({ tags: ['products'] });
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

Pass any JSON-serializable frontmatter variable (including `Astro.props`) to the style with the `define:vars` directive, then read it as a CSS variable via `var()`:

```
---
const foregroundColor = "rgb(221 243 228)";
const backgroundColor = "rgb(24 121 78)";
---
<style define:vars={{ foregroundColor, backgroundColor }}>
  h1 {
    background-color: var(--backgroundColor);
    color: var(--foregroundColor);
  }
</style>
```

Values are serialized with `JSON.stringify()`. Note that `define:vars` on a `<script>` tag implies `is:inline` (unbundled script); for styles it simply exposes the variables on the page.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/directives-reference/

Set a cache provider in your Astro config: import `memoryCache` from `astro/config` and pass it to `cache.provider`.

```
---
```
```
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
import node from '@astrojs/node';
export default defineConfig({
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
});
```

`memoryCache()` is a built-in in-memory LRU provider for single-instance deployments (options: `max`, default 1000, plus `query.sort/include/exclude` for cache-key handling). Route caching was added in `astro@7.0.0` and applies to on-demand rendered pages/endpoints; you then use `Astro.cache` (or `context.cache`) and `routeRules`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Use the `routeRules` option in `astro.config.mjs` alongside a cache provider:

```
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
import node from '@astrojs/node';
export default defineConfig({
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
  routeRules: {
    '/api/[...path]': { swr: 600 },
    '/products/[...slug]': { maxAge: 3600, tags: ['products'] },
    '/blog/[...slug]': { maxAge: 300, swr: 60 },
  },
});
```

Patterns support static paths, dynamic parameters (`/products/[id]`), and rest parameters (`/api/[...path]`); glob wildcards like `*` are not supported. Matching follows file-based routing priority (more specific wins), and any per-route `cache.set()` calls merge with or override these config defaults.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

No, writing a custom logger implementation is not the right approach for hiding `info` messages in Astro's output during CI.

Astro does not expose a Cache Provider API or routing mechanism that would allow filtering log levels via a custom provider. The provided documentation focuses on cache providers and request pipeline customization, which are unrelated to logging behavior.

To suppress `info` messages in CI environments, use Astro’s built-in configuration options like `logLevel: 'warn'` in your `astro.config.mjs`, or control logging through environment variables (`CI=true`) that Astro respects. Custom loggers would require modifying the underlying Node.js process or using a third-party logging library — neither of which is supported by Astro's current architecture.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';

export default defineConfig({
  logLevel: 'warn',
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Use a rest parameter under the `/api/` prefix: `'/api/[...path]'` mapped to `{ swr: 600 }` in `routeRules`. Glob wildcards such as `*` are not supported; a `[...rest]` parameter is what matches a whole group of routes. Patterns use the same syntax, matching, and priority rules as Astro's file-based routing, so more specific patterns take precedence.

```js
routeRules: {
  '/api/[...path]': { swr: 600 },
}
```

Use JavaScript-style template expressions in the component body, driven by variables defined in the frontmatter script. Astro's templating syntax is similar to JSX, so standard JS conditions work directly in the template. Logical operators render an element only when the condition is truthy; the ternary operator chooses between two alternatives. When a condition is false, nothing is displayed.

```
---
const happy = true;
const finished = false;
const goal = 3;
---
{happy && <p>I am happy to be learning Astro!</p>}
{finished && <p>I finished this tutorial!</p>}
{goal === 3 ? <p>My goal is to finish in 3 days.</p> : <p>My goal is not 3 days.</p>}
```

In a `.astro` page component, set cache options using `Astro.cache.set()` with the options object:
```astro
---
export const prerender = false;
Astro.cache.set({
  maxAge: 120,
  swr: 60,
  tags: ['home'],
});
---
<html><body>Cached page</body></html>
```
This caches for 120 seconds, serves stale content for 60 seconds while revalidating, and tags with 'home' for later invalidation.

Call `cache.set(false)` in the page to explicitly opt that request out of caching, overriding a matched route rule:

```
---
if (isPersonalized) {
  Astro.cache.set(false);
}
---
```

Per-route `cache.set()` calls merge with config-level route rules, so route code can override or disable the defaults set in config.

Add a `<script>` tag directly inside the Astro component; in the tutorial it is placed in `src/components/ThemeIcon.astro` after the `<style>` tag:

```
<script is:inline>
  const handleToggleClick = () => {
    const element = document.documentElement;
    element.classList.toggle("dark");
    const isDark = element.classList.contains("dark");
    localStorage.setItem("theme", isDark ? "dark" : "light");
  }
  document.getElementById("themeToggle")?.addEventListener("click", handleToggleClick);
</script>
```

Alternatively, drop in a UI framework component — it stays static unless you add a `client:` directive to send its JavaScript to the browser.

Import `sessionDrivers` from `astro/config` and set `session.driver` to `sessionDrivers.redis()`:

```
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({
      url: process.env.REDIS_URL
    }),
  }
})
```

This built-in driver (added in `astro@5.7.0`) wraps the Unstorage Redis driver, so it may require extra packages and environment variables or credentials. For runtime-overridable configuration, define the driver in a separate file and use it as the `entrypoint` instead.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

Create two files: a config file exporting a function returning `SessionDriverConfig` with `entrypoint` pointing to the runtime file, and a runtime file exporting a default function that returns a `SessionDriver` object with `setItem`, `getItem`, `removeItem` methods using an LRU cache.

driver/config.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriverConfig } from 'astro'
export interface Config { max?: number }
export function memoryDriver(config: Config = {}): SessionDriverConfig {
  return { entrypoint: new URL('./runtime.js', import.meta.url), config }
}
```

driver/runtime.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriver } from 'astro'
import type { Config } from './config'
import { LRUCache } from 'lru-cache'
export default function(config: Config): SessionDriver {
  const cache = new LRUCache({ max: config.max })
  return {
    setItem: async (key, value) => { cache.set(key, value) },
    getItem: async (key) => { return cache.get(key) },
    removeItem: async (key) => { cache.delete(key) },
  }
}
```

Register in `astro.config.ts`:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config'
import { memoryDriver } from './driver/config'
export default defineConfig({ session: { driver: memoryDriver({ max: 500 }) } })
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

If `REDIS_URL` is not set, the Redis driver will fail to connect at runtime, likely throwing a connection error (e.g., `ECONNREFUSED` or authentication failure). Resolve by ensuring the environment variable is defined in your deployment environment (`.env` file locally, platform secrets in production). Use Astro's `env.schema` with `envField.string({ context: "server", access: "secret" })` to validate it exists at build/dev time.

```ts
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, envField } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      REDIS_URL: envField.string({ context: "server", access: "secret" })
    }
  }
})
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

Configure the Redis driver by importing `sessionDrivers` from `astro/config` and passing `process.env.REDIS_URL` to `sessionDrivers.redis()` in your Astro config. The environment variable must be set in your deployment environment (`.env` locally, platform secrets in production).

```ts
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL })
  }
})
```

Note: `.env` files are not loaded inside configuration files by default; ensure `REDIS_URL` is available in the process environment when Astro config runs.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

Implement a custom session driver with two files: a config file returning `SessionDriverConfig` with an `entrypoint` to your runtime file, and a runtime file exporting a default function returning a `SessionDriver` object that uses a simple key-value store (e.g., `Map`, file system, or database) instead of Redis. Follow the same pattern as the memory driver example but replace `LRUCache` with your store.

driver/config.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriverConfig } from 'astro'
export interface Config { /* your options */ }
export function kvDriver(config: Config = {}): SessionDriverConfig {
  return { entrypoint: new URL('./runtime.js', import.meta.url), config }
}
```

driver/runtime.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriver } from 'astro'
import type { Config } from './config'
const store = new Map<string, any>()
export default function(config: Config): SessionDriver {
  return {
    setItem: async (key, value) => { store.set(key, value) },
    getItem: async (key) => { return store.get(key) },
    removeItem: async (key) => { store.delete(key) },
  }
}
```

Register in `astro.config.ts`:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config'
import { kvDriver } from './driver/config'
export default defineConfig({ session: { driver: kvDriver({ /* options */ }) } })
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

Avoid `Astro.cache.set()` with tags—and instead call `Astro.cache.set(false)`—whenever the response varies per user, e.g. your personalized dashboard. Tag-based invalidation purges entries globally; it does not partition the cache per user. With the experimental adapter cache providers (Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare), cached responses live in the host's shared CDN and cache hits are served straight from the edge without invoking your server function. The risk is that one user's personalized page or API response is served to a different user—a data leak. Cache only data identical for every visitor, gate calls with `if (Astro.cache.enabled)`, and opt personalized requests out explicitly:

```astro
---
if (isPersonalized) {
  Astro.cache.set(false);
}
---
```

Use the built-in Redis driver, or build a custom one on unstorage.

Simplest path (from `astro/config`); install any extra package the driver needs and provide credentials (e.g. `REDIS_URL`):

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({
      url: process.env.REDIS_URL
    }),
  }
})
```

For customization: install the `unstorage` package, then either export a function returning a `SessionDriverConfig` with `entrypoint: "unstorage/drivers/redis"` and a serializable `config`, register it under `session.driver`, or import the unstorage driver and wrap it in your own `SessionDriver` implementing `setItem`, `getItem`, and `removeItem`—useful for defaults like a 7-day `ttl` or runtime config overrides. Some drivers also require environment variables or credentials.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

Store the prop in a `data-*` attribute on an HTML element, then read it from `dataset` in the script. Frontmatter runs on the server, and a processed `<script>` is a separate browser module with no access to server variables—the data attribute bridges the gap:

```astro
---
const { message = 'Welcome, world!' } = Astro.props;
---
<astro-greet data-message={message}>
  <button>Say hi!</button>
</astro-greet>

<script>
  class AstroGreet extends HTMLElement {
    connectedCallback() {
      const message = this.dataset.message;
      const button = this.querySelector('button');
      button?.addEventListener('click', () => alert(message));
    }
  }
  customElements.define('astro-greet', AstroGreet);
</script>
```

Each component instance gets its own value, so the same component can render with different props on one page.

Pass `format` in the `paginate()` options (added in Astro 7.1.0) as a function that receives the computed URL and returns a transformed one, letting you reshape URLs before rendering:

```js
return paginate(allPokemon, {
  pageSize: 10,
  format: (url) => `${url}.html`,
});
```

Other options are `pageSize` (default 10), `params`, and `props`.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

The `logHandlers.compose()` function broadcasts the same message to all configured loggers, but each logger applies its own filtering based on its configured log level. In your configuration, the console logger is set to `warn` level, so it only outputs `warn` and `error` messages. The JSON logger has no explicit level set, so it defaults to `info`, which means it should receive all `info` messages.

However, if you're seeing only `warn` or higher-level logs in the console but not in the JSON output, this likely indicates that your application is using a different logger instance for each handler. The issue may stem from how the `logHandlers.compose()` function handles message propagation across loggers and whether they are correctly configured to receive all messages.

To ensure both loggers process all messages, explicitly set matching levels on both handlers:

```js
import { defineConfig, logHandlers } from 'astro/config';

export default defineConfig({
  logger: logHandlers.compose(
    logHandlers.console({ level: 'info' }),
    logHandlers.json({ level: 'info' })
  )
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/logger-reference/

To pass additional data to each page in `getStaticPaths()`, include a `props` object alongside `params` in each returned route object. Unlike `params`, `props` are not URL-encoded and can contain any serializable data (objects, arrays, etc.). The page component accesses this data via `Astro.props`.

```astro
---export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts");
  const posts = await response.json();
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
    props: { post }
  }));
}
const { post } = Astro.props;---
<h1>{post.title}</h1>
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

Use the `GetStaticPaths` type utility (added in Astro 2.1.0) with `satisfies` to infer `Astro.params` and `Astro.props` types. Import `GetStaticPaths`, `InferGetStaticParamsType`, and `InferGetStaticPropsType` from `"astro"`.

```astro
---import type { GetStaticPaths, InferGetStaticParamsType, InferGetStaticPropsType } from "astro";
export const getStaticPaths = (async () => {
  const posts = await getCollection("blog");
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
    props: { draft: post.data.draft, title: post.data.title }
  }));
}) satisfies GetStaticPaths;
type Params = InferGetStaticParamsType<typeof getStaticPaths>;
type Props = InferGetStaticPropsType<typeof getStaticPaths>;
const { id } = Astro.params; // { id: string }
const { title } = Astro.props; // { draft: boolean; title: string }---
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/typescript/

The `getStaticPaths()` function runs in an isolated scope at build time. It cannot reference variables or functions from its parent component scope—only imported modules are allowed. The compiler emits a warning if you violate this rule.

```astro
---// ❌ This breaks: 'data' is from parent scope
const data = await fetchData();
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return data.map(...); // Compiler warning
}
// ✅ This works: import at top level
import { fetchData } from "../utils/data";
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const data = await fetchData();
  return data.map(...);
}---
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

The `page` prop from `paginate()` includes `page.lastPage` (total number of pages) and `page.total` (total items across all pages). Access them via `Astro.props.page`.

```astro
---export const getStaticPaths = ({ paginate }) => {
  const items = [...Array(100)].map((_, i) => ({ id: i + 1 }));
  return paginate(items, { pageSize: 10 });
};
const { page } = Astro.props;---
<p>Page {page.currentPage} of {page.lastPage}</p>
<p>Showing {page.start + 1}–{page.end} of {page.total} items</p>
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

`routePattern` (added in `astro@5.14.0`) is a `string` property in the `getStaticPaths()` options object that exposes the current route's original dynamic segment definition (e.g. `/[...locale]/[files]/[slug]`), data from the Astro render context otherwise unavailable inside `getStaticPaths()`. Because it always reflects the file path pattern rather than concrete values (unlike `params`), you can pass it to a helper that expands each data record into localized param sets:

```astro
---
import type { GetStaticPathsOptions } from "astro";
import { getLocalizedData } from "../../../utils/i18n";
export async function getStaticPaths({ routePattern }: GetStaticPathsOptions) {
  const response = await fetch("...");
  const data: any[] = await response.json();
  return data.flatMap((file) => getLocalizedData(file, routePattern));
}
const { locale, files, slug } = Astro.params;
---
```

The returned `params` (`locale`, `files`, `slug`) then generate the routes and are readable via `Astro.params`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

Import `memoryCache()` from `astro/config` and set it as `cache.provider`; no adapter is required for the built-in in-memory LRU runtime provider (suitable for single-instance deployments):

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({ max: 500 }),
  },
});
```

`max` (default `1000`) caps cached entries with LRU eviction. Optional `query` options tune cache keys: `sort` (default `true`), `exclude` (defaults to common tracking params like `utm_*`) or `include` (mutually exclusive with `exclude`). The provider adds an `X-Astro-Cache: HIT|MISS|STALE` header, sorts query params, excludes tracking params, and honors `Vary` (ignoring `Cookie`). Cache Provider API added in `astro@7.0.0`; remember `routeRules`/`cache.set()` only take effect when a provider is configured.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

No — glob wildcards like `/docs/*` are not supported in `routeRules`. Use a rest parameter instead: `/docs/[...path]`. Route rules use the same `[param]` and `[...rest]` syntax, matching, and priority rules as Astro's file-based routing, so more specific patterns take precedence.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
  routeRules: {
    '/docs/[...path]': { maxAge: 3600, swr: 60 },
  },
});
```

Supported pattern forms: static paths (`/about`), dynamic parameters (`/products/[id]`), and rest parameters (`/docs/[...path]`). Note that path-based `cache.invalidate({ path })` is exact-match only — no glob or wildcard patterns either. `routeRules` was added in `astro@7.0.0`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

With `cacheCloudflare()` configured, the Cloudflare adapter automatically enables the Cloudflare [Workers Cache](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/cache/) with default settings, while the provider sets `Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control` and `Cache-Tag` headers (both tag- and path-based invalidation supported; responses are tagged with the request path). You'd modify its configuration if you want to **preserve the cache when you deploy a new version of the site**, or otherwise tune Workers Cache behavior to your needs.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import cloudflare from '@astrojs/cloudflare';
import { cacheCloudflare } from '@astrojs/cloudflare/cache';
export default defineConfig({
  adapter: cloudflare(),
  cache: { provider: cacheCloudflare() },
});
```

`@astrojs/cloudflare@14.0.0` added this provider; during the experimental phase it must be enabled manually.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

`context.cache.invalidate()` is unavailable when the cache feature is not enabled—no `cache.provider` is configured in `astro.config.mjs`, so `context.cache.enabled` is `false` and cache methods are not functional ("Cache is not enabled"). Guard the call:

```js
if (context.cache.enabled) {
  await context.cache.invalidate({ tags: ['data'] });
}
```

In dev mode `enabled` is always `false` and `invalidate()` is a no-op.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/cache-not-enabled/

`routePattern` (added in `astro@5.14.0`) is a `string` property destructured from the `getStaticPaths()` options object, exposing the current route's original dynamic segment definition from the render context. Unlike `params`, which holds explicit values for a page (e.g. `/fr/fichiers/article-1/`), `routePattern` always reflects the file path pattern (e.g. `[...locale]/[files]/[slug]`), making it useful for calculating `params` and `props` for each route:

```astro
---
import type { GetStaticPathsOptions } from "astro";
import { getLocalizedData } from "../../../utils/i18n";

export async function getStaticPaths({ routePattern }: GetStaticPathsOptions) {
  const data: any[] = await (await fetch("...")).json();
  return data.flatMap((file) => getLocalizedData(file, routePattern));
}
const { locale, files, slug } = Astro.params;
---
```

Use the type utilities exported from `"astro"`: `InferGetStaticParamsType` for the type of `Astro.params`, `InferGetStaticPropsType` for `Astro.props`, or `GetStaticPaths` to infer both at once. Attach them to your `getStaticPaths` function with `satisfies GetStaticPaths` (added in `astro@2.1.0`):

```astro
---
import type {
  InferGetStaticParamsType,
  InferGetStaticPropsType,
  GetStaticPaths,
} from "astro";

export const getStaticPaths = (async () => {
  return [{ params: { id: "1" }, props: { draft: false, title: "Post" } }];
}) satisfies GetStaticPaths;

type Params = InferGetStaticParamsType<typeof getStaticPaths>;
type Props = InferGetStaticPropsType<typeof getStaticPaths>;

const { id } = Astro.params;    // { id: string }
const { title } = Astro.props;  // { draft: boolean; title: string }
---
```

The `params` object in each array entry returned by `getStaticPaths()` tells Astro which routes to prerender. Its keys must match the dynamic route parameters in the file name (e.g., `[id].astro` requires `params: { id: 'value' }`). Values must be strings (or `undefined` for rest parameters) because they are encoded into the URL.

```astro
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } },  // generates /posts/1
    { params: { id: '2' } },  // generates /posts/2
  ];
}
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/#params

Register a renderer by creating an integration that calls `addRenderer()` in the `astro:config:setup` hook. Provide a name, `serverEntrypoint` (required), and optional `clientEntrypoint` for hydration. The hook receives `addRenderer` as a callback parameter.

```js
export default function createIntegration() {
  return {
    name: "@example/my-renderer",
    hooks: {
      "astro:config:setup": ({ addRenderer }) => {
        addRenderer({
          name: "@example/my-renderer",
          clientEntrypoint: "@example/my-renderer/client.js",
          serverEntrypoint: "@example/my-renderer/server.js",
        });
      },
    },
  };
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/renderer-reference/

`AstroComponentMetadata` describes the component being rendered, including its hydration directive: `displayName`, `hydrate`, `hydrateArgs`, `componentUrl`, `componentExport`, and `astroStaticSlot`. In your renderer's `renderToStaticMarkup()`, read `metadata?.hydrate` to skip hydration-only work (e.g. serializing transfer state) when the component won't be hydrated:

```ts
async function renderToStaticMarkup(
  Component: any,
  props: Record<string, unknown>,
  slots: Record<string, string>,
  metadata?: AstroComponentMetadata,
) {
  const willHydrate = !!metadata?.hydrate;
  return render(Component, props, { includeTransferState: willHydrate });
}
```

Create a server entrypoint file that default-exports an object implementing `SSRLoadedRendererValue`, then register it with `addRenderer()` in your integration's `astro:config:setup` hook. `check()` decides whether the renderer handles a component, and `renderToStaticMarkup()` renders it:

```ts
import type { AstroComponentMetadata, NamedSSRLoadedRendererValue } from 'astro';
async function check(Component: unknown) {
  return typeof Component === 'function' && Component.name === 'MyCustomComponent';
}
async function renderToStaticMarkup(
  Component: any,
  props: Record<string, unknown>,
  slots: Record<string, string>,
  metadata?: AstroComponentMetadata,
) {
  const rendered = Component(props);
  return {
    attrs: metadata?.hydrate ? { 'data-my-renderer': 'true' } : undefined,
    html: `<${rendered.tag}>${rendered.text}${slots.default ?? ''}</${rendered.tag}>`,
  };
}
const renderer: NamedSSRLoadedRendererValue = {
  name: 'my-renderer',
  check,
  renderToStaticMarkup,
};
export default renderer;
```

Register the renderer from an integration's `astro:config:setup` hook via `addRenderer({ name, clientEntrypoint, serverEntrypoint })`: Astro imports the server module during development and builds, while the client module is imported only in the browser to hydrate components carrying a `client:*` directive, so no client JavaScript loads unless you explicitly mark a component interactive. The client entrypoint default-exports a function receiving the island element and returning an async hydrator:

```ts
export default (element: HTMLElement) =>
  async (
    Component: any,
    props: Record<string, unknown>,
    slots: Record<string, string>,
    { client }: { client: string },
  ) => {
    const rendered = Component({ ...props, slots });
    if (client === 'only') {
      element.innerHTML = '';
    }
    const node = document.createElement(rendered.tag);
    node.textContent = rendered.text;
    element.appendChild(node);
    element.addEventListener('astro:unmount', () => node.remove(), { once: true });
  };
```

Missing `clientEntrypoint` with a `client:*` directive raises `NoClientEntrypoint`, and `client:only` requires a renderer hint (e.g. `client:only="react"`) or `NoClientOnlyHint` is thrown.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/renderer-reference/

