```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/experimental-flags/

In `src/middleware.js|ts`, guard on `context.request.method` and return a `Response` directly instead of calling `next()`. Since `onRequest()` must return a `Response` — either directly or by calling `next()` — never invoking `next()` skips the rest of the rendering pipeline:

```js
export function onRequest (context, next) {
  if (context.request.method !== "GET") {
    return new Response(null, { status: 405 });
  }
  return next();
}
```

Wrap your handler in `defineMiddleware()` from `astro:middleware`: `context` and `next()` are automatically typed, and TypeScript errors if you return an unsupported value.

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
});
```

In plain JS, use the JSDoc form `/** @type {import("astro").MiddlewareHandler} */`. To type `Astro.locals` (autocompletion in `.astro` files and middleware), declare a global `App.Locals` interface in `src/env.d.ts` — e.g. extend `EdgeLocals` from `@astrojs/vercel` when using edge middleware.

The CMS handles authoring; Astro handles presentation. A headless CMS lets you write content and manage assets outside your Astro project — providing a visual/WYSIWYG editor, defined content types, and collaboration — but it does not generate the site that displays the content. Astro fetches that content data (via the CMS's REST API, a JavaScript SDK, or an Astro integration) and renders it wherever and however you want. For example, with WordPress you fetch your site's REST API and render fields with `set:html`:

```
const res = await fetch("https://[YOUR-SITE]/wp-json/wp/v2/posts");
const posts = await res.json();
```

`notFound()` returns a 404 `Response` for use in your i18n routing middleware when `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"` (added in `astro@4.6.0`, type `(context: APIContext, response?: Response) => Promise<Response> | undefined`). It triggers when the current path isn't a root (`/` or `/<base>`) and the URL doesn't contain a locale; if you pass a `Response`, the emitted response keeps the original's headers. Return it instead of calling `next()`:

```
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) {
    return pathNotFound;
  }
  return next();
})
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-i18n/

Set `i18n.routing: "manual"` in `astro.config.mjs` (added in `astro@4.6.0`), which disables Astro's i18n middleware so you write your own routing logic; no other `routing` options (e.g. `prefixDefaultLocale`) may be combined with it. In your middleware, import helpers from `astro:i18n` — `notFound()` returns a 404 response when the path isn't a root or the URL has no locale:

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) return pathNotFound;
  return next();
});
```

Other available helpers: `redirectToDefaultLocale()`, `redirectToFallback()`, and `middleware()` (to run Astro's i18n middleware alongside yours via `sequence()`).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/internationalization/

In your middleware file — `src/middleware.js` (or `src/middleware.ts`) — inside an `onRequest` handler created with `defineMiddleware` from `astro:middleware`. `notFound` is imported from the `astro:i18n` virtual module and is only available when `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"` (added in `astro@4.6.0`):

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) return pathNotFound;
  return next();
});
