Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-i18n/

Add an `i18n` object to `defineConfig()` in `astro.config.mjs` (added in `astro@3.5.0`), with required `locales` and `defaultLocale` fields:

```js
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    locales: ["es", "en", "fr"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
    routing: { prefixDefaultLocale: true },
  },
});
```

`locales` accepts plain codes or objects mapping `codes` to a custom URL `path` (e.g. `{ path: "french", codes: ["fr", "fr-BR", "fr-CA"] }`); folder names must match the `locales` items (or the `path`). Optional `fallback` maps missing translations to another locale. `astro.config.js`, `.mjs`, and `.ts` are all supported; `.mjs` is recommended.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Return the 404 `Response` emitted by `notFound()`. When `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"` (added in `astro@4.6.0`), call `notFound(context)` in your routing middleware; if it returns a `Response`, return it immediately to respond with a 404, otherwise continue with `next()`.

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) {
    return pathNotFound;
  }
  return next();
});
```

Use `notFound()` from `astro:i18n` (added in Astro 4.6.0) when you have set `i18n.routing: "manual"`: it is the built-in way to return a 404 from your routing middleware when the current path isn't a root (`/` or `/<base>`) or the URL doesn't contain a locale, saving you from hand-coding those checks. It exists only under manual routing (which itself requires you to provide an i18n middleware file), and it optionally accepts a `Response`, emitting a new 404 response that keeps the original's headers.

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) {
    return pathNotFound;
  }
  return next();
})
```

In manual routing mode (`i18n.routing` set to `"manual"`), use `notFound(context, response?)` (added in `astro@4.6.0`; returns `Promise<Response> | undefined`) in your routing middleware to return a 404 when the current path isn't a root (e.g. `/` or `/<base>`) or the URL doesn't contain a locale. If `notFound()` returns a `Response`, return it immediately; otherwise continue with `next()`. If a `Response` is passed, the emitted response retains the same headers as the original.

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) {
    return pathNotFound;
  }
  return next();
});
```

In manual routing mode (`i18n.routing` set to `"manual"`), call `notFound(context)` (returns `Promise<Response> | undefined`, added in `astro@4.6.0`) from middleware to enforce that the path is a root or contains a locale and return a 404 otherwise. You can also use `pathHasLocale(path: string)` (added in `astro@4.6.0`) earlier to validate paths before calling other i18n utilities that depend on a locale from the URL; `notFound()` performs the necessary checks when handling requests in manual routing.

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound, pathHasLocale } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const urlPath = context.url.pathname;
  // Optional guard before other i18n utilities
  if (!pathHasLocale(urlPath) && urlPath !== '/' && !urlPath.startsWith('/<base>')) {
    // notFound will handle the response
  }
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) return pathNotFound;
  return next();
});
```

`notFound()` returns `Promise<Response> | undefined` (added in `astro@4.6.0`, available only when `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"`). In middleware, if it returns a `Response`, return it immediately to stop the request; if it returns `undefined`, call `next()`. When you pass a second `response` argument, the emitted response will contain the same headers as the original response.

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) {
    return pathNotFound;
  }
  return next();
});
```

Use `astro/hono` (added in `astro@7.0.0`): it exports Astro's handlers as Hono middleware functions, so you can mix them with any Hono middleware from the ecosystem by exporting a Hono app from `src/fetch.ts`:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { logger } from 'hono/logger';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';

const app = new Hono();
app.use(logger());
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

Register Astro handlers with `app.use()` in your desired order, and add your own Hono middleware (e.g. `logger()`) or path-scoped guards such as `app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth)` alongside them.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

Export a Hono app from `src/fetch.ts` and register Astro's Hono handlers on it; page routes themselves still come from your `src/pages/` files, served through `pages()`:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';

const app = new Hono();
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

You can then layer Hono routing on top, e.g. `app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth)` to guard a path or `app.use(logger())` for Hono middleware. The `astro/hono` module was added in `astro@7.0.0`.

`notFound()` (from `astro:i18n`, added in `astro@4.6.0`, available only when `i18n.routing` is `"manual"`) returns a 404 `Response` for paths your manual routing should reject: when the current path is not a root (`/` or `/<base>`) and the URL contains no locale. For such locale-less routes it produces the 404 response (carrying over headers if you pass a `Response`); for valid paths it returns `undefined`, so you proceed with `next()`:

```
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);  if (pathNotFound) {    return pathNotFound;  }  return next();})
