The `astro/hono` module (added in Astro 7.0.0) provides Hono-compatible wrappers for Astro's advanced routing handlers so you can use Hono as your routing framework. Each export returns a Hono middleware function, letting you mix Astro handlers with any Hono middleware from the ecosystem. Export a Hono app from `src/fetch.ts`:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { logger } from 'hono/logger';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';
const app = new Hono();
app.use(logger());
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

You can also register route guards (e.g. `app.use('/dashboard', requireAuth)`) before the Astro handlers to protect specific paths.

The `pages` handler (added in `astro@7.0.0`) wraps Astro's page rendering as a Hono middleware function, so a Hono app exported from `src/fetch.ts` can serve your Astro pages. Register it with `app.use()`, typically together with the other Astro handlers:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { logger } from 'hono/logger';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';
const app = new Hono();
app.use(logger());
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

Because it is ordinary Hono middleware, use it effectively by ordering it after other middleware — a logger, or an auth guard such as `app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth)` — so protected routes are checked before Astro renders them.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

`astro/hono` (added in `astro@7.0.0`) provides Hono-compatible wrappers of Astro's advanced routing handlers, letting you use Hono as your routing framework. It exports the same handler names as `astro/fetch` — `FetchState`, `astro`, `actions`, `cache`, `i18n`, `middleware`, `pages`, `redirects`, `sessions`, `trailingSlash` — but each returns a Hono middleware function instead, so Astro handlers can be mixed with any middleware from the Hono ecosystem (e.g. `hono/logger`) or with route guards.

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';
const app = new Hono();
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

Export the configured app from `src/fetch.ts`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

Use `astro/hono` instead of `astro/fetch` when you prefer Hono as your routing framework: both modules export the same handler names, but `astro/hono` returns each handler as a Hono middleware function, so you can register Astro's handlers with `app.use()` and mix them freely with Hono ecosystem middleware (e.g. `hono/logger`) and with your own route guards. With `astro/fetch` you get the plain handlers instead, without that Hono integration.

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
