Call `i18n(state, response)` after rendering: it post-processes the returned `Response` against your i18n configuration, handling locale redirects, 404s for invalid locales, and fallback routing. Its type is `(state: FetchState, response: Response) => Promise<Response>`, so it must receive the rendered response and return the final one.

```ts
import { FetchState, i18n, middleware, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const response = await middleware(state, (s) => pages(s));
    return i18n(state, response);
  },
};
```

The `astro/fetch` module (including `i18n()`) was added in `astro@7.0.0`.

Call `sessions(state)` early in your fetch handler, before middleware runs; it registers the session provider, after which sessions are created lazily when your code accesses `ctx.session` and persisted automatically at the end of the request.

```ts
import { FetchState, sessions } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    await sessions(state);
    // ...render pipeline...
  },
};
```

If no session driver is configured or sessions are disabled, `ctx.session` will be `undefined`. Sessions are not supported in edge middleware.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/sessions/

`trailingSlash()` from `astro/fetch` applies your project's trailing-slash setting inside a custom fetch pipeline: it checks whether the request pathname needs trailing-slash normalization and returns a redirect `Response` if so, or `undefined` when no redirect is needed.

The setting itself is configured as `trailingSlash` in `astro.config.mjs` (e.g. `"always"`, `"never"`, `"ignore"`); `trailingSlash(state)` only enforces it per request.

```
import { FetchState, trailingSlash } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const redirect = trailingSlash(state);
    if (redirect) return redirect;
    // continue pipeline...
  },
};
```

Call `await sessions(state)` early in your custom `fetch` pipeline, before middleware runs — it registers the session provider for the request.

```
import { FetchState, sessions } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    await sessions(state);
    // ...render pipeline...
  },
};
```

Sessions are created lazily when your code accesses `ctx.session` and are persisted automatically at the end of the request, so ordinary `session.get()` / `session.set()` calls (plus `regenerate()` and `destroy()`) are all that's needed for persistence. When no session driver is configured or sessions are disabled, `sessions()` registers no provider and `ctx.session` is `undefined`. Sessions are not supported in edge middleware.

Use `trailingSlash()` from `astro/fetch` in your custom fetch pipeline to automatically normalize URLs. It checks if the request pathname needs trailing-slash normalization per your config and returns a redirect `Response` when needed, otherwise `undefined`. Call it early in the pipeline before rendering.

```ts
import { FetchState, trailingSlash, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const redirect = trailingSlash(state);
    if (redirect) return redirect;
    return pages(state);
  },
};
```
