To generate static paths for individual blog posts in Astro 7 using a dynamic route at `src/pages/blog/[slug].astro`, you must export a `getStaticPaths()` function that returns an array of objects with `params` and optional `props`. This function fetches blog post data, maps each item to a path, and passes relevant props to the page component.

Example implementation:

```astro
---
import { cosmicClient } from "../../lib/cosmic";
import type { BlogPost } from "../../lib/cosmic";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const entries = await cosmicClient.getEntries<BlogPost>({
    bucket: "blog-posts",
    type: "posts"
  });

  return entries.map((item) => ({
    params: { slug: item.slug },
    props: {
      title: item.title,
      content: item.content,
      date: new Date(item.date).toLocaleDateString(),
    }
  }));
}

const { content, title, date } = Astro.props;
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title>{title}</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <time>{date}</time>
    <article set:html={content} />
  </body>
</html>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/#getstaticpaths

The `envField.string()` helper is used to define a typed environment variable with a string data type in Astro's type-safe environment variables schema. It allows you to specify the variable’s context (client or server), access level (public or secret), and additional properties like `optional` or `default`.

To validate a URL-type variable, use `envField.string()` with a custom validation function via the `validate` option in your schema.

Example:

```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      API_URL: envField.string({
        context: "client",
        access: "public",
        validate(value) {
          try {
            new URL(value);
            return true;
          } catch {
            return false;
          }
        },
      }),
    },
  },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

Export a function that returns the `SvgOptimizer` object and takes your configuration as a parameter (custom optimizers supported since `astro@6.2.0`), then assign it to the experimental `svgOptimizer` flag (added in `astro@5.16.0`):

```ts
import type { SvgOptimizer } from "astro";
import { optimize, type Options } from "./optimizer";

export function mySvgOptimizer(options?: Options): SvgOptimizer {
  return {
    name: "my-optimizer",
    optimize: (contents) => optimize(contents, options),
  };
}
```

The `optimize()` signature is `(contents: string, path: string) => string | Promise<string>`.

Omit `'removeXMLNS'` from the `plugins` array passed to `svgoOptimizer()` — the default is `[]`, and only plugins listed there are applied, so leaving it out keeps the `xmlns` attribute intact:

```js
svgoOptimizer({
  plugins: ['preset-default'],
});
```

For a plugin that ships inside `preset-default`, instead disable it via `overrides`:

```js
svgoOptimizer({
  plugins: [{
    name: 'preset-default',
    params: { overrides: { removeDesc: false } },
  }],
});
```

Pass them in the `plugins` array of the options object given to `svgoOptimizer()`. The array (type `Array<string | PluginConfig>`, default `[]`) accepts plain names for `preset-default` or built-in plugins, plus objects with `name` and `params` for custom or reconfigured plugins:

```js
svgoOptimizer({
  plugins: [
    { name: 'preset-default', params: { overrides: { convertPathData: false } } },
    'removeXMLNS',
    { name: "removeXlink", params: { includeLegacy: true } }
  ]
});
```

With `fontProviders.local()`, `options.variants` is required: each variant represents a `@font-face` declaration and must include a `src` (paths relative to the project root, a package import, a URL, or objects with a `tech` value). `weight` and `style` are optional and are inferred from the first source when omitted; `weight` may be a range string for variable fonts (`"100 900"`). Each variant may also set `display`, `unicodeRange`, `stretch`, `featureSettings`, and `variationSettings`.

```astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: fontProviders.local(),
    name: "Custom",
    cssVariable: "--font-custom",
    options: {
      variants: [
        { weight: 400, style: "normal", src: ["./src/assets/fonts/custom-400.woff2"] },
        { weight: 700, style: "normal", src: ["./src/assets/fonts/custom-700.woff2"] }
      ]
    }
  }]
});
```

Store font files outside `public/` (e.g. in `src/`) to avoid duplicated build output.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

Enable the `experimental.incrementalBuild` flag (added in Astro 7.2.0, type `boolean`, default `false`) in your Astro config:

```astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  experimental: {
    incrementalBuild: true,
  },
});
```

Only pages returned from `getStaticPaths()` that include a `cacheKey` string can be skipped; Astro also hashes each page's module dependency graph, so changed code, config, or dependencies invalidate the cache. The cache lives in `cacheDir` (`node_modules/.astro/` by default), which must persist between builds (e.g. in CI) or every page re-renders. Use `astro build --force` to ignore the cache. Note the cache is disabled when `build.concurrency > 1`.

Create `src/env.d.ts` and extend the global `ImportMetaEnv` interface (Astro already provides a base type for `import.meta.env` in `astro/client.d.ts`), then declare `HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT` on it:

```ts
// src/env.d.ts
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT: string;
  // more env variables...
}
interface ImportMeta {
  readonly env: ImportMetaEnv;
}
```

The file must be located in `src/` (i.e. `src/env.d.ts`) and extends the global types, giving you TypeScript IntelliSense when reading `import.meta.env.HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT`. Without `PUBLIC_`-prefixed names, the variable remains server-side only. Alternatively, register it in the `env.schema` with `envField` for generated type-safe imports from `astro:env/server`.

Set it under `options.experimental.variableAxis` for the Google provider. The type is `Partial<Record<VariableAxis, ([string, string] | string)[]>>`, where each entry is either a `[min, max]` pair or a single value:

```js
{
  provider: fontProviders.google(),
  options: {
    experimental: {
      variableAxis: {
        slnt: [["-15", "0"]],
        CASL: [["0", "1"]],
        CRSV: ["1"],
        MONO: [["0", "1"]],
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

Export a function that returns a `FontProvider` object with a `name`, an `init()` hook that fetches your registry's font data, a `listFonts()` that returns the available names, and a `resolveFont()` that looks up `familyName` in that data and returns `{ fonts }` (or `undefined`):

```ts
import type { FontProvider } from "astro";
export function registryFontProvider(): FontProvider {
  let data: Fonts = {}
  return {
    name: "registry",
    init: async () => { data = await retrieveFonts(token); },
    listFonts: () => Object.keys(data),
    resolveFont: ({ familyName, ...rest }) => {
      const fonts = data[familyName];
      if (fonts) return { fonts };
      return undefined;
    },
  };
}
```

Then register it in `astro.config.ts` under the `fonts` array with `provider: registryFontProvider()`, a `name`, and a `cssVariable`. Optional `config`, family-level generics, and `context.storage` (caching) / `context.root` (path resolution) in `init()` are also available.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

Add an optional `listFonts()` property to the `FontProvider` object returned by your provider factory. Its type is `() => Awaitable<string[] | undefined>` (default `undefined`), so it returns an array of font name strings — commonly by delegating to the underlying initialized provider:

```ts
async listFonts() {
  return await initializedProvider?.listFonts?.();
}
```

Because it may be async and may return `undefined`, resolve it with `await` and handle the no-data case; in the private-registry example it simply returns `Object.keys(data)` populated during `init()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

