Return a devalue-serializable value from `transform()` — the error occurs because the result cannot be serialized. Valid returns are plain JSON or devalue-compatible types such as `Date`, `Map`, and `Set`. To format dates, keep them as `Date` objects rather than returning something devalue can't handle (e.g. a locale string built from an unsupported type), or format them later in your component:

```ts
transform(data) {
  return { ...data, pubDate: new Date(data.pubDate) };
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/unsupported-config-transform-error/

`ResponseSentError` means the response has already been sent to the browser, so it can no longer be altered. Resolve it by making all changes to headers, cookies, and the status code in the page component itself, before the response is sent:

```
---
if (!post) {
  Astro.response.status = 404;
  Astro.response.statusText = "Not found";
}
---
```

Setting these after streaming/response completion (outside page components) triggers the error. Note you must mutate `Astro.response.headers` individually rather than reassigning the whole `headers` object.

Set the status in the page component's frontmatter via `Astro.response`, before the response is sent — changes to status, headers, and cookies are only allowed in page components:

```
---
import { getPost } from "../api";
const post = await getPost(Astro.params.id);
if (!post) {
  Astro.response.status = 404;
  Astro.response.statusText = "Not found";
}
---
```

Trying to alter the response after it has been sent throws `ResponseSentError`, so run this conditional logic during rendering rather than afterward (e.g. in streaming callbacks or components once output has begun).

You should use an **external image service** rather than a local one, since your CDN handles optimization via RIAPI-style URLs.

An external service is ideal because it simply maps input options to a remote URL without needing to transform images locally. This avoids unnecessary build-time processing and leverages the CDN's existing capabilities.

Example:
```ts
import type { ExternalImageService, ImageTransform } from "astro";

const service: ExternalImageService = {
  getURL(options, imageConfig) {
    const config = imageConfig.service.config;
    return `${config.cdnUrl}/${options.src}?q=${options.quality}&w=${options.width}&h=${options.height}`;
  },
  getHTMLAttributes(options) {
    const { src, format, quality, ...attributes } = options;
    return {
      ...attributes,
      loading: 'lazy',
      decoding: 'async'
    };
  }
};

export default service;
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/image-service-reference/

`getStaticPaths()` runs in its own isolated scope once, before any page loads, so the `Astro` global (and any parent-scope variables) are unavailable — only file imports can be referenced; the compiler warns if you break this. Work around it by doing all data fetching inside the function and importing constants as modules, then reading route data in the page body, where `Astro.params` and `Astro.props` do work:

```astro
---
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const res = await fetch("...");
  const posts = await res.json();
  return posts.map((post) => ({ params: { id: post.id }, props: { post } }));
}
const { id } = Astro.params;
const { post } = Astro.props;
---
<h1>{id}: {post.name}</h1>
```

Fetch the Payload REST route (`/api/posts` by default), then map over the `posts.docs` array and render each `title` and `content` with `set:html` in `src/pages/index.astro`:

```astro
---
import HomeLayout from "../layouts/HomeLayout.astro";
const res = await fetch("http://localhost:5000/api/posts");
const posts = await res.json();
---
<HomeLayout title='Astro Blog'>
  {
    posts.docs.map((post) => (
      <h2 set:html={post.title} />
      <p set:html={post.content} />
    ))
  }
</HomeLayout>
```

Payload wraps the documents in `docs`; top-level fields are pagination metadata.

`getEntry()` from `astro:content` returns `Promise<CollectionEntry | undefined>`, so it does not throw if the entry is missing. To safely handle non-existent entries, check the result before accessing its properties.

```astro
---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
const post = await getEntry('blog', 'non-existent');
if (!post) {
  throw new Error("Post not found");
}
// Now safe to use post.data and post.body
---
```

For referenced entries, the same applies: verify the returned value is defined before rendering.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/

The "Remote image is not allowed" error occurs because the image URL does not match any entry in `image.domains` or `image.remotePatterns`. For a subdomain of your main domain, you must explicitly allow it since `domains` only accepts exact domain names (no wildcards).

```js
// astro.config.mjs
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ['example.com', 'blog.example.com'],
  },
});
```

If you need to allow all subdomains, use `remotePatterns` with a wildcard hostname instead: `hostname: '**.example.com'`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

For a secure configuration in Astro 7.3.5, use both together as complementary allowlists: `image.domains` for exact hostnames (no wildcards), and `image.remotePatterns` for flexible matching with protocol/hostname/pathname wildcards. Astro only optimizes remote images whose source URL matches at least one allowed entry.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ['cdn.example.com'],
    remotePatterns: [
      {
        protocol: 'https',
        hostname: '**.amazonaws.com',
        pathname: '/images/**',
      },
    ],
  },
});
```

This ensures you allow only the specific sources you intend while keeping the allowlist explicit and auditable.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

`Astro.redirect()` accepts the target path as a string, so include query parameters directly in that path. You can also optionally specify an HTTP status code (e.g., `302`, `303`) for on-demand rendered routes; for static prerendered routes, the status is not supported.

```astro
---
if (!authorized) {
  return Astro.redirect('/login?next=/dashboard');
}
---
```

```ts
// API route (on-demand)
export function GET({ redirect }: APIContext) {
  return redirect('/login?next=/dashboard', 303);
}
```

