Install Sharp manually with `pnpm add sharp` in your project root. Sharp is the default image service for `astro:assets`, and strict package managers like pnpm require it installed directly in your project for image processing to work (otherwise you get the `MissingSharp` error). If you aren't using `astro:assets` for image processing and don't want to install Sharp, configure the no-op passthrough service instead:

```js
import { defineConfig, passthroughImageService } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  image: { service: passthroughImageService() },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/missing-sharp/

`passthroughImageService()` configures a no-op image service that performs no processing, useful when your adapter does not support Astro's built-in Sharp image optimization but you still want to use the `<Image />` and `<Picture />` components. It avoids Sharp image processing entirely:

```js
import { defineConfig, passthroughImageService } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  image: { service: passthroughImageService() },
});
```

Without it, a non-Sharp-compatible adapter would fail; with it, components render without transforms.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-config/

In dev mode `cache.enabled` is always `false`, so `cache.set()` and `cache.invalidate()` are no-ops — your code runs without warnings but nothing is actually cached. Test caching locally by building then previewing. Gate caching logic on `Astro.cache.enabled`:

```astro
---
if (Astro.cache.enabled) {
  const tags = await getProductTags(Astro.params.id);
  Astro.cache.set({ maxAge: 3600, tags });
}
---
```

`cache.enabled` is `false` when no provider is configured or in development mode.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

Install `@astrojs/vue` and call `vue()` from the `integrations` array in `astro.config.mjs`:

