The `serialize` option in `@astrojs/sitemap` works around the Integration API's limitation by allowing per-page customization of sitemap metadata like `changefreq`, `lastmod`, and `priority`. It receives each sitemap entry as a `SitemapItem` and can modify or remove it before writing to disk.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap, { ChangeFreqEnum } from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  integrations: [
    sitemap({
      serialize(item) {
        if (/exclude-from-sitemap/.test(item.url)) {
          return undefined;
        }
        if (/your-special-page/.test(item.url)) {
          item.changefreq = ChangeFreqEnum.DAILY;
          item.lastmod = new Date().toISOString();
          item.priority = 0.9;
        }
        return item;
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

This function runs per page, enabling dynamic configuration based on URL or other criteria.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

To exclude a specific page like `https://example.com/secret-vip-lounge/` from your Astro sitemap, use the `filter()` configuration option in your `astro.config.mjs` file. The function receives each page's full URL and should return `false` to exclude it.

Example:
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  integrations: [
    sitemap({
      filter: (page) => page !== 'https://example.com/secret-vip-lounge/',
    }),
  ],
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

The minimum configuration needed for `@astrojs/sitemap` in Astro 7 is to import and add the integration to your `astro.config.mjs` file. However, you must also define the `site` property in your config, as it's required for generating correct URLs.

```mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  integrations: [sitemap()],
});
```

If you omit the `site` property, the integration will not work correctly because it needs a base URL to construct full page URLs for the sitemap. Without it, the generated sitemap entries would be incomplete or incorrect.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

For your use case, you should use the `filter()` option in the `sitemap()` integration.

The `filter()` function allows you to programmatically exclude pages based on their URL. It is called for every page on your site and returns `true` to include or `false` to exclude a page. This approach works well when you want to filter out entire directories like `/drafts/`.

In contrast, `customPages` is used to add externally-generated pages that are not created by Astro but should be included in the sitemap. It does not support exclusion logic and is meant for adding specific URLs outside of your normal site structure.

Example configuration:
```
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    sitemap({
      filter: (page) => !page.includes('/drafts/'),
    }),
  ],
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/#filter

The `lastmod` value you set globally in the sitemap configuration is applied site-wide unless overridden by the `serialize()` function. If some pages display a different `lastmod` in the generated sitemap.xml, it's because your `serialize()` function modifies those values for individual pages.

For example:

```js
sitemap({
  lastmod: new Date('2024-01-15'),
  serialize(item) {
    if (/special-page/.test(item.url)) {
      item.lastmod = new Date().toISOString(); // Overrides global lastmod
    }
    return item;
  },
});
```

This behavior is intentional and allows per-page customization of sitemap metadata. The `serialize()` function runs for each page, so any modifications made there take precedence over the global setting.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

To dynamically generate a `robots.txt` file with AI crawler rules in Astro, create an API route at `src/pages/robots.txt.ts`. Use the `Astro.response` object to set the content type and return the appropriate `robots.txt` content.

Example:

```ts
// src/pages/robots.txt.ts
export function GET() {
  const robotsTxt = `
User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: ClaudeBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: OAI-SearchBot
Allow: /

User-agent: ChatGPT-User
Allow: /

User-agent: *
Allow: /
`.trim();

  return new Response(robotsTxt, {
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8',
    },
  });
}
```

This route will be invoked at build time or runtime depending on your adapter and deployment setup. Ensure that the `User-agent` rules match those specified in the [AI crawlers spec](https://specification.website/spec/agent-readiness/robots-for-ai-crawlers/) for accurate compliance.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/agent-readiness/robots-for-ai-crawlers/

