To avoid `GetStaticPathsInvalidRouteParam`, ensure all `params` values are strings or `undefined`. Convert numbers and arrays to strings in your `getStaticPaths()` function.

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = [
    { id: 1 },
    { id: [1, 2] },
  ];

  return posts.map(({ id }) => ({
    params: {
      id: Array.isArray(id) ? id.join('-') : String(id),
    }
  }));
}
```

This ensures valid URL encoding for routes like `[id].astro`. For rest parameters `[...id].astro`, use `undefined` when no segments are needed. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-static-paths-invalid-route-param/

Yes, the fetched data will appear in the initial server-rendered HTML when using `client:visible` or other standard hydration directives like `client:load`, as long as the Solid component is not marked with `client:only`.

In Astro, Solid components are automatically wrapped in a top-level Suspense boundary during server rendering. This allows async data fetched via `createResource()` to be resolved and included in the initial HTML output before hydration occurs.

Example:
```tsx
// CharacterName.tsx
import { createResource } from 'solid-js';

function CharacterName() {
  const [name] = createResource(() =>
    fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/people/1')
      .then((res) => res.json())
      .then((data) => data.name)
  );

  return (
    <>
      <h2>Name:</h2>
      <div>{name()}</div>
    </>
  );
}
```

When used in an Astro page with `client:visible`, the component will render server-side with the resolved name, then hydrate on the client.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/solid-js/

You can use `withState()` from `@astrojs/react/actions` to integrate Astro Actions with React’s `useActionState()` hook for handling form submissions. Pass the action wrapped by `withState()` as the first argument to `useActionState()`, along with an initial state object.

