In Astro v7, when using the React integration, `compiler: true` enables the React Compiler with its default settings, while `compiler: { compilationMode: 'annotation' }` explicitly configures the React Compiler to only compile components that are annotated with `/* @refresh reset */` or `use client`.

The React Compiler is a new feature introduced in Astro v7.0 and replaces Babel for JSX processing. It offers performance improvements by pre-compiling components at build time.

When using `compiler: true`, all components will be compiled, which can improve performance but may require more careful handling of edge cases or interactions with other tools like Babel plugins.

Using `compiler: { compilationMode: 'annotation' }` is useful for incremental adoption. It allows you to selectively apply the compiler only where needed, reducing risk and complexity during migration.

Example configuration:
```js
import react from '@astrojs/react';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    react({
      compiler: {
        compilationMode: 'annotation'
      }
    })
  ]
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/react/

To use multiple JSX frameworks like React and Preact in the same Astro project, configure each framework integration with `include` patterns that specify which files belong to each framework. This is required starting from Astro v3.0.

Example configuration in `astro.config.mjs`:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import preact from '@astrojs/preact';
import react from '@astrojs/react';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    preact({
      include: ['**/preact/*'],
    }),
    react({
      include: ['**/react/*'],
    }),
  ],
});
```

Place framework-specific components in dedicated directories (e.g., `/components/preact/` and `/components/react/`) to simplify the `include` patterns. For TypeScript, set `jsxImportSource` in `tsconfig.json` and use pragma comments in conflicting files.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/preact/

Use a rest parameter route `[...slug].astro` when you want to match paths with zero or more URL segments, and `undefined` represents the case where no segments are provided. For example, `/route/` (no params) vs `/route/some/path`. A regular `[slug].astro` route requires a string param and will not match paths without the required segment.

Example:
```
// [...slug].astro
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { slug: '1' } }, // /route/1
    { params: { slug: undefined } }, // /route/
  ];
}
```

Returning `{ params: { id: undefined } }` from `getStaticPaths()` in a rest parameter route `[...id].astro` generates a path with no additional segments, such as `/route/`.

You would use this instead of a string value when you want to generate a URL that matches the route without any parameters. For example:

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } }, // /route/1
    { params: { id: undefined } }, // /route/
  ];
}
```

This is useful for generating a "default" or "empty" version of the route.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-static-paths-invalid-route-param/

Astro requires `getStaticPaths()` route parameters to be strings because they are encoded into URLs and must conform to URL path segments.

In your `/route/[id].astro` dynamic route, returning `{ params: { id: 2 } }` fails because the value for `id` is a number (`2`) instead of a string (`'2'`). Even though numbers can be converted to strings in JavaScript, Astro strictly enforces string types for route parameters.

To fix this error, convert numeric IDs to strings:

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } }, // Works
    { params: { id: '2' } }, // Works
  ];
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-static-paths-invalid-route-param/

When `getStaticPaths()` returns an object like `{ params: { id: '1' } }`, Astro uses the parameter values to construct a URL that matches the route pattern. For a file named `[id].astro` in the `pages/blog/` directory, the value of `id` becomes part of the final path.

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } },
    { params: { id: '2' } }
  ];
}
```

This generates URLs like `/blog/1` and `/blog/2`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/#getstaticpaths

Your `getStaticPaths()` function returned an array containing objects missing the required `params` property, or the `params` property was empty/undefined. Each object must have a `params` key with an object value specifying route parameters.

Example of invalid code:
```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: {} }, // ❌ Empty params
    { } // ❌ Missing params entirely
  ];
}
```

Correct usage:
```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } }, // ✅ Correct
  ];
}
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-static-paths-expected-params/

To generate the route `site.com/blog/1` from `pages/blog/[id].astro`, your `getStaticPaths()` function must return an array containing an object with a `params` property. The `params` object must include the key `id` with the value `'1'`.

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } }
  ];
}
```

This tells Astro to prerender the route `/blog/1` at build time. The `params` values must be strings because they are encoded into URLs.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/#getstaticpaths

To use `getImage()` results client-side, compute the image data during server render and pass it to the client via a `data-*` attribute or similar.

In your `.astro` file:

```astro
---import { getImage } from "astro:assets";import myImage from "../assets/my_image.png";
const optimizedImage = await getImage({ src: myImage, width: 300 });---
<img id="myImage" data-src={optimizedImage.src} data-width={optimizedImage.width} data-height={optimizedImage.height} />
<script>
  const img = document.getElementById('myImage');
  const src = img.dataset.src;
  const width = parseInt(img.dataset.width);
  const height = parseInt(img.dataset.height);
  // Update image at runtime
  img.src = src;
</script>
```

This approach ensures `getImage()` runs server-side, and the computed fields are passed to client code via DOM attributes. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-image-not-used-on-server/

The error occurs because `getImage()` is a server-only function and cannot be called on the client. To use an optimized image in client-side code, call `getImage()` during server rendering (e.g., in component frontmatter), then pass the resulting `src` to the client.

Example:
```astro
---
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import myImage from '../assets/my_image.png';

const optimizedImage = await getImage({ src: myImage, width: 300 });
---

<script>
  const imageSrc = {{ optimizedImage.src }};
  // Use imageSrc in client-side code
  document.getElementById('myImage').src = imageSrc;
</script>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-image-not-used-on-server/

Yes, you can use Astro's API routes to dynamically generate a `robots.txt` file based on the environment. This approach allows you to conditionally set rules like disallowing certain paths in staging or production.

Example implementation:

```ts
// src/pages/robots.txt.ts
export const GET = () => {
  const isProduction = import.meta.env.PROD;
  const disallowedPaths = isProduction ? ['/admin/', '/cart'] : ['/admin/', '/cart', '/staging'];

  return new Response(
    `User-agent: *
${disallowedPaths.map(path => `Disallow: ${path}`).join('\n')}
Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap.xml`,
    {
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' },
    }
  );
};
```

This route will be accessible at `/robots.txt` and can use environment variables to customize behavior between environments.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/seo/robots-txt/

You can dynamically generate a `robots.txt` file in Astro by creating a `src/pages/robots.txt.ts` API route. This route uses the `site` configuration value to construct the sitemap URL and return a `robots.txt` response.

Example implementation:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro';

const getRobotsTxt = (sitemapURL: URL) => `
User-agent: *
Allow: /
Sitemap: ${sitemapURL.href}
`;

export const GET: APIRoute = ({ site }) => {
  const sitemapURL = new URL('sitemap-index.xml', site);
  return new Response(getRobotsTxt(sitemapURL));
};
```

This approach ensures the `robots.txt` file always points to the correct sitemap location based on your configured `site` URL. Docs: https://specification.website/spec/seo/robots-txt/

To migrate from `getEntryBySlug` and `getDataEntryById` to the new Content Layer API in Astro v5+, replace both functions with `getEntry('collectionName', idOrSlug)`. Since content collections now use `id` instead of `slug`, update your code to pass the entry's ID. For example:

