Setting `middlewareMode` to `'edge'` causes Astro middleware to run on every request—including cache hits—because the middleware is executed in a Vercel Edge Function **before** the ISR cache is checked. This ensures that middleware logic runs consistently for all requests, even when the cached version of the page is served.

By default, with `middlewareMode: 'classic'`, Astro's middleware is bundled into the ISR function and only executes during cache misses or regenerations. When using `'edge'`, the middleware acts as a gatekeeper that runs prior to checking the cache, meaning it will execute for every incoming request regardless of whether the route is cached.

This behavior allows developers to perform authentication, logging, or other logic on each request, even if the content served is from the ISR cache. It also ensures that `context.locals` is available and updated per-request when using edge middleware.

Example configuration:
```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vercel from '@astrojs/vercel';

export default defineConfig({
  adapter: vercel({
    middlewareMode: 'edge',
    isr: true,
  }),
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/vercel/#running-astro-middleware-on-vercel-edge-functions

When using `middlewareMode: 'edge'` with the Vercel adapter, the `context.locals` object is serialized as JSON and passed via a header to the serverless function responsible for rendering on-demand pages. This ensures that the middleware's effects are available during rendering.

The serverless function validates that requests originate from the edge function before serving content, rejecting unauthorized requests with a 403 Forbidden response. The edge function runs before the ISR cache, so it executes on both cache misses and hits.

Example configuration:
```ts
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vercel from '@astrojs/vercel';

export default defineConfig({
  adapter: vercel({
    middlewareMode: 'edge',
  }),
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/vercel/#running-astro-middleware-on-vercel-edge-functions

To enable Incremental Static Regeneration (ISR) for on-demand rendered pages in the Vercel adapter, set `isr: true` in the adapter configuration:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vercel from '@astrojs/vercel';

export default defineConfig({
  // ...
  adapter: vercel({
    isr: true,
  }),
});
```

By default, when ISR is enabled, Astro bundles your middleware into the ISR function. This means it only runs on cache misses or regenerations, not on cache hits. If you need middleware to run on every request, set `middlewareMode` to `'edge'`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/vercel/

To configure the Vercel adapter to write Content Security Policy (CSP) headers to Vercel’s `vercel.json` instead of injecting a `<meta>` tag, you must enable two configuration flags in your `astro.config.mjs`:

1. Set `security.csp` to `true` to generate CSP headers.
2. Set `adapter: vercel({ staticHeaders: true })` to instruct the adapter to write those headers into `vercel.json`.

Example:
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vercel from '@astrojs/vercel';

export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    csp: true,
  },
  adapter: vercel({
    staticHeaders: true,
  }),
});
```

This approach requires `@astrojs/vercel@10.0.0` or later.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/vercel/#staticheaders

To configure the `@astrojs/sitemap` integration for a site deployed at `https://myblog.com`, add the integration to your `astro.config.mjs` and set the `site` option to your domain.

astro.config.mjs
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://myblog.com',
  integrations: [
    sitemap(),
  ],
