The format knowledge for a proprietary `.ledger` file must be implemented in a custom [live loader](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#creating-a-live-loader) passed to `defineLiveCollection()`. Astro will throw a **FileParserNotFound** error if no parser is provided when using the `file` loader, requiring you to supply a custom parser function.

Example setup:
```ts
// src/live.config.ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { ledgerLoader } from './loaders/ledger-loader';

const ledgerEntries = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: ledgerLoader({ filePath: './data/transactions.ledger' }),
});
```

If you forget to provide a parser, Astro raises `FileParserNotFound` and halts execution.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/file-parser-not-found/

Passing a `parser` to the `file()` loader is not required for every collection. It's only necessary when loading files that aren't natively supported (like CSV) or when parsing nested JSON documents.

For standard JSON, YAML, or TOML files, Astro automatically handles parsing without needing a custom parser.

Example of using a custom parser for CSV:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { file } from 'astro/loaders';
import { parse as parseCsv } from 'csv-parse/sync';

const products = defineCollection({
  loader: file('src/data/products.csv', {
    parser: (text) => parseCsv(text, { columns: true, skipEmptyLines: true }),
  }),
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/#parser

The `parser` function belongs as an option within the second argument of the `file()` loader call in your collection definition. It is passed as a property named `parser` inside an object.

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { file } from 'astro/loaders';

const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file('src/data/dogs.json', {
    parser: (text) => JSON.parse(text),
  }),
});
```

The value of `parser` must be a function that takes a string (`text`) as input and returns an array or object representing the parsed content. This allows you to define how non-standard formats like CSV are processed into collection entries.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/#parser

The "Font family not found" error occurs when the `cssVariable` passed to the `<Font />` component does not match a configured font. Ensure that the `cssVariable` value corresponds to a valid font defined in your `astro.config.mjs`.

Example configuration:
```js
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [
    {
      provider: fontProviders.fontsource(),
      name: "Cousine",
      cssVariable: "--font-cousine",
      fallbacks: ["monospace"]
    }
  ]
});
```

Then use it in your component:
```astro
import { Font } from 'astro:fonts';

<Font cssVariable="--font-cousine" />
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/fonts/

The `FileParserNotFound` error occurs when the `file()` loader encounters a file type it cannot parse by default, such as CSV, and no custom `parser` function is provided.

To resolve this, pass a `parser` function that converts the file's content into an array of objects with unique `id` properties. For example, using `csv-parse/sync`:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { file } from 'astro/loaders';
import { parse as parseCsv } from 'csv-parse/sync';

const cats = defineCollection({
  loader: file('src/data/cats.csv', {
    parser: (text) => parseCsv(text, { columns: true, skipEmptyLines: true }),
  }),
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/#parser

Astro’s `@astrojs/sitemap` integration does not automatically generate sitemap index files for sites exceeding the 50,000 URL limit. It produces a single sitemap file containing all routes.

To handle large sites, you must manually implement a sitemap index by splitting sitemaps into smaller groups and referencing them in an index file, as defined by the [sitemaps.org specification](https://specification.website/spec/seo/sitemap-index/). The integration itself does not manage this process. You can use `xslURL` to style your sitemaps if needed.

Example configuration:
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [sitemap({ xslURL: '/sitemap.xsl' })],
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

Use the `glob()` loader to create entries from a pattern matching multiple local files, such as all `.md` files in a directory.

Example setup:
```ts
// src/content.config.ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { glob } from 'astro/loaders';

const posts = defineCollection({
  loader: glob('src/posts/**/*.md'),
});

export const collections = { posts };
```

The `glob()` loader supports patterns like `**/*.md` to match files recursively. It automatically generates IDs based on file paths and parses frontmatter.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/#glob-loader

To preserve existing links when migrating from a custom i18n setup where all URLs include locale prefixes (including `/en/` for the default locale), set `prefixDefaultLocale: true` in your Astro configuration's `i18n.routing` object.

This ensures that even the default locale uses a URL prefix, maintaining consistency with your current URL structure. For example:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    locales: ['en', 'es', 'fr'],
    defaultLocale: 'en',
    routing: {
      prefixDefaultLocale: true
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/internationalization/

When `routing: "manual"` is set, all other `routing` options such as `prefixDefaultLocale`, `redirectToDefaultLocale`, and `fallbackType` are disallowed and will cause a configuration error.

Example config that causes an error:
```js
// astro.config.mjs
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    locales: ["es", "en", "fr"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
    routing: {
      prefixDefaultLocale: true, // ❌ Error: Cannot use with manual routing
      redirectToDefaultLocale: true, // ❌ Error: Cannot use with manual routing
      fallbackType: "redirect" // ❌ Error: Cannot use with manual routing
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/#i18nrouting

To configure the `@astrojs/sitemap` integration to only include specific pages, use the `filter()` function option. This function receives each page's full URL and should return `true` to include the page or `false` to exclude it.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import sitemap from '@astrojs/sitemap';

export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  integrations: [
    sitemap({
      filter: (page) => !page.includes('/private/'),
    }),
  ],
});
```

This example excludes all pages under the `/private/` path. You can customize the filtering logic to match your specific requirements.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/sitemap/

The `file()` loader reads multiple entries from a single local file (JSON, YAML, or TOML), requiring each entry to have a unique `id`. It's ideal for structured data like lists of items in a single file. The `glob()` loader reads multiple files matching a pattern (e.g., `**/*.md`), automatically generating IDs from filenames and supporting frontmatter parsing.

Use `file()` when your content is organized as an array or object within one file, such as a list of dogs or cats with predefined IDs. Use `glob()` when you have many individual files (like blog posts) that should be treated separately, each with its own frontmatter.

Example:
```ts
// Using file() loader
const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/dogs.json"),
});

// Using glob() loader
const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
});
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

To create a custom middleware that redirects all routes except `/about` to the default locale in Astro, use the `redirectToDefaultLocale` function from `astro:i18n`. This function is available only when `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"`.

Example:

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { redirectToDefaultLocale } from "astro:i18n";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  if (context.url.pathname.startsWith("/about")) {
    return next();
  } else {
    return redirectToDefaultLocale(context, 302);
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-i18n/#redirecttodefaultlocale

To map the French locale to `https://fr.example.com` and Spanish to `https://example.es` while keeping other locales on the main domain, configure `i18n.domains` in your `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';

export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
  output: 'server',
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  i18n: {
    defaultLocale: 'en',
    locales: ['en', 'fr', 'es'],
    routing: {
      prefixDefaultLocale: false,
    },
    domains: {
      fr: 'https://fr.example.com',
      es: 'https://example.es'
    }
  }
});
```

This configuration uses `i18n.domains` to specify custom domains for the `fr` and `es` locales. Other locales like `en` will use the main domain with path prefixes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/#i18ndomains

When using `i18n.fallback: { fr: 'es' }` with `routing.fallbackType: 'rewrite'`, visiting `/fr/missing-page` will display the content from `/es/missing-page` without redirecting. The URL in the browser remains `/fr/missing-page`, and the page is rendered using the fallback language's content.

Example config:
