Place a rest parameter file at `src/pages/[...slug].astro` and export a `getStaticPaths()` that returns one entry per depth, mapping each to `params` and `props`; in the rest-parameter route the root page uses `slug: undefined`, and nested paths use slash-separated strings.

```astro
---
export function getStaticPaths() {
  const pages = [
    { slug: undefined, title: "Astro Store", text: "Welcome to the Astro store!" },
    { slug: "products", title: "Astro products", text: "We have lots of products for you" },
    { slug: "products/astro-handbook", title: "The ultimate Astro handbook", text: "If you want to learn Astro, you must read this book." },
  ];
  return pages.map(({ slug, title, text }) => {
    return { params: { slug }, props: { title, text } };
  });
}
const { title, text } = Astro.props;
---
```

This generates `/`, `/products`, and `/products/astro-handbook`. Params must be strings or `undefined` (numbers, booleans, and arrays throw `GetStaticPathsInvalidRouteParam`), and `getStaticPaths()` must be exported in static mode or you get `GetStaticPathsRequired`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

Added in Astro 7.0, the default export of `src/fetch.ts` must be an object satisfying the `Fetchable` interface (imported as a type from `'astro'`), whose `fetch()` method receives a standard `Request` and must return a `Response`:

```ts
import type { Fetchable } from 'astro';
export default {
  async fetch(request) {
    return new Response("Hello from advanced routing!");
  }
} satisfies Fetchable;
```

This replaces Astro's built-in pipeline (trailing-slash normalization, redirects, sessions, actions, middleware, page rendering, i18n, caching) with your own. The entrypoint may be `.ts`, `.js`, `.mjs`, or `.mts`; its filename can be changed with the `fetchFile` config option or disabled with `fetchFile: null` — needed if you already have an unrelated `src/fetch.ts`, since v7 treats that name as reserved.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

Configured `redirects` in `astro.config.mjs` are static rules known at build time, while `Astro.redirect()` is a runtime check executed during page rendering, letting you redirect based on request data such as cookies. Use config redirects for permanently moved pages (they follow file-based route priority, lower than an existing page file, support dynamic routes like `"/blog/[...slug]": "/articles/[...slug]"` when parameters match, and external `http(s)` URLs); with no adapter they build to `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` HTML without status codes, while SSR or a static adapter serves `301` for GET (`308` otherwise), configurable via `{ status, destination }`.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
export default defineConfig({
  redirects: { "/old-page": { status: 302, destination: "/new-page" } }
});
```

Use `Astro.redirect()` for conditional, per-request decisions — e.g. sending logged-out users to `/login` after reading a cookie. Because of HTML streaming in on-demand rendering, it must be called at the page level, not in child components.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Read the parent page URL from the `Referer` header instead of `Astro.url`. Server islands run in their own isolated context outside the page request, so both `Astro.url` and `Astro.request.url` return `/_server-islands/MyComponent`. The `Referer` header contains the address of the page loading the island:

```astro
---
const referer = Astro.request.headers.get("Referer");
if (!referer) { throw new Error("Referer header is missing"); }
const url = new URL(referer);
const productId = url.searchParams.get("product");
---
```

Note that if the page itself is prerendered, query parameters aren't available to pass as props.

Nothing is rendered for that expression: with a falsy left-hand side the `&&` short-circuits, so the right-hand side HTML is never output. This is how Astro conditionally displays HTML using JSX logical operators:

```astro
---
const visible = true;
---
{visible && <p>Show me!</p>}
{visible ? <p>Show me!</p> : <p>Else show me!</p>}
```

The same applies when the left side is `null` — no HTML is emitted for that expression. Remember these values are computed once at render time and are dynamic, not reactive.

Each returned object must have a `params` property containing every named parameter in the filename, with the rest parameter carrying the remaining path segments unencoded and without a leading slash:

```js
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { org: "withastro", repo: "astro", branch: "main", file: "docs/public/favicon.svg" } },
  ];
}
```

That produces `/withastro/astro/tree/main/docs/public/favicon.svg`, and the page reads the values from `Astro.params`. Omitting `params` (or an empty one) raises `GetStaticPathsExpectedParams`, and not exporting `getStaticPaths()` at all raises `GetStaticPathsRequired` in static mode.

Create a server module that default-exports an object implementing the `SSRLoadedRendererValue` interface — typically providing `check()` to detect your components and `renderToStaticMarkup()` to produce their HTML — and register it with `serverEntrypoint` via `addRenderer()` in the `astro:config:setup` hook of your integration:

```js
// my-renderer/index.js
export default function createIntegration() {
  return {
    name: "@example/my-renderer",
    hooks: {
      "astro:config:setup": ({ addRenderer }) => {
        addRenderer({
          name: "@example/my-renderer",
          clientEntrypoint: "@example/my-renderer/client.js",
          serverEntrypoint: "@example/my-renderer/server.js",
        });
      },
    },
  };
}
```

`renderToStaticMarkup()` receives `Component`, `props`, `slots`, and optional `metadata`, and returns `{ html, attrs }`.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/renderer-reference/

Create a driver config module and a driver implementation module, then register the config in `astro.config.mjs`:

driver/config.ts
```
import type { SessionDriverConfig } from 'astro'
export interface Config { max?: number; }
export function memoryDriver(config: Config = {}): SessionDriverConfig {
    return { entrypoint: new URL('./runtime.js', import.meta.url), config }
}
```

driver/runtime.ts
```
import type { SessionDriver } from 'astro'
import type { Config } from './config'
import { LRUCache } from 'lru-cache'
export default function(config: Config): SessionDriver {
    const cache = new LRUCache({ max: config.max })
    return {
        setItem: async (key, value) => { cache.set(key, value) },
        getItem: async (key) => { return cache.get(key) },
        removeItem: async (key) => { cache.delete(key) },
    }
}
```

astro.config.mjs
```
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config'
import { memoryDriver } from './driver/config'
export default defineConfig({
    session: { driver: memoryDriver({ max: 800 }) }
})
```

Wrap your handler with `defineMiddleware()` imported from the virtual module `astro:middleware`, exporting the result as `onRequest`:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  /* your middleware logic */
});
```

The helper's type is `(fn: MiddlewareHandler) => MiddlewareHandler`. Its benefit: `context` and `next()` are automatically typed, and TypeScript errors if you return a value middleware doesn't support — `onRequest` must return a `Response`, either directly or from `next()`. For plain JS with JSDoc, use `/** @type {import("astro").MiddlewareHandler} */` instead. To type `Astro.locals`, declare `App.Locals` globally in `env.d.ts` for autocompletion inside middleware and `.astro` files.

Declare the `App.SessionData` interface in `src/env.d.ts`; each declared key gets its typed return value from `session.get()` with autocomplete, while undeclared keys fall back to `any`:

```ts
declare namespace App {
  interface SessionData {
    user: { id: string; name: string };
    cart: string[];
  }
}
```

Now `await Astro.session?.get('cart')` is typed `string[] | undefined`, and `Astro.session?.set('user', { id: 1, name: 'Houston' })` raises a type error since `id` must be a `string`. This is type-checking only and does not affect runtime behavior — changing types after users have stored data can cause runtime errors, since values are serialized with devalue (strings, numbers, `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `URL`, arrays, plain objects).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/sessions/

Because session drivers are configured at build time, so any environment variables used in the config are inlined into the build and cannot be overridden at runtime. Moving the driver into a separate file used as the driver's `entrypoint` defers that configuration to runtime, allowing live values (e.g. `REDIS_HOST`/`REDIS_PORT` from `astro:env/server`) to be read when the driver connects:

```js
session: {
  driver: {
    entrypoint: new URL("./src/session-driver.ts", import.meta.url),
  },
},
```

The entrypoint file exports a default function returning a `SessionDriver`, and its environment variables are evaluated at runtime rather than baked into the build.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/sessions/

They are the same session object — only the access path differs by context: `Astro.session` is available in `.astro` components and pages, while `context.session` (destructured from `APIContext`) is available in API endpoints, middleware, and actions. Both offer identical `get()`, `set()`, `regenerate()`, and `destroy()` methods.

Use `Astro.session?.get('cart')` when rendering a page or component (e.g. showing cart count in `Cart.astro`), and `session?.get("cart")` inside an endpoint handler when working with a `Request`/`Response`, such as a JSON API:

```ts
import type { APIContext } from "astro";
export async function GET({ session }: APIContext) {
  const cart = await session?.get("cart");
  return Response.json({ cart });
}
```

Note `session` is `undefined` (and logs an error) if no driver is configured, sessions are disabled, or the route is prerendered.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/

