```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v6/

In Astro 6, client build output configuration is scoped to Vite's client environment. Move your config from `vite.build.rollupOptions.output` to `vite.environments.client.build.rollupOptions.output`:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    environments: {
      client: {
        build: {
          rollupOptions: {
            output: {
              entryFileNames: 'js/[name]-[hash].js',
            },
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
});
```

Set the logger's level to `'silent'` — the `AstroLoggerLevel` value that enables silent logging (equivalent to the `--silent` CLI flag) — using the `logger` option added in `astro@7.0.0`:

```js
import { defineConfig, logHandlers } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  logger: logHandlers.json({ level: 'silent' })
});
```

Built-in handlers take a `level` option; handler-specific options depend on the configured logger, or you can supply your own handler via `logger.entrypoint` plus `logger.config` (e.g. `config: { level: 'silent' }`).

Add a redeclaration of the `astroHTML.JSX` namespace in `src/env.d.ts`:

```ts
declare namespace astroHTML.JSX {
  interface HTMLAttributes {
    "data-count"?: number;
    "data-label"?: string;
  }
  interface CSSProperties {
    "--theme-color"?: "black" | "white";
  }
}
```

`astroHTML` is injected globally inside `.astro` components, so this works in your components automatically. In plain TypeScript files, add the triple-slash directive first: `/// <reference types="astro/astro-jsx" />`, then reference e.g. `astroHTML.JSX.ImgHTMLAttributes`. Note that a `.d.ts` file must not contain top-level imports/exports, or TypeScript will treat it as a module augmentation and drop the global types.

Because a `.d.ts` file is an ambient module declaration: although its syntax resembles ES modules, it does not allow top-level imports/exports. If TypeScript encounters one, the file is treated as a module augmentation, which breaks your global types. Instead, use dynamic `import()` type syntax to reference the types inline:

```ts
declare namespace App {
  interface Locals {
    session: import("./lib/server/session").Session | null;
    user: import("my-external-library").User;
  }
}
```

Both relative paths and external package names work this way, and the types remain ambient (globally available) without turning the file into a module.

Await them as methods instead of accessing them as properties:

```js
// Before (Astro 5)
const mappings = manifest.serverIslandMappings;
const driver = manifest.sessionDriver;

// After (Astro 6)
const mappings = await manifest.serverIslandMappings?.();
const driver = await manifest.sessionDriver?.();
```

`serverIslandMappings` is typed as `() => Promise<ServerIslandMappings> | ServerIslandMappings` and `sessionDriver()` as `() => Promise<{ default: SessionDriverFactory | null }>`, both added in `astro@6.0.0`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v6/

Configure a local font provider by defining `variants` in `font.options` with required `weight`, `style`, and `src` properties. Each variant represents a `@font-face` declaration.

```js
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: fontProviders.local(),
    name: "Custom",
    cssVariable: "--font-custom",
    options: {
      variants: [
        { weight: 400, style: "normal", src: ["./src/assets/fonts/custom-400.woff2"] },
        { weight: 700, style: "normal", src: ["./src/assets/fonts/custom-700.woff2"] }
      ]
    }
  }]
});
```

Configure chunked collection storage with a custom 1MB chunk size in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  experimental: {
    collectionStorage: {
      type: "chunked",
      chunkSize: 1024 * 1024
    }
  }
});
```

This splits content collection data across multiple files in `.astro/data-store/` instead of a single file, useful for platforms with asset size limits. Default chunk size is 20MB. Added in Astro 7.1.0.

In Astro 6, access non-public variables in your code with `import.meta.env` (e.g. `import.meta.env.DB_PASSWORD`), not `process.env` — Astro does not rewrite them to `process.env` references. Non-public variables are server-side only: they cannot appear in client code at all; only `PUBLIC_`-prefixed variables reach the browser. Under SSR they resolve at runtime through the adapter (most use `process.env`, Deno uses `Deno.env.get()`), checking the server environment before falling back to `.env` files.

```js
// server-side only
const data = await db(import.meta.env.DB_PASSWORD);
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

Import Zod from `"astro/zod"` instead of `"astro:content"`, which no longer exports `z`:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { glob, file } from 'astro/loaders';

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ base: './src/content/blog', pattern: '**/*.{md,mdx}' }),
  schema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),
});
```

`astro/zod` is a re-export of the Zod library and supports all Zod 4 features, so `z.object()`, `z.coerce.date()`, `.transform()`, and the rest of your schema work unchanged in `src/content.config.ts`.

In Astro 6 the `target` parameter is gone: `astro:build:setup` runs once with all environments (`ssr`, `client`, `prerender`) configured via Vite's Environment API, so you configure the client environment through `vite.environments`:

```js
{
  hooks: {
    'astro:build:setup': ({ vite }) => {
      vite.environments.client.build.minify = false;
    }
  }
}
```

This replaces the Astro 5 pattern of branching on `target === 'client'` and setting `vite.build.minify`.

`NodeApp` is deprecated in Astro 6 (along with `NodeAppHeadersJson`, `loadManifest()`, and `loadApp()`) in favor of `createApp()` plus the `createRequest()` and `writeResponse()` utilities (both added in `astro@6.0.0`). The handler now converts the Node request itself and streams the response back:

```js
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';
import { createRequest, writeResponse } from 'astro/app/node';

const app = createApp();
export const handler = async (req, res) => {
  const request = createRequest(req);
  const response = await app.render(request);
  await writeResponse(response, res);
};
```

`createRequest(req, options?)` optionally takes `skipBody` and `allowedDomains`; `writeResponse()` returns a promise of the `ServerResponse`. Separately, the old `app.render(request, routeData, locals)` signature was removed — pass `{ routeData, locals }` as one options object.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-app/

Set `fetchFile: null` in your `astro.config.mjs` to disable the advanced routing entrypoint entirely:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  fetchFile: null,
});
```

This tells Astro not to look for `src/fetch.ts` (or `.js` / `.mjs` / `.mts`) as a routing entrypoint, so your existing file won't be processed as advanced routing configuration.

Astro 6.0 deprecated `astro:schema` and the `z` re-export from `astro:content`, consolidating everything on the `astro/zod` export. Two changes:

1. Replace `import { z } from "astro:schema"` with `import { z } from "astro/zod"`.
2. Remove `z` from your `astro:content` import and import it separately:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { z } from "astro/zod";
import { glob, file } from "astro/loaders";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/data/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({ title: z.string(), pubDate: z.coerce.date() }),
});
```

`astro/zod` is a re-export of Zod supporting all Zod 4 features, so schema usage is unchanged.

Move the logger configuration from `experimental.logger` to the top-level `logger` field in `astro.config.mjs`. Import `logHandlers` from `astro/config` and assign `logHandlers.json({ pretty: true })` directly to `logger`.

```js
import { defineConfig, logHandlers } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  logger: logHandlers.json({ pretty: true }),
});
```

Compare `event.type` against the string literal `'astro:before-preparation'`, e.g.:

```js
document.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', (event) => {
  if (event.type === 'astro:before-preparation') {
    const originalLoader = event.loader;
  }
});
```

The lifecycle event names are `astro:before-preparation`, `astro:after-preparation`, `astro:before-swap`, `astro:after-swap`, and `astro:page-load`, all fired on `document`. `TRANSITION_BEFORE_PREPARATION` and the `isTransitionBeforePreparationEvent()` helper are no longer importable from `astro:transitions/client`; the event name itself is the check.

Install `@astrojs/markdown-remark` (it is no longer installed by default in Astro 7), then set `unified()` as your Markdown processor in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { unified } from '@astrojs/markdown-remark';
import remarkImgAttr from 'remark-imgattr';
import rehypeGithubAlerts from 'rehype-github-alerts';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      remarkPlugins: [
        [remarkImgAttr, { defaults: { width: 700, format: 'avif' } }],
      ],
      rehypePlugins: [rehypeGithubAlerts],
    }),
  },
});
```

Options are passed as a nested array: `[plugin, optionsObject]`. The deprecated `markdown.remarkPlugins`, `markdown.rehypePlugins`, and `markdown.remarkRehype` options also still work, but they too now require `@astrojs/markdown-remark` to be installed.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/markdown-content/

`navigate()` performs no sanitization, so an attacker can craft `?redirect=http://example.com` to send users off your site, or `?redirect=javascript:alert('Evil code')` to execute arbitrary code (XSS). Mitigate by validating the value against an allowlist of known paths before navigating:

```js
import { navigate } from 'astro:transitions/client';
const redirect = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('redirect');
const allowedPaths = ['/home', '/about', '/contact'];
if (redirect && allowedPaths.includes(redirect)) navigate(redirect);
```

Enabling Astro's Content Security Policy feature also helps protect against XSS when using user input with `navigate()`.

Wrap the menu setup in an `astro:page-load` listener, which fires at the end of every client-side navigation:

```js
document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
  document.querySelector(".hamburger").addEventListener("click", () => {
    document.querySelector(".nav-links").classList.toggle("expanded");
  });
});
```

Alternatively, add `data-astro-rerun` to an inline script (`<script is:inline data-astro-rerun>...</script>`, added in `astro@4.5.0`) to force it to re-execute after every transition; adding any attribute implicitly makes the script `is:inline`.

The exact scripts object to add to `package.json` is:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "astro dev",
    "build": "astro build",
    "preview": "astro preview"
  }
}
```
These scripts map to the `astro dev`, `astro build`, and `astro preview` CLI commands respectively.

A custom animation passed to `transition:animate` must be a `TransitionDirectionalAnimations` object: `forwards` and `backwards` keys, each holding a `TransitionAnimationPair` with `old` and `new` entries, each of which is a `TransitionAnimation` or array of them.

```ts
export interface TransitionAnimation {
  name: string; // keyframe name
  delay?: number | string;
  duration?: number | string;
  easing?: string;
  fillMode?: string;
  direction?: string;
}
export interface TransitionAnimationPair {
  old: TransitionAnimation | TransitionAnimation[];
  new: TransitionAnimation | TransitionAnimation[];
}
export interface TransitionDirectionalAnimations {
  forwards: TransitionAnimationPair;
  backwards: TransitionAnimationPair;
}
```

Only `name` is required; you may use different objects or keyframes for forward vs. backward navigation. The keyframes themselves must be defined globally (e.g. `<style is:global>`), and the object is built in the frontmatter of every component using it.

Guard the global before initializing: `window` is preserved across view-transition navigations, while inline scripts may re-execute when a page is revisited, so check for existing state and initialize only once.

```html
<script is:inline>
  if (!window.analytics) {
    window.analytics = initAnalytics();
  }
</script>
```

Anything that must run on every navigation (per-page setup, binding new DOM) should go in an `astro:page-load` listener instead. Don't add `data-astro-rerun`, which forces re-execution after every transition and would recreate the duplicate initialization problem.

Install `@astrojs/rss`, then create a file in `src/pages/` with an `.xml.js` extension (e.g. `src/pages/rss.xml.js`). Import the `rss` helper and export a `GET` function that returns the feed:

```js
import rss from '@astrojs/rss';
export function GET(context) {
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz's Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut's guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    items: [],
    customData: `<language>en-us</language>`,
  });
}
```

Ensure `site` is set in your `astro.config` so article links are generated correctly. The feed is available at `site/rss.xml` and supports both static builds and on-demand generation with an SSR adapter. You can populate `items` using content collections or glob imports (e.g. `pagesGlobToRssItems`).

Add the plugin to the `vite.plugins` array in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import yaml from '@rollup/plugin-yaml';

export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    plugins: [yaml()]
  }
});
```

You can then import YAML with `import yml from './data.yml';`. Your editor won't infer types automatically, so add a declaration file in `src` (e.g. `src/files.d.ts`) with `declare module "*.yml" { const value: any; export default value; }` for type hints.

Use the `getImage()` function from `astro:assets` to generate each variant, then render them in a `<picture>` element with media queries:

```astro
---
import type { ImageMetadata } from "astro";
import { getImage } from "astro:assets";

interface Props {
  mobileImgUrl: string | ImageMetadata;
  desktopImgUrl: string | ImageMetadata;
  alt: string;
}

const { mobileImgUrl, desktopImgUrl, alt } = Astro.props;

const mobileImg = await getImage({ src: mobileImgUrl, format: "webp", width: 200, height: 200 });
const desktopImg = await getImage({ src: desktopImgUrl, format: "webp", width: 800, height: 200 });
---
<picture>
  <source media="(max-width: 799px)" srcset={mobileImg.src} />
  <source media="(min-width: 800px)" srcset={desktopImg.src} />
  <img src={desktopImg.src} alt={alt} />
</picture>
```

Pass imported images as props: `<MyCustomImageComponent mobileImgUrl={mobileImage} desktopImgUrl={desktopImage} alt="user profile picture" />`.

The editor lacks type hints because `@rollup/plugin-yaml` only handles runtime transformation, not TypeScript declarations. To fix this, create a `src/files.d.ts` (or any `*.d.ts` in `src/`) with:

```ts
declare module "*.yml" {
  const value: any;
  export default value;
}
```

This declares the module shape for TypeScript, enabling IntelliSense for `.yml` imports. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/add-yaml-support/

Install the plugin and add it to `astro.config.mjs`:

```bash
npm install rollup-plugin-visualizer --save-dev
```

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { visualizer } from "rollup-plugin-visualizer";

export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    plugins: [visualizer({ emitFile: true, filename: "stats.html" })]
  }
});
```

Run `npm run build`. The `stats.html` report appears at `dist/stats.html` (static sites) or `dist/client/stats.html` and `dist/server/stats.html` (on-demand rendering). Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/analyze-bundle-size/

Install `@rollup/plugin-yaml` as a dev dependency, then import it in `astro.config.mjs` and add it to the Vite plugins array:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import yaml from '@rollup/plugin-yaml';

export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    plugins: [yaml()]
  }
});
```

You can then import YAML files directly:

```js
import yml from './data.yml';
```

For TypeScript support, add a `*.d.ts` file in `src` declaring the module. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/add-yaml-support/

Set `vite.environments.client.build.rollupOptions.output.entryFileNames` in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    environments: {
      client: {
        build: {
          rollupOptions: {
            output: {
              entryFileNames: 'js/[name]-[hash].js',
            },
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
});
```

Paths are relative to `outDir`. Note: since Astro 6.0, client build output config is scoped to Vite's client environment (the Astro 5.x path `vite.build.rollupOptions.output` no longer applies). These patterns apply only to production builds (`npm run build`) and affect only project `src` assets, not `public/`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/customizing-output-filenames/

In frontmatter, check the method and await the parsed body:

```astro
---
export const prerender = false; // Not needed in 'server' mode
if (Astro.request.method === "POST") {
  const data = await Astro.request.formData();
  const name = data.get("username");
}
---
```

`Astro.request` is a standard `Request` object, so `formData()` returns a `FormData` you read with `.get(name)`. This only works in on-demand rendered pages (a server adapter installed). Alternatively, define an action with `accept: 'form'` and read the submission via `Astro.getActionResult()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/build-forms/

The correct nested structure is `vite.environments.client.build.rollupOptions.output`:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    environments: {
      client: {
        build: {
          rollupOptions: {
            output: {
              entryFileNames: 'js/[name]-[hash].js',
              chunkFileNames: 'js/chunks/[name]-[hash].js',
              assetFileNames: 'static/[name]-[hash][extname]',
            },
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
});
```

This scoping to the client environment was introduced in Astro 6.0; in Astro 5.x these options were configured at `vite.build.rollupOptions.output`.

Yes — you can store any type of data inside `locals`: strings, numbers, and complex types such as functions, Maps, arrays, and nested objects, assigned as properties on the existing object (never reassigning `locals` itself):

```js
export function onRequest (context, next) {
    context.locals.user = { id: 1, name: "John Wick" };
    context.locals.welcomeTitle = () => {
        return "Welcome back " + context.locals.user.name;
    };
    context.locals.orders = new Map([["1", { product: "socks" }]]);
    return next();
};
```

Read them in a page via `Astro.locals.welcomeTitle()` or `Array.from(Astro.locals.orders.entries())`. Runtime caveat: with the Vercel adapter's `middlewareMode: 'edge'`, `context.locals` is JSON-serialized into a header for the serverless function, so on that path values must survive JSON serialization.

Use the `image()` schema helper whenever a local image is referenced from a content entry's frontmatter: it validates and imports the image, turning it into metadata you can pass directly as `src` to `<Image/>`, `<img>`, or `getImage()`. Direct imports suit images referenced by a component itself; a string filepath passed as `src` throws `LocalImageUsedWrongly`.

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
const blogCollection = defineCollection({
  schema: ({ image }) => z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    cover: image(),
    coverAlt: z.string(),
  }),
});
```

Note: the old `image` export from `astro:content` was removed in v3 (use the `schema` helper), and `image().refine()` custom validation is unsupported.

Guard the lookup before calling it: `if (!images[imagePath])` — then handle the miss gracefully (skip the image or render a fallback) instead of invoking. The crash you're seeing is thrown by the recipe's own guard, because a CMS-supplied path that isn't in the glob makes `images[imagePath]` `undefined`, and calling `undefined()` fails. Since CMS paths can drift from files on disk, replace the `throw` with a fallback branch so an unknown path renders without breaking the component:

```astro
---
const images = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>('/src/assets/*.{jpeg,jpg,png,gif}');
const image = images[imagePath];
---
{image ? <Image src={image()} alt={altText} /> : null}
```

Send the `secret` and `token` to Google's verification API as an `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` form body using `URLSearchParams`, sent via `POST` to `https://www.google.com/recaptcha/api/siteverify`.

```js
export async function POST({ request }) {
  const data = await request.json();
  const recaptchaURL = 'https://www.google.com/recaptcha/api/siteverify';
  const requestBody = new URLSearchParams({
    secret: "YOUR_SITE_SECRET_KEY", // or environment variable
    response: data.recaptcha
  });
  const response = await fetch(recaptchaURL, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: requestBody.toString()
  });
  const responseData = await response.json();
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(responseData), { status: 200 });
}
```

Install both packages (`npm install @astrojs/markdown-satteri satteri`), define the plugin with `defineHastPlugin` from `satteri` (e.g. in `src/hast/hast-external-links.ts`), then import it and pass it to `hastPlugins` under a `satteri()` processor in `astro.config.mjs`:

```ts
import { satteri } from '@astrojs/markdown-satteri';
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { hastExternalLinks } from './src/hast/hast-external-links';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: satteri({
      hastPlugins: [hastExternalLinks],
    }),
  },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/external-links/

The `init()` function of a dev toolbar app receives `canvas` as its first argument, which is a standard `ShadowRoot`. You add DOM elements to it using standard DOM APIs such as `canvas.appendChild()` or `canvas.append()`. Each app gets its own dedicated ShadowRoot, and the parent element is positioned with `position: absolute` so it doesn't affect page layout.

```
import { defineToolbarApp } from "astro/toolbar";
export default defineToolbarApp({
  init(canvas) {
    const h1 = document.createElement("h1");
    h1.textContent = "Hello!";
    canvas.append(h1);
  },
});
```

`init()` runs only once, when the app is first loaded (when the browser is idle or the user first clicks the app, whichever comes first), and it receives `canvas`, `app`, and `server` to build the app's core behavior. `onToggled()` is not a top-level app hook but a client-side event helper registered on the `app` EventTarget inside `init()`; it fires every time the app's active state changes, passing a `state` boolean:

```js
import { defineToolbarApp } from "astro/toolbar";
export default defineToolbarApp({
  init(canvas, app) {
    app.onToggled(({ state }) => {
      canvas.appendChild(document.createTextNode(`The app is now ${state ? "enabled" : "disabled"}!`));
    });
  },
});
```

For intercepting the moment the app is switched off (e.g. confirmation or cleanup), use the separate top-level `beforeTogglingOff()` hook, which returns a boolean to allow or cancel toggling off.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/dev-toolbar-app-reference/

Create a remark mdast plugin that writes to `file.data.astro.frontmatter`, then register it via the `unified()` processor from `@astrojs/markdown-remark` in `markdown.processor`.

remark-modified-time.mjs:

```js
import { execSync } from "node:child_process";
export function remarkModifiedTime() {
  return function (tree, file) {
    const filepath = file.history[0];
    const result = execSync(`git log -1 --pretty="format:%cI" "${filepath}"`);
    file.data.astro.frontmatter.lastModified = result.toString();
  };
}
```

astro.config.mjs:

```js
import { unified } from "@astrojs/markdown-remark";
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import { remarkModifiedTime } from "./remark-modified-time.mjs";

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      remarkPlugins: [remarkModifiedTime],
    }),
  },
});
```

Install `dayjs` and `@astrojs/markdown-remark`. Every Markdown document then has a `lastModified` frontmatter property; in a content collection, read it from `remarkPluginFrontmatter` returned by `render(entry)`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/modified-time/

They are two different Markdown processors with different packages, plugin APIs, and config keys. `@astrojs/markdown-remark` uses the `unified()` processor with `remarkPlugins`, where the plugin is a plain function `(tree, file)` that resolves the path from `file.history[0]` and sets `file.data.astro.frontmatter.lastModified`. `@astrojs/markdown-satteri` uses the `satteri()` processor with `mdastPlugins`, where the plugin is created with `defineMdastPlugin()` from `satteri`, uses a `before(root, context)` hook, resolves the path from `context.fileURL` via `fileURLToPath`, and writes to `context.data.astro.frontmatter.lastModified`.

```js
// Sätteri
markdown: { processor: satteri({ mdastPlugins: [mdastModifiedTimePlugin] }) }
// Unified
markdown: { processor: unified({ remarkPlugins: [remarkModifiedTime] }) }
```

Both require installing `dayjs` plus their respective processor packages (`satteri` additionally needs the `satteri` package). Displaying the value is identical for both.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/modified-time/

The recipe's plugin runs `git log -1` to derive the timestamp, but some deployment hosts perform shallow clones that don't retrieve the full Git history, so `git log` can't return an accurate (or any) commit date — the recipe warns the result may be inaccurate on such platforms.

The suggested mitigation is to use the file system's modified time instead of Git: replace the `execSync` Git call with `statSync` and use `mtime`:

```js
import { statSync } from "node:fs";
export function remarkModifiedTime() {
  return function (tree, file) {
    const filepath = file.history[0];
    const result = statSync(filepath);
    file.data.astro.frontmatter.lastModified = result.mtime.toISOString();
  };
}
```

The same substitution applies to the Sätteri variant (`statSync(filepath).mtime.toISOString()` written to `context.data.astro.frontmatter.lastModified`). Note Git remains the recommended source when full history is available.

Create a server endpoint (API route) that accepts the reCAPTCHA token from the client and verifies it with Google's API. The secret key is defined server-side (e.g., as an environment variable) and never exposed to the client. The client sends the token to your endpoint via `fetch`, and the endpoint returns the verification result.

```js
// src/pages/recaptcha.js
export async function POST({ request }) {
  const data = await request.json();
  const recaptchaURL = 'https://www.google.com/recaptcha/api/siteverify';
  const requestBody = new URLSearchParams({
    secret: "YOUR_SITE_SECRET_KEY",
    response: data.recaptcha
  });
  const response = await fetch(recaptchaURL, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: requestBody.toString()
  });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(await response.json()), { status: 200 });
}
```

The client calls this endpoint with the token obtained from the reCAPTCHA widget.

The error occurs when you pass a function as the `schema` property in `defineLiveCollection()`. Live collections require a schema object (e.g., a Zod `z.object({...})`), not a function. Replace the function with a schema object:

```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: storeLoader({ /* ... */ }),
  schema: z.object({
    name: z.string(),
    price: z.number(),
  }),
});
```

Note that for live collections, the schema is optional. If you omit it, the loader's types are used instead.

Return an array of objects from `getStaticPaths()`, each with a `params` object whose property values are strings (or `undefined` for rest parameters). For `/pages/blog/[id].astro`:
```js
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: "blog" } },
    { params: { id: "about" } }
  ];
}
```
Values must be strings — numbers, booleans, or arrays will cause a `GetStaticPathsInvalidRouteParam` error.

The `@astrojs/rss` helper produces trailing-slash links by default regardless of your `trailingSlash` config setting, which only governs Astro's own routing—not the feed generator. To make feed links match `trailingSlash: 'never'`, explicitly pass `trailingSlash: false` to the `rss()` helper:

```js
import rss from '@astrojs/rss';
export function GET(context) {
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz’s Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut’s guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    trailingSlash: false,
    items: posts.map((post) => ({ link: post.url, ...post.frontmatter })),
  });
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

Yes. Import `rssSchema` from `@astrojs/rss` and apply it as your collection schema so every entry is validated against the expected RSS properties at the schema level:

```ts
// src/content.config.ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { rssSchema } from '@astrojs/rss';
const blog = defineCollection({
  schema: rssSchema,
});
export const collections = { blog };
```

This ensures each blog entry produces a valid RSS feed item without defining individual properties yourself.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

Set the `content` key on each `rss.items` entry to the post's rendered HTML (available since `astro@1.6.14`). With content collections, parse the post `body` with a Markdown parser and sanitize it:

```js
import rss from '@astrojs/rss';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import sanitizeHtml from 'sanitize-html';
import MarkdownIt from 'markdown-it';
const parser = new MarkdownIt();
export async function GET(context) {
  const blog = await getCollection('blog');
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz’s Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut’s guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    items: blog.map((post) => ({
      link: `/blog/${post.id}/`,
      content: sanitizeHtml(parser.render(post.body), {
        allowedTags: sanitizeHtml.defaults.allowedTags.concat(['img']),
      }),
      ...post.data,
    })),
  });
}
```

Note: this does not process components or JSX in MDX files. Relative-path images and internal links are unsupported—use full URL paths. With glob imports, use `compiledContent()` instead (not supported for MDX).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

`getCollection()` (from `astro:content`) queries content collections with performant, content-focused APIs, returns entries with frontmatter on `post.data` and an `id`/`slug` (not a full URL), and can be validated with `rssSchema`. You map each entry to item properties yourself. `import.meta.glob()` requires wrapping in `pagesGlobToRssItems()` (added in `@astrojs/rss@2.1.0`), accesses frontmatter via `post.frontmatter`, and provides `post.url`—but it assumes, without verifying, that all required feed properties exist in frontmatter. Per the imports guide, content collections are the preferred replacement for `import.meta.glob()` when loading content files:

```js
import rss, { pagesGlobToRssItems } from '@astrojs/rss';
export async function GET(context) {
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz’s Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut’s guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    items: await pagesGlobToRssItems(import.meta.glob('./blog/*.{md,mdx}')),
  });
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

Move the file to `src/content.config.ts` and ensure every collection has a `loader` defined; the legacy `src/content/config.ts` location is unsupported (removed in Astro 6) and throws `LegacyContentConfigError`. Collections without loaders throw `ContentCollectionMissingLoader`.

```ts
// src/content.config.ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { file } from "astro/loaders";

const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/dogs.json"),
});

export const collections = { dogs };
```

Install `@astrojs/markdown-satteri` (plus `satteri`, which provides `defineHastPlugin`), import `satteri` instead of `unified` from `@astrojs/markdown-remark`, and rename the keys: `rehypePlugins` → `hastPlugins`, `remarkPlugins` → `mdastPlugins`. The plugin must be a Sätteri hast plugin created with `defineHastPlugin` from `satteri` and passed directly in the array — options go as an object to the plugin function (e.g. `callouts({...})`), not as a `[plugin, options]` tuple as with unified.

```js
import { satteri } from '@astrojs/markdown-satteri';
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { hastExternalLinks } from './src/hast/hast-external-links';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: satteri({
      hastPlugins: [hastExternalLinks],
    }),
  },
});
```

Keep `unified()` from `@astrojs/markdown-remark` if you still need remark/rehype plugins.

Set `previewEntrypoint` in `setAdapter()` to a module path (e.g., `'@example/my-adapter/preview.js'`). That module must default-export a function accepting server config (host, port) and returning a `PreviewServer`. This enables `astro preview` for hybrid/server output adapters.

```js
// my-adapter.mjs
setAdapter({
  name: '@example/my-adapter',
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
  previewEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/preview.js',
});
```

```ts
// my-adapter/preview.ts
import type { CreatePreviewServer } from 'astro';
export default async (config) => {
  const host = config.host ?? '0.0.0.0';
  const port = config.port ?? 4321;
  return createServer(host, port);
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

`"auto"` (recommended): you provide a valid module via `serverEntrypoint` or Vite `rollupOptions.input`; Astro handles resolution. `"explicit"` (deprecated, default in v6): you must export host-required values using `createExports()` in the server entrypoint and pass them via `exports` to `setAdapter()`. New adapters should use `"auto"`; `"explicit"` supports legacy v5 adapters only.

```js
// auto (recommended)
setAdapter({
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
});

// explicit (deprecated)
setAdapter({
  entrypointResolution: 'explicit',
  exports: createExports({ ... }),
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Enable `adapterFeatures: { staticHeaders: true }` in `setAdapter()`. Astro then provides a `routeToHeaders` map in the `astro:build:generated` hook containing headers for static pages (e.g., CSP). Use this map to generate platform-specific header files like `_headers` or framework config.

```js
setAdapter({
  name: '@example/my-adapter',
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
  adapterFeatures: { staticHeaders: true },
});
```

```js
// In hook
'astro:build:generated': ({ routeToHeaders }) => {
  // generate platform header config from routeToHeaders
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Never set `astro.frontmatter` to `null`, `undefined`, or a non-object — instead append/modify properties on the existing `data.astro.frontmatter` object, which already contains all of the document's frontmatter.

Unified remark plugin (example-remark-plugin.mjs):
```js
export function exampleRemarkPlugin() {
  return function (tree, file) {
    file.data.astro.frontmatter.newProperty = "New property";
    file.data.astro.frontmatter.computedProperty = `${file.data.astro.frontmatter.title} | My Site Name`;
  };
}
```
Register it via `markdown.processor: unified({ remarkPlugins: [exampleRemarkPlugin] })` in `astro.config.mjs`. Setting frontmatter to `null`/`undefined` or an invalid JSON object is exactly what triggers `InvalidFrontmatterInjectionError`. A syntax mistake in the file's own frontmatter instead causes a `MarkdownFrontmatterParseError`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/invalid-frontmatter-injection-error/

Both are `AdapterSupportsKind` values (added in `astro@5.0.0`) used in `supportedAstroFeatures`: `"limited"` means the adapter supports a subset of the full feature — use it when the feature works but not in all cases or with all options, typically paired with a `message` object explaining the constraints in the CLI. `"unsupported"` means the feature isn't supported and users may encounter build issues — use it to warn them upfront. Any value can be an object `{ support, message }`.

```js
supportedAstroFeatures: {
  sharpImageService: {
    support: "limited",
    message: 'This adapter supports the built-in sharp image service, but with some limitations.'
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

The `middlewareEntryPoint` will be `undefined` in the `astro:build:ssr` hook if your adapter doesn't opt in to the `middlewareMode: 'edge'` feature. You must check for this before using it:

```js
'astro:build:ssr': ({ middlewareEntryPoint }) => {
  if (middlewareEntryPoint) {
    createEdgeMiddleware(middlewareEntryPoint)
  }
}
```

Without `middlewareMode: 'edge'` in `adapterFeatures`, the middleware code is bundled with the server bundle (classic mode) and no separate entry point is exposed.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Astro reads the `supportedAstroFeatures` map (added in Astro 3.0.0) to determine each feature's support level (`stable`, `experimental`, `limited`, or unsupported). Based on the configured support level, the desired logging verbosity, and the user's project configuration, Astro will:
- Run specific validation checks for features marked as unsupported or limited
- Emit contextual warnings/errors to the CLI logs (e.g., "The feature is experimental and subject to issues or changes" or "Your project will NOT be able to build")
- Optionally include custom `message` strings from the adapter for limited support

```js
supportedAstroFeatures: {
  sharpImageService: {
    support: 'limited',
    message: 'This adapter has limited support for Sharp. Certain features may not work as expected.'
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Create a preview entrypoint module that exports a `CreatePreviewServer` function, then register it via `previewEntrypoint` in your adapter:

```ts
// my-adapter/preview.ts
import type { CreatePreviewServer } from 'astro';
import { createServer } from './utils/server';

const createPreviewServer: CreatePreviewServer = async (config) => {
  const host = config.host ?? '0.0.0.0';
  const port = config.port ?? 4321;
  const server = createServer(host, port);
  return server;
};

export default createPreviewServer;
```

```js
// my-adapter.mjs
setAdapter({
  name: '@example/my-adapter',
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
  previewEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/preview.js',
