The function receives `config` with `host` and `port` and must return a `PreviewServer` object with `port`, `host`, `urls`, `closed()`, and `stop()` methods.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

For a route like `[category]/[id].astro`, return an array of objects where each `params` contains both parameters:

```astro
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { category: 'tech', id: '1' } },
    { params: { category: 'news', id: '2' } }
  ];
}
```

Each `params` key must match the folder/file parameter names in the route path.

Use `Astro.response`, a standard `ResponseInit` object. Set `Astro.response.status` to the numeric code and `Astro.response.statusText` to the status message:

```
---
import { getPost } from "../api";
const post = await getPost(Astro.params.id);
if (!post) {
  Astro.response.status = 404;
  Astro.response.statusText = "Not found";
}
---
```

You can also set headers via `Astro.response.headers.set()`.

Pass a `Request` as the rewrite payload, constructing it with the target URL and a `headers` option; in a page you'd write:

```astro
---
return Astro.rewrite(new Request(new URL("../", Astro.url), {
  headers: {
    "x-custom-header": JSON.stringify(Astro.locals.someValue)
  }
}));
---
```

The same works from an endpoint via `context.rewrite()`. These headers are sent with the request that renders the parent page, so middleware and the parent route see them. Note that `Astro.rewrite()` cannot be used after the request body has been read — clone the request first (`Astro.request.clone().formData()`).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/

Call `redirect()` from the `APIContext` and return its `Response` from the handler:

```ts
import type { APIContext } from "astro";
import { isLoggedIn } from "../utils";

export function GET({ redirect, request }: APIContext) {
  const cookie = request.headers.get("cookie");
  if (!isLoggedIn(cookie)) {
    return redirect("/login", 302);
  } else {
    // return user information
  }
}
```

`redirect(path, status?)` was added in `astro@1.5.0`; the status is optional and defaults to `302` on on-demand routes (custom status codes are not supported for statically-generated routes, which use a `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` tag).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/

Set `middlewareMode: 'edge'` in `adapterFeatures` and consume the `astro:build:ssr` hook. The hook provides `middlewareEntryPoint` (a URL to the physical file), which will be `undefined` if the feature isn't opted into. Use it to emit an edge middleware file via your bundler.

```js
'adapterFeatures': { middlewareMode: 'edge' },
// ...
'astro:build:ssr': ({ middlewareEntryPoint }) => {
  if (middlewareEntryPoint) {
    createEdgeMiddleware(middlewareEntryPoint)
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

You're missing the type declarations in `src/env.d.ts`; `.env` provides the runtime value but not the TypeScript types. Add:

```
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly NEON_DATABASE_URL: string;
}
interface ImportMeta {
  readonly env: ImportMetaEnv;
}
```

This augments `ImportMetaEnv` so TypeScript recognizes `import.meta.env.NEON_DATABASE_URL`, which is described as being for better TypeScript support. Note that `tsc` ignores `.astro` files; use `astro check` to check both `.astro` and `.ts` files.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/neon/

`.env` provides the actual value at runtime — Astro loads `NEON_DATABASE_URL` from the project-root `.env` file into `import.meta.env` — while the declarations in `src/env.d.ts` only improve TypeScript support by typing the variable:

```
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly NEON_DATABASE_URL: string;
}
interface ImportMeta {
  readonly env: ImportMetaEnv;
}
```

So `.env` supplies the connection string (with `.env.development`/`.env.production` for branching); `src/env.d.ts` merely makes `import.meta.env.NEON_DATABASE_URL` type-check.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/neon/

Define the cart in a shared module with `map()`, keyed by item ID, and update single entries with `.setKey()` so only that key is rewritten instead of the whole store:

```ts
// src/cartStore.ts
import { atom, map } from 'nanostores';

export const isCartOpen = atom(false);
export type CartItem = { id: string; name: string; imageSrc: string; quantity: number };
export const cartItems = map<Record<string, CartItem>>({});

export function addCartItem({ id, name, imageSrc }: Pick<CartItem, 'id' | 'name' | 'imageSrc'>) {
  const existing = cartItems.get()[id];
  if (existing) {
    cartItems.setKey(id, { ...existing, quantity: existing.quantity + 1 });
  } else {
    cartItems.setKey(id, { id, name, imageSrc, quantity: 1 });
  }
}
```

Subscribe only where the value is rendered: use `useStore(cartItems)` in the UI, but plain `.get()` inside event handlers, since `useStore` is what triggers re-renders. Write to the store from hydrated components or `<script>` tags — never from `.astro` frontmatter or non-hydrated components, whose writes don't reach client-side components.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/sharing-state-islands/

Return `Astro.redirect("/login")` from the page frontmatter after processing the POST:

```js
if (!hasErrors) {
  await registerUser({ name, email, password });
  return Astro.redirect("/login");
}
```

This requires server rendering (e.g. `export const prerender = false` in `'server'` mode).

Client directives only work on UI framework components (React, Svelte, etc.) directly imported into a `.astro` file; a plain Astro component has no hydration runtime, so `client:load` does nothing. Add interactivity with a `<script>` tag inside the component instead:

```astro
<button class="card-btn">Click</button>
<script>
  document.querySelector('.card-btn')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // handle click
  });
</script>
```

Create `src/middleware.js|ts`, or alternatively `src/middleware/index.js|ts`, and export `onRequest` from it as a **named** export — it must not be a default export. The function receives `context` and `next()` and must return a `Response`, either directly or from calling `next()`:

```js
// src/middleware.js
export function onRequest(context, next) {
  context.locals.title = "New title";
  return next();
}
```

With TypeScript you can wrap it in `defineMiddleware()` from `"astro:middleware"`, still as a named export: `export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {...});`

Since Astro 5.0, `locals` can no longer be completely overridden in middleware, API endpoints, or pages — Astro performs runtime checks and throws an error if the `locals` object itself is replaced (this was allowed in Astro 4.x). Assign properties onto the existing object instead:

```js
// src/middleware.js
export function onRequest(context, next) {
  context.locals.user = { id: 1, name: "John" };
  context.locals.title = "Hi";
  return next();
}
```

or, if you're merging several keys at once, `Object.assign(ctx.locals, { one: 1, two: 2 })`. Writing individual properties on `locals` (like `locals.title = ...`) remains fully supported.

Export a `GET` function from a `.js`/`.ts` file in `src/pages/` and have it return a `Response`:

```js
// src/pages/[id].json.js
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { getProduct } from "../db";

export const GET = (async ({ params }) => {
  const product = await getProduct(params.id);
  if (!product) return new Response(null, { status: 404, statusText: "Not found" });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(product), {
    status: 200,
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  });
}) satisfies APIRoute;
```

Two requirements: (1) on-demand rendering must be enabled — routes render on demand by default in `server` mode (deploying requires an adapter), while in `static` mode you must opt each endpoint out of prerendering with `export const prerender = false`; (2) the handler must return a `Response` (set `Content-Type: application/json` for JSON), since the endpoint doesn't return a string by itself.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/endpoints/

HTML attributes in Astro are converted to strings, so functions can't be passed to HTML elements — your `onClick={handleClick}` never registers a listener, and the button does nothing. Use a client-side `<script>` tag instead and attach the handler in vanilla JavaScript:

```astro
---
---
<button id="button">Click Me</button>
<script>
  function handleClick () {
    console.log("button clicked!");
  }
  document.getElementById("button")?.addEventListener("click", handleClick);
</script>
```

Astro components render to HTML with no client runtime, so interactivity must come from a plain script (or a hydrated framework component).

Redirects must be returned from the page component, not a child component. `Astro.redirect()` only works when the page itself returns its response; child components cannot trigger redirects because Astro uses HTML streaming in on-demand rendering.

```astro
--- // src/pages/account.astro (page level)
import { isLoggedIn } from '../utils';
if (!isLoggedIn(Astro.cookies.get('session')?.value)) {
  return Astro.redirect('/login');
}
---
```

Astro 6 deprecates the string driver signature; instead build the driver with the `sessionDrivers` export (added in `astro@5.7.0`) and assign it to `session.driver`, with `cookie` and `ttl` as sibling keys of `driver` inside `session`:

```js
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({
      url: process.env.REDIS_URL
    }),
    cookie: {
      secure: true
    },
    ttl: 3600
  }
})
```

The driver options (`url`) move inside the `sessionDrivers.redis()` call rather than living in a separate `options` object; `cookie` and `ttl` remain top-level `session` options.

Set `Astro.response.status` and `Astro.response.statusText` in the page's frontmatter before rendering:

```astro
---
export const prerender = false; // Not needed in 'server' mode
import { getPost } from '../api';
const post = await getPost(Astro.params.id);
if (!post) {
  Astro.response.status = 404;
  Astro.response.statusText = 'Not found';
}
---
```

Alternatively, return a `Response` directly from the page:

```astro
if (!post) {
  return new Response(null, { status: 404, statusText: 'Not found' });
}
```

Both work only on routes rendered on demand, and response headers/status must be set at the page level — not inside components or layouts, since headers are already sent by then.

Create a config helper that returns a `CacheProviderConfig` object with an `entrypoint` pointing to your runtime module and a `config` property containing the serialized options. Then call the helper in `astro.config.mjs` and pass the result to `cache.provider`.

```ts
// my-provider/config.ts
import type { CacheProviderConfig } from 'astro';
interface MyProviderOptions { apiKey: string; region?: string; }
export function myCache(options: MyProviderOptions): CacheProviderConfig {
  return {
    entrypoint: 'my-provider/runtime',
    config: options,
  };
}
```

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { myCache } from 'my-provider/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: { provider: myCache({ apiKey: '...', region: 'us-east-1' }) },
});
```

The runtime module default-exports a `CacheProviderFactory` that receives the serialized `config` and returns a `CacheProvider`.

Set `query.exclude` to an empty array `[]`. This overrides the default tracking-parameter exclusion list and includes every query parameter in the cache key.

```js
memoryCache({
  query: { exclude: [] },
});
```

Note that `exclude` and `include` are mutually exclusive — using both together throws an error.

Structure your package with two parts:

1. **Config helper** — a function users call in `astro.config.mjs` that returns a `CacheProviderConfig` (`{ entrypoint, config }`). This tells Astro where to find the runtime module and what options to pass.

2. **Runtime module** — a file that default-exports a `CacheProviderFactory` function. This module is bundled into the SSR output, so it must be runtime-agnostic (avoid Node.js built-ins unless your target runtime supports them).

```ts
// my-provider/config.ts
import type { CacheProviderConfig } from 'astro';
export function myCache(options): CacheProviderConfig {
  return { entrypoint: 'my-provider/runtime', config: options };
}
```

```ts
// my-provider/runtime.ts
import type { CacheProviderFactory } from 'astro';
const factory: CacheProviderFactory = (config) => {
  return { name: 'my-cache-provider', async invalidate() {} };
};
export default factory;
```

Users then register it via `cache: { provider: myCache({...}) }` in their Astro config.

All of them: the `context` object passed as the first argument to endpoint functions (typed `APIContext`) and to middleware mirrors the properties of the `Astro` global available in `.astro` frontmatter. So `props`, `params`, `request`, and `redirect()` exist in both places — as `Astro.props`, `Astro.params`, `Astro.request`, `Astro.redirect()` in a page's frontmatter, and as destructured arguments in an endpoint:

```ts
import type { APIContext } from 'astro';
export function GET({ props, params, request, redirect }: APIContext) {
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ id: params.id, author: props.author }));
}
```

`props` and `params` can be populated by `getStaticPaths()` on static routes. Runtime distinctions: on prerendered pages `request.url` omits search parameters (they're present on-demand), and `redirect(path, status)` supports status codes only on-demand — prerendered routes emit a `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` tag instead (default `302` on-demand).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/

`getStaticPaths()` is the wrong tool for this route because it exists to generate prerendered routes at build time; in `server` mode it only takes effect if you explicitly opt in with `export const prerender = true`. An on-demand route already resolves `params` per request — any value matching the dynamic pattern — so enumerating entries up front adds nothing, and its `props` are meant for data passed to prerendered templates. Fetch the extra data per request from the param instead:

```astro
---
import { getPost } from '../api';
const post = await getPost(Astro.params.id);
if (!post) return Astro.redirect('/404');
---
<h1>{post.name}</h1>
```

If you actually want these pages built statically, add `export const prerender = true` and keep `getStaticPaths()` (its `params` must be strings, since they're URL-encoded).

Set `query.exclude: []` in the `memoryCache()` options to include all query parameters in the cache key. By default, tracking parameters like `utm_*` are excluded.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({
      query: { exclude: [] }
    })
  }
});
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Tag-based invalidation purges *all* cached entries whose tags include any of the provided tags, while path-based invalidation targets a single URL and is exact-match only (no glob or wildcard support). Choose tags when content spans multiple routes or shares a logical grouping—e.g. tagging every product page `products` lets you purge them all at once after a catalog update. Choose `path` when you need to invalidate one specific URL, such as revalidating `/api/data` alone. You can combine both strategies in one `invalidate()` call depending on what changed:

```ts
await context.cache.invalidate({ tags: ['data'] });
await context.cache.invalidate({ path: '/api/data' });
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

It fails because your runtime module is bundled into your SSR output and must be runtime-agnostic: non-Node runtimes like Cloudflare Workers don't provide Node.js built-in modules, so importing `node:fs` (or `node:path`) will error at runtime. The correct pattern is to keep the runtime module free of filesystem access and instead pass initialization data through the config helper—the `config` object you return from `myCache()` is serialized and delivered to the `CacheProviderFactory` at runtime:

```ts
export function myCache(options: MyProviderOptions): CacheProviderConfig {
  return {
    entrypoint: 'my-provider/runtime',
    config: options, // passed to the factory at runtime
  };
}
```

Load any JSON index at config time (in `astro.config.mjs`) and embed it in `config`, or fetch it remotely inside the factory. On Cloudflare, bindings and env vars come from `wrangler.jsonc` rather than the filesystem.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Set `query.sort` to `false` in the `memoryCache()` options; it defaults to `true`, which alphabetically sorts query parameters so both orderings resolve to one cache entry:

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({ query: { sort: false } }),
  },
});
```

With sorting disabled, `/page?b=2&a=1` and `/page?a=1&b=2` are cached as separate entries. Note that tracking-parameter exclusion still applies by default—use `query.exclude: []` if those parameters must also contribute to the key.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

`max` controls the maximum number of entries kept in the in-memory cache (type `number`, default `1000`). When the cache exceeds this limit, the least recently used entry is evicted—making the provider an LRU cache. For a site with thousands of pages, size `max` to your working set of hot pages; entries beyond the limit aren't rejected, they simply cycle out as newer ones arrive:

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({ max: 500 }),
  },
});
```

Because this provider stores entries in-process, it's suitable only for single-instance deployments.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

Add these scripts to your `package.json`:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "astro dev",
    "build": "astro build",
    "preview": "astro preview"
  }
}
```
Forward flags through npm with `--` separator: `npm run dev -- --port 8080`. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cli-reference/

Serve it from an SSR-enabled app (output: 'server') with a `GET` server endpoint routed to `/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server` that returns `application/json`:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro'
export const GET: APIRoute = () => {
  return new Response(
    JSON.stringify({
      issuer: "https://login.example.com",
      authorization_endpoint: "https://login.example.com/oauth2/authorize",
      token_endpoint: "https://login.example.com/oauth2/token",
      jwks_uri: "https://login.example.com/oauth2/jwks",
      response_types_supported: ["code"],
      grant_types_supported: ["authorization_code", "refresh_token"],
      scopes_supported: ["read", "write"]
    }),
    { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } },
  )
}
```

Publish it over HTTPS only if you actually run an authorisation server; an OIDC provider publishes `openid-configuration` instead (a server that is both may publish both).

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/well-known/oauth-authorization-server/

Pass a `Promise` to `waitUntil()`, destructured from the `context` (first argument of `onRequest()`), so the runtime keeps the response alive while your background work finishes:

```ts
async onRequest({ request, waitUntil }, next) {
  const response = await next();
  waitUntil?.(flushMetrics());
  return response;
}
```

Its type is `(promise: Promise<unknown>) => void`, and it is optional — provided only when the runtime supports it — so guard with `waitUntil?.()` or an `if` before relying on it. Intended uses include revalidating a stale cache entry or flushing metrics after the response is sent, without delaying that response.

Set `session: false` in `astro.config.mjs` to disable sessions entirely. This excludes the session runtime from the SSR bundle, and adapters (Node, Cloudflare, Netlify) skip their default drivers. The smaller bundle reduces cold-start time on serverless/edge runtimes.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({ session: false });
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/sessions/

Set the top-level `cacheDir` option in `astro.config.mjs`. It defaults to `"./node_modules/.astro"`, and accepts either an absolute path or a path relative to the project root:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cacheDir: './.astro-cache',
});
```

Files in this directory are reused by subsequent builds to speed up build time.

Set `build.serverEntry` (default `'entry.mjs'`):

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    serverEntry: 'server.mjs'
  }
});
```

It only names the server entrypoint file emitted when building to SSR — it does not affect client build output. Keep the `.mjs` extension so the runtime detects the file as a JavaScript module. Note that this value is usually set by your adapter for the target host, so an adapter's value may need to be overridden.

`markdown.remarkPlugins` is replaced by `markdown.processor` (added in astro@6.4.0). To use remark-toc, install `@astrojs/markdown-remark` and pass `unified()` as the processor:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { unified } from '@astrojs/markdown-remark';
import remarkToc from 'remark-toc';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      remarkPlugins: [[remarkToc, { heading: "contents" }]],
    }),
  },
});
```

Plugins with options are passed as a nested array: the plugin first, then an options object.

Set `fetchFile: null` when your project already has a `src/fetch.ts` file used for other purposes and you want to disable Astro's advanced routing entrypoint entirely. The default `'fetch'` makes Astro treat `src/fetch.ts` as the advanced routing configuration file (added in v7.0.0). If that file exists for a different purpose, Astro will try to process it as routing config and may cause errors. Alternatively, set `fetchFile` to a different filename (e.g., `'handler'`) to use a custom entrypoint.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({ fetchFile: null });
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Use the `redirects` config with an object specifying `status: 302` and `destination` for the external URL. The default redirect status is 301 for GET and 308 for other methods when using SSR or a static adapter.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  redirects: {
    '/news': { status: 302, destination: 'https://example.com/news' }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

`fallbackType: "redirect"` (default) sends a 301/302 response — the browser navigates to the fallback URL and the address bar updates. `fallbackType: "rewrite"` serves the fallback page's content at the original URL — the address bar stays on the requested path (e.g., `/fr/about/`) while showing `/en/about/` content.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    defaultLocale: "en",
    locales: ["en", "fr"],
    fallback: { fr: "en" },
    routing: { fallbackType: "rewrite" }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Create `public/.well-known/webauthn` with your JSON origins array. Astro serves files from `public/` at the root path during dev and copies them to the build output unchanged. The file must be served over HTTPS as `application/json` with a 200 response.

```json
{
  "origins": [
    "https://example.com",
    "https://example.co.uk",
    "https://example.de"
  ]
}
```

Use the Container API's `locals` option to pass different `checkAuth` implementations per test:

```javascript
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import Card from "../src/components/Card.astro";
import test from "node:test";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();

test("User is authenticated", async () => {
  const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
    locals: {
      checkAuth() { return true; },
    },
  });
  // assert result contains authenticated content
});

test("User is not authenticated", async () => {
  const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
    locals: {
      checkAuth() { return false; },
    },
  });
  // assert result contains unauthenticated content
});
```

Change the `publicDir` option in `astro.config.mjs` to `'./assets'`. Files in this directory are served and copied as-is — **no transformation or bundling** occurs.

```javascript
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  publicDir: './assets'
});
```

Import `getContainerRenderer` from each integration's `container-renderer` entrypoint and `loadRenderers` from the `astro:container` virtual module, then pass the returned array to `experimental_AstroContainer.create({ renderers })`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import { getContainerRenderer as reactContainerRenderer } from "@astrojs/react/container-renderer";
import { getContainerRenderer as svelteContainerRenderer } from "@astrojs/svelte/container-renderer";
import { loadRenderers } from "astro:container";
import Parent from "./Parent.astro";

const renderers = await loadRenderers([
  reactContainerRenderer(),
  svelteContainerRenderer(),
]);
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create({ renderers });
const result = await container.renderToString(Parent);
```

These entrypoints exist for `@astrojs/react`, `@astrojs/preact`, `@astrojs/solid-js`, `@astrojs/svelte`, `@astrojs/vue`, and `@astrojs/mdx`.

Manually import renderers when the container runs at runtime or inside other "shells" (e.g. PHP, Ruby, Java), and for on-demand rendered applications — cases where the `astro:container` virtual module helpers aren't available. Automatic loading only covers static applications or containers not called at runtime (e.g. testing with `vitest`).

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import reactRenderer from "@astrojs/react/server.js";
import vueRenderer from "@astrojs/vue/server.js";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
container.addServerRenderer({ renderer: vueRenderer });
container.addServerRenderer({ renderer: reactRenderer });
container.addClientRenderer({
  name: "@astrojs/react",
  entrypoint: "@astrojs/react/client.js",
});
```

Add all server renderers before client renderers; client renderers are only needed to hydrate `client:*` directives.

Use `loadRenderers()` from the `astro:container` virtual module whenever you're running inside Vite — e.g. Vitest tests or Astro integrations — since the virtual module's helpers are only available there; it resolves `getContainerRenderer()` outputs into the `AddServerRenderer[]` you pass as `renderers` to `experimental_AstroContainer.create()`. Outside Vite — on-demand rendered apps or cases where the container runs at runtime inside another "shell" (PHP, Ruby, Java) — the virtual module isn't available, so you must import server/client renderers manually and register them with `addServerRenderer()`/`addClientRenderer()` instead. For static apps and container calls that never run at runtime (e.g. `vitest`), renderers can be added through the Container API automatically.

```js
import { loadRenderers } from "astro:container";
const renderers = await loadRenderers([reactContainerRenderer(), svelteContainerRenderer()]);
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create({ renderers });
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/container-reference/

Pass `streaming: true` in the options object to `experimental_AstroContainer.create()`; it's a boolean that defaults to `false`, and it enables rendering components using HTML streaming.

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create({ streaming: true });
```

This is distinct from the `@astrojs/react` integration's `experimentalDisableStreaming: true` option, which disables React's default streaming behavior for all React components in your project via `astro.config.mjs` — that setting doesn't affect the Container API's `streaming` option.

No — `renderers` defaults to `[]`, and you only need it when the component renders UI framework components (React, Vue, Svelte, etc.) or MDX via an official Astro integration. A component with plain HTML and templates can be rendered with a bare `experimental_AstroContainer.create()`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(Card);
```

Pass `locals` in the second argument (`ContainerRenderOptions`) of `renderToString()` or `renderToResponse()`; its type is `App.Locals`, and the component reads it via `Astro.locals` as usual.

```js
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
  locals: {
    checkAuth() {
      return true;
    },
  },
});
```

In the component, `const { checkAuth } = Astro.locals;` then returns the injected value, so the same component can be rendered authenticated or unauthenticated by passing different `locals` per call.

Generate a digest from the entry data with `generateDigest()` and pass it as `digest` when calling `store.set()`; `set()` compares it against the stored entry's digest and returns `false` when they match, skipping the update:

```ts
load: async ({ store, parseData, generateDigest }) => {
  for (const item of feed.items) {
    const data = await parseData({ id: item.guid, data: item });
    const digest = generateDigest(data);
    store.set({ id: item.guid, data, digest }); // false if unchanged
  }
}
```

`generateDigest()` (added in `astro@5.0.0`) produces a non-cryptographic content digest of an object or string; the digest must be a string that changes whenever the data changes. The format is up to the loader.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

No — use the built-in `file()` loader pointed at a JSON (or YAML/TOML) file holding your array:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { file } from "astro/loaders";
const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/dogs.json"),
});
export const collections = { dogs };
```

Each object must include a unique `id` key, because `file()` does not auto-generate IDs (unlike `glob()`). Build a custom object loader with `load()` and `store.set()` only when you need custom fetching or transformation logic.

The `types` string must export an `Entry` type or interface — its contents are written verbatim into a generated TypeScript file, and a missing `Entry` export makes that file invalid. For example:

```ts
createSchema: async () => {
  const schema = await getSchema();
  const types = await getTypes();
  return {
    schema,
    types: `export type Entry = ${types}`,
  };
},
```

If type generation itself fails, `astro sync` reports a `GenerateContentTypesError`.

`props` pass data the component reads via `Astro.props`, while `slots` pass HTML content that renders wherever `<slot />` is written. Choose `props` for values (strings, objects, numbers) the component consumes in its script/template, and `slots` for markup or child elements the caller authors. Both are optional keys on `ContainerRenderOptions`:

```js
const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
  props: { name: "Hello, world!" },
  slots: { default: "Some value" }, // named slots: header, footer as keys
});
```

Pass a `request` in the second argument to `renderToString()` or `renderToResponse()`; it supplies the path/URL for `Astro.url` and headers/cookies for `Astro.request`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import Card from "../src/components/Card.astro";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
  request: new Request("https://example.com/blog", {
    headers: { "x-some-secret-header": "test-value" },
  }),
});
```

The string form only sets the cookie name; every other option stays at its default (`sameSite: 'lax'`, `httpOnly: true`, `secure: true`). The object form sets `name` along with any other `AstroCookieSetOptions`, which are merged with those defaults. So if you need to change `sameSite` or `secure`, you must use the object form:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    cookie: {
      name: "my-session-cookie",
      sameSite: "lax",
      secure: true,
    }
  }
});
```

(`session.cookie` was added in `astro@5.7.0`.)

Intercept the path in `src/middleware.js` and return a 302 (or 303) redirect to your real change-password page:

```js
export function onRequest(context, next) {
  if (context.url.pathname === '/.well-known/change-password') {
    return new Response(null, {
      status: 302,
      headers: { Location: '/account/security/password' },
    });
  }
  return next();
}
```

Serve it over HTTPS on the canonical host, point it at the page for changing an *existing* password (not the forgotten-password reset flow, and not sign-up), and make that destination handle signed-out users by sending them to sign in first. Never return 404 for this URL — it tells password managers the feature is unsupported. Middleware for prerendered pages runs at build time, so this route must be rendered on demand for the redirect to be served per request. Only implement this if your site actually has user accounts.

Pass the prop via the `props` option of `renderToString()`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import Card from "../src/components/Card.astro";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
  props: { name: "Hello, world!" },
});
```

The `props` option is typed `Record<string, unknown>` and supplies `Astro.props`, which your component destructures as `const { name } = Astro.props`. Then assert `result` contains "Hello, world!".

Pass the `params` option to `renderToString()`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import LocaleSlug from "../src/pages/[locale]/[slug].astro";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(LocaleSlug, {
  params: {
    locale: "en",
    slug: "getting-started",
  },
});
```

The `params` option (`Record<string, string | undefined>`) is intended for components that generate dynamic routes, and resolves `Astro.params` inside the component during rendering.

Use `renderToResponse()` with `routeType: "endpoint"` and a `Request` object specifying the method. The container calls the matching exported function (GET, POST, PATCH, etc.) from the endpoint module.

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import * as Endpoint from "../src/pages/api/endpoint.js";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const response = await container.renderToResponse(Endpoint, {
  routeType: "endpoint",
  request: new Request("https://example.com", { method: "PATCH" }),
});
const json = await response.json();
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/container-reference/

Use the `astro:config:setup` hook and call `addDevToolbarApp()` inside it with an object containing the required `id`, `name`, and `entrypoint` properties (plus optional `icon`):

```js
/** @type {() => import('astro').AstroIntegration} */
export default () => ({
  name: "my-integration",
  hooks: {
    "astro:config:setup": ({ addDevToolbarApp }) => {
      addDevToolbarApp({
        id: "my-app",
        name: "My App",
        icon: "<svg>...</svg>",
        entrypoint: "./my-app.js",
      });
    },
  },
});
```

Since Astro 5.0.0, `entrypoint` may also be a `URL`, e.g. `new URL("./my-app.js", import.meta.url)`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/dev-toolbar-app-reference/

The `toolbar` object isn't available in `astro:config:setup` — you must register `toolbar.onAppToggled()` in the `astro:server:setup` hook instead:

```js
export default () => ({
  name: "my-integration",
  hooks: {
    "astro:config:setup": ({ addDevToolbarApp }) => {},
    "astro:server:setup": ({ toolbar }) => {
      toolbar.onAppToggled('my-app', ({ state }) => {
        console.log(`The app is now ${state ? 'enabled' : 'disabled'}!`);
      });
    },
  },
});
```

Use `astro:config:setup` only for `addDevToolbarApp()`; all toolbar messaging helpers (`send`, `on`, `onAppInitialized`, `onAppToggled`) live on the `toolbar` object in `astro:server:setup`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/dev-toolbar-app-reference/

No. Vite's built-in `connection` event fires **before** Dev Toolbar apps are initialized, so messages sent in response to it may reach the app before it can receive them. Instead, use `toolbar.onAppInitialized()` in your `astro:server:setup` hook, which guarantees the app is fully initialized before you send:

```js
'astro:server:setup': ({ toolbar }) => {
  toolbar.onAppInitialized('my-app', () => {
    toolbar.send('server-message', { message: 'Hello!' });
  });
},
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/dev-toolbar-app-reference/

Call `parseData()` from the `LoaderContext` passed to `load()` before `store.set()`. It validates and parses the data according to the collection schema, and returns a `Promise<TData>` resolving to the validated, parsed data object you then write to the store:

```ts
load: async ({ store, logger, parseData }) => {
  const feed = loadFeed(feedUrl);
  store.clear();
  for (const item of feed.items) {
    const id = item.guid;
    const data = await parseData({ id, data: item });
    store.set({ id, data });
  }
},
```

It is the loader's responsibility to do this: no validation happens when getting or setting data in the store.

A `: Loader` return annotation checks only the returned value; it does not provide type safety inside your loader object, so `store`, `parseData`, and the schema lose inference. Use the TypeScript `satisfies` operator on the returned object instead — it validates the object against `Loader` while keeping inference inside `load` and preserving type inference when the loader is used in a collection:

```ts
import type { Loader } from "astro/loaders";

export function feedLoader(options: { url: string }) {
  return {
    name: "feed-loader",
    load: async ({ store, parseData }) => {
      const data = await parseData({ id, data: item });
      store.set({ id, data });
    },
  } satisfies Loader;
}
```

Since Astro 6, `satisfies Loader` is also required when you provide a `schema`, because collection types are now inferred rather than generated with `zod-to-ts`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

Use the `glob()` loader from `astro/loaders`, passing a `pattern` of glob matches and a `base` directory:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { glob } from 'astro/loaders';

const pages = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/data/pages" }),
});

export const collections = { pages };
```

The `pattern` must be written relative to the `base` directory (e.g. `"**/*.md"`, not `"src/data/pages/**/*.md"`), since `base` is the directory from which `pattern` is resolved; it defaults to `"."`. Patterns use micromatch glob syntax, and each entry's `id` is auto-generated from its file name.

Use `glob()` for the 300 separate files and `file()` for the single array:

```ts
const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.json", base: "./src/data/authors" }),
});
const products = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/products.json"),
});
```

`glob()` fetches entries from directories of separate Markdown/MDX/Markdoc/JSON/YAML/TOML files and auto-generates a unique `id` from each file name; `file()` fetches multiple entries from a single local file, parsing a JSON/YAML array of objects or a TOML file's top-level tables. They don't swap because their unit of input differs: `file()` reads exactly one file and will not auto-generate IDs, so every object must already carry a unique `id`; `glob()` reads one entry per file and derives IDs from file names, so a lone multi-entry array isn't its shape.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

The `glob()` loader's default `generateId()` produced it: it uses `github-slugger` to create a kebab-cased slug from the file name, so `Jane-Doe.json` becomes `jane-doe`. Keep the original casing by passing a custom `generateId` that strips only the extension:

```ts
const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({
    pattern: "**/*.json",
    base: "./src/data/authors",
    generateId: ({ entry }) => entry.replace(/\.json$/, ""),
  }),
});
```

`entry` is the path relative to `base`. Alternatively, override a single entry's id by adding a `slug` property to that JSON data object (or Markdown frontmatter).

No. Use `app.onToolbarPlacementUpdated()` instead — it fires whenever the user moves the Dev Toolbar and is explicitly intended for repositioning your app's UI:

```js
import { defineToolbarApp } from "astro/toolbar";
export default defineToolbarApp({
  init(canvas, app) {
    app.onToolbarPlacementUpdated((options) => {
      console.log(`The toolbar is now placed at ${options.placement}!`);
    });
  },
});
```

The callback receives `{placement: 'bottom-left' | 'bottom-center' | 'bottom-right'}` (added in `astro@4.7.0`). `toggleState()` only enables/disables the app; it does not trigger repositioning.

The `NoPrerenderedRoutesWithDomains` error occurs because static prerendered pages aren't supported when using i18n with custom domains. To fix this, disable prerendering by setting `output: "server"` in your Astro config and ensure no pages are prerendered. The configuration requires an adapter (like `@astrojs/node`) and a `site` URL.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import node from "@astrojs/node";

export default defineConfig({
  site: "https://example.com",
  output: "server",
  adapter: node({ mode: "standalone" }),
  i18n: {
    locales: ["en", "fr"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
    domains: { fr: "https://fr.example.com" }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/no-prerendered-routes-with-domains/

`ActionCalledFromServerError` is thrown when you invoke an action directly from a server-rendered page or endpoint without wrapping it in `Astro.callAction()`—Astro requires this wrapper in server code so the call returns the standard `{ data, error }` result shape instead of behaving like a client invocation. Use `context.callAction()` inside a server endpoint.

```
---
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
const searchQuery = Astro.url.searchParams.get('search');
if (searchQuery) {
  const { data, error } = await Astro.callAction(actions.findProduct, { query: searchQuery });
  // handle result
}
---
```

The first argument is the action, the second its input; you get the same `data`/`error` objects as on the client. Alternatively, trigger actions via an HTML form (`action={actions.yourAction}`) and read results with `Astro.getActionResult()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Astro injects heading IDs **after** your custom plugins run, so any `id` your plugin sets is preserved and overrides Astro's default — no conflict. If a later plugin needs to read Astro's generated IDs, import Astro's own heading-ID plugin and place it before those plugins:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { unified, rehypeHeadingIds } from '@astrojs/markdown-remark';
import { otherPluginThatReliesOnHeadingIDs } from 'some/plugin/source';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      rehypePlugins: [rehypeHeadingIds, otherPluginThatReliesOnHeadingIDs],
    }),
  },
});
```

For the Sätteri processor, use `satteriHeadingIdsPlugin` from `@astrojs/markdown-satteri` the same way.

Export every action from the `server` object in `src/actions/index.ts` — that file is the single registry the server resolves names against, so importing actions anywhere else or renaming without updating `actions/index` and the calling code produces `ActionNotFoundError`. If you colocate definitions in separate files (e.g. `src/actions/user.ts`), re-export them through `server`:

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { user } from './user';
export const server = {
  myAction: defineAction({ /* ... */ }),
  user,
}
```

Call them via `import { actions } from 'astro:actions'`, then `actions.myAction` / `actions.user.getUser()`. Note also that actions require a server output (an adapter and on-demand rendered routes), otherwise they can't resolve at all.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/action-not-found-error/

All actions must be exported from the `server` object in `src/actions/index.ts`; that file is what the framework resolves action names against, so organization affects resolution directly. You may define actions inline, or move them to separate files and import them — including grouping into nested objects:

```ts
// src/actions/user.ts
export const user = {
  getUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
  createUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
}

// src/actions/index.ts
import { user } from './user';
export const server = {
  myAction: defineAction({ /* ... */ }),
  user,
}
```

They're then callable as `actions.user.getUser()` and `actions.user.createUser()`. If an action isn't re-exported through `server` (or the name doesn't match after a rename), you get `ActionNotFoundError`. Actions also require server output: add an adapter and enable on-demand rendering, or you'll hit `ActionsWithoutServerOutputError`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Set `output: 'server'` in `astro.config.mjs` to enable SSR by default. Then ensure your adapter uses its server entrypoint (e.g., `@astrojs/vercel/serverless`) rather than a static-only entrypoint.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  output: 'server'
});
```

`Astro.glob()` was removed in Astro 6.0. Replace it with `import.meta.glob()` (Vite's native glob import). Note that `import.meta.glob()` no longer returns a Promise — use `{ eager: true }` to load modules synchronously. For content collections, prefer `getCollection()`.

```js
// Before (Astro 5)
const posts = await Astro.glob('./posts/*.md');

// After (Astro 6+)
const posts = Object.values(import.meta.glob('./posts/*.md', { eager: true }));
```

Because the `AstroGlobUsedOutside` error was removed in Astro v6.0.0 along with the removal of `Astro.glob()` itself. With the API gone, Astro no longer detects and reports the misuse, so a plain `.ts` utility file just hits an undefined global and fails with a runtime `ReferenceError`. Replace it with Vite's equivalent:

```ts
const matches = import.meta.glob('./posts/*.md', { eager: true });
const posts = Object.values(matches);
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/astro-glob-used-outside/

Add a `cache.provider` configuration in `astro.config.mjs`. The cache feature must be enabled by providing a cache provider, such as the built-in `memoryCache()`:

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
});
```

The `file` loader accepts only a single local file, not a glob pattern — `file('./src/data/**/*.json')` is invalid. Use the `glob` loader instead, passing a `pattern` relative to a `base` directory:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { glob } from "astro/loaders";

const data = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.json", base: "./src/data" }),
});
export const collections = { data };
```

Note `glob()` takes an options object (`pattern`, optional `base`, `generateId`, `retainBody`, `deferRender`), while `file()` takes a path string and reads one JSON/YAML/TOML/CSV file, requiring an `id` on each entry.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

To render `<h2>Hi, Partner!</h2>` in `GreetingHeadline.astro`, declare the props using a TypeScript `Props` interface with optional `greeting` and required `name` fields, then destructure them from `Astro.props` with a default value for `greeting`.

src/components/GreetingHeadline.astro

```
---interface Props {
  name: string;
  greeting?: string;
}
const { greeting = "Hello", name } = Astro.props;
---
<h2>{greeting}, {name}!</h2>
```

To safely display error information in `src/pages/500.astro`, evaluate the `error` prop before rendering to avoid exposing sensitive data like stack traces. Use a type guard or conditional logic to inspect the error and return appropriate content.

Example:

```astro
---interface Props {
  error: unknown;
}
const { error } = Astro.props;

function isError(e: unknown): e is Error {
  return e instanceof Error;
}
---

<div>
  {isError(error) 
    ? <p>Error: {error.message}</p> 
    : <p>An unknown error occurred.</p>}
</div>
```

