You should place Astro integrations and plugins in `dependencies` rather than `devDependencies`, as Astro requires them at build time and potentially runtime.

Astro needs all dependencies— including integrations and plugins—at build time. While some tools may only need devDependencies during development, Astro's integration system requires these packages to be present when building your site or running the dev server.

If you're publishing an integration or component as a package, use `dependencies` for its required peer dependencies and `devDependencies` for tooling like testing frameworks or build utilities. For example:

```json
{
  "name": "my-integration",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "dependencies": {
    "astro": "^4.0.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/node": "^20.0.0"
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/basics/project-structure/

The `scripts` field in your project's `package.json` file defines commands like `build` and `dev`.

To fix the error, ensure that `package.json` includes a `build` script. Astro projects typically use `astro build` for building.

Example:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "astro build"
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/basics/project-structure/

No, you do not need to consolidate your existing React and Vue components onto a single UI framework when migrating to Astro.

Astro supports multiple JSX frameworks simultaneously through its integration configuration. You can specify which files belong to which framework using the `include` option in each framework’s integration settings.

For example, if you have React components in `/components/react/` and Vue components in `/components/vue/`, you would configure your `astro.config.mjs` like this:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import react from '@astrojs/react';
import vue from '@astrojs/vue';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    react({ include: ['**/react/*'] }),
    vue({ include: ['**/vue/*'] }),
  ],
});
```

This allows you to keep your existing React and Vue components separate while using them within the same Astro project.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/solid-js/#combining-multiple-jsx-frameworks

To add a Vue component to an .astro page in an Astro project that already has `@astrojs/react` installed and configured, you must install and add the `@astrojs/vue` integration package to the integrations array in `astro.config.mjs`.

Install the package using your preferred package manager:

```bash
npm install @astrojs/vue
```

Then, import and add it to your `astro.config.mjs` file:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vue from '@astrojs/vue';

export default defineConfig({
  // ...
  integrations: [vue()],
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations/

Use **Content Collections** to validate and type-safety your Markdown frontmatter in Astro.

Content Collections let you define a schema for your Markdown files' frontmatter using TypeScript or JavaScript. This ensures that the data in your frontmatter matches the expected structure, providing compile-time errors if anything is missing or incorrect. It also enables auto-completion when editing your content and makes it easier to manage large numbers of posts with consistent formatting.

Example usage:
```ts
// src/content/blog.ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';

const blog = defineCollection({
  schema: ({ image }) => ({
    title: String,
    pubDate: Date,
    description: String,
    image: image(),
    tags: [String],
  }),
});

export const collections = {
  blog,
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

For `astro build` to succeed, only `src/pages/` (which contains at least one page file) is strictly required. The `package.json` is also necessary to define dependencies and scripts, but the other files (`astro.config.mjs`, `tsconfig.json`) are not strictly required for building.

The minimal project structure includes:
- `src/pages/` — contains your pages.
- `package.json` — defines dependencies and build scripts.

Recommended files that improve development experience include:
- `astro.config.mjs` — configures Astro behavior.
- `tsconfig.json` — enables TypeScript support in editors.

Example minimal `package.json`:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "astro build"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "astro": "^7.0.0"
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/basics/project-structure/

Your Prettier configuration likely needs the `tailwindcss` plugin to be explicitly enabled for `.astro` files, as `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` only applies sorting within CSS-in-JS or standalone CSS files by default.

To fix this, ensure your `.prettierrc` includes a file-specific override that enables Tailwind class sorting in `.astro` files:

```json
{
  "plugins": ["prettier-plugin-astro", "prettier-plugin-tailwindcss"],
  "overrides": [
    {
      "files": "*.astro",
      "options": {
        "parser": "astro"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

This tells Prettier to parse `.astro` files with the `astro` parser and apply Tailwind sorting rules. If you're using a different config file like `prettier.config.js`, make sure it exports an object that includes both plugins and overrides.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/styling/#tailwind

In Astro 7, Biome's experimental `.astro` support does not fully lint all aspects of `.astro` files. It primarily handles JavaScript/TypeScript code within `<script>` tags and some basic formatting. For full coverage, including Astro-specific features like component validation, JSX syntax in `.astro` files, and integration with Astro’s runtime APIs, you need to use additional tools.

Use `eslint-plugin-astro` for comprehensive linting of `.astro` files, including Astro-specific rules. You can configure it alongside Biome by setting up an ESLint configuration that targets `.astro` files specifically.

Example setup in your config:

```js
// eslint.config.js
import astro from 'eslint-plugin-astro';

export default [
  {
    files: ['**/*.astro'],
    plugins: { astro },
    rules: {
      'astro/no-unused-vars': 'error',
      'astro/valid-compile': 'error'
    }
  }
];
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v5/

When defining an Astro action with `accept: 'form'`, unchecked checkboxes are converted to `false` (not `null`) during validation, while empty text inputs become `null`. This distinction arises because Astro applies special handling for form field types:

- For checkbox inputs, Astro uses `z.coerce.boolean()` which converts the absence of a value to `false`.
- For other input types like text, an empty submission results in `null`.

This behavior ensures that boolean values are explicitly handled while preserving the semantic meaning of missing data for string fields.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  example: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      checkbox: z.coerce.boolean(),
      text: z.string().nullable(),
    }),
    handler: async (input) => {
      // unchecked checkbox → false
      // empty text field → null
    },
  }),
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Use the `isInputError()` utility function from `'astro:actions'` to check if the error is an input validation error. If it is, access the `fields` object on the error to retrieve field-specific messages.

```ts
import { actions, isInputError } from 'astro:actions';
const result = Astro.getActionResult(actions.newsletter);
const inputErrors = isInputError(result?.error) ? result.error.fields : {};
```

The `inputErrors` object will contain error messages for each invalid field, e.g., `{ email: ['Invalid email format'] }`.

When an action throws `new ActionError({ code: 'UNAUTHORIZED', message: 'User must be logged in.' })`, the HTTP status code set on the response is **401 Unauthorized**. The action result object contains an `error` property with the `ActionError` instance, including its `code` and `message`. On the server, when calling actions via `Astro.callAction()`, the error can be accessed through the returned object's `error` field.

Frontend code using `actions.myAction()` (e.g., in a `.tsx` component) receives the same result structure. The error message is accessible as `error.message`.

Example:

```ts
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';

const { data, error } = await actions.likePost({ postId });
if (error?.code === 'UNAUTHORIZED') {
  console.log(error.message); // "User must be logged in."
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/#actionerror

If `error.code` is undefined after calling `actions.likePost({ postId })`, it implies that the action threw an unexpected error — likely a non-`ActionError` — which doesn't conform to the expected structure. This can happen if the server-side handler throws a generic JavaScript error or an error not created with `ActionError`.

To safely handle such errors, check for the presence of the `error` object and use a fallback or generic message instead of relying on `code`. For example:

```tsx
if (error) {
  console.error('Unexpected error:', error);
  // Handle as a general failure
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/#handling-backend-errors-in-your-action

To use `actions.logout()` and Astro’s client-side `navigate()` function in `src/components/LogoutButton.tsx` to redirect to `/` only when the action succeeds, call the action inside a click handler, check for errors in the response, and invoke `navigate('/')` if no error occurs.

Example:

```tsx
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
import { navigate } from 'astro:transitions/client';

export function LogoutButton() {
  return (
    <button onClick={async () => {
      const { error } = await actions.logout();
      if (!error) navigate('/');
    }}>
      Logout
    </button>
  );
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/#handling-client-redirects

When calling actions from a server endpoint, use `context.callAction()` instead of `Astro.callAction()`.

It accepts two arguments:
1. The action function (e.g., `actions.logout`)
2. Input parameters for the action (e.g., `{ userId: '123' }`)

Example:
```ts
// src/pages/api/webhook.ts
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';

export async function POST(context) {
  const result = await context.callAction(actions.processWebhook, { payload: context.request.json() });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(result));
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/#callaction

To call the `getGreeting` action from an Astro component script, import `actions` from `'astro:actions'`. The two possible properties on the object returned by the action call are `data` and `error`.

Example:
```astro
<script>
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';

async () => {
  const result = await actions.getGreeting({ name: 'Astro' });
  console.log(result.data); // "Hello, Astro!"
  console.log(result.error); // undefined
}
</script>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/

You must not assume the request is authorized when the action is called from a public endpoint, because actions are publicly accessible by default. To enforce proper authorization, you must add an authentication check in the action handler, such as verifying a session cookie or user context, and throw an `ActionError` with code `"UNAUTHORIZED"` if the user is not authenticated.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from 'astro:actions';
export const server = {
  blog: {
    like: defineAction({
      handler: async (_input, context) => {
        if (!context.locals.user) {
          throw new ActionError({ code: 'UNAUTHORIZED' });
        }
        // Proceed with action logic
      }
    })
  }
};
```

When an action call returns an `error`, it can originate from either the server-side handler or input validation.

If the error comes from the handler function (server-side logic), it means the action encountered an issue during execution, such as a runtime exception or failed database query. This type of error is thrown directly within the `handler()` function defined in `defineAction()`.

The second origin is input validation errors, which occur when the data passed to the action doesn't match the expected schema defined via the `input` property using Zod. These are automatically caught and formatted by Astro's action system before being returned as an error object.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  myAction: defineAction({
    input: z.object({ name: z.string() }),
    handler: async (input) => {
      if (input.name === "error") throw new Error("Server error");
      return `Hello, ${input.name}!`;
    }
  })
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Throwing `new ActionError({ code: 'NOT_FOUND', message: '...' })` provides explicit error status codes (like 404) and structured error handling, improving debugging and user feedback compared to returning `undefined` or `null`.

This allows your frontend to check `error.code === 'NOT_FOUND'` and respond appropriately, such as displaying a targeted message or redirecting the user. It also ensures consistent error responses across actions.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from "astro:actions";
export const server = {
  getUser: defineAction({
    handler: async (_, ctx) => {
      const user = await db.findUser(ctx.params.id);
      if (!user) {
        throw new ActionError({
          code: 'NOT_FOUND',
          message: 'User not found'
        });
      }
      return user;
    },
  }),
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/#actionerror

To redirect the browser to `/products/${id}` after a successful form submission in `src/pages/products/create.astro`, you need to handle the action result in middleware and use the POST/Redirect/GET pattern.

Create or update your middleware file (`src/middleware.ts`) with logic to persist action results using Netlify Blobs, then redirect on success:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';
import { getActionContext } from 'astro:actions';
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";
import { getStore } from "@netlify/blobs";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  if (context.isPrerendered) return next();

  const { action, setActionResult, serializeActionResult } = getActionContext(context);
  const actionStore = getStore("action-session");

  // Retrieve persisted result from session
  const sessionId = context.cookies.get("action-session-id")?.value;
  const session = sessionId ? await actionStore.get(sessionId, { type: "json" }) : undefined;

  if (session) {
    setActionResult(session.actionName, session.actionResult);
    await actionStore.delete(sessionId);
    context.cookies.delete("action-session-id");
    return next();
  }

  // Handle form submission
  if (action?.calledFrom === "form") {
    const actionResult = await action.handler();

    const sessionId = randomUUID();
    await actionStore.setJSON(sessionId, {
      actionName: action.name,
      actionResult: serializeActionResult(actionResult),
    });

    context.cookies.set("action-session-id", sessionId);

    if (actionResult.error) {
      const referer = context.request.headers.get("Referer");
      if (!referer) throw new Error("Internal: Referer unexpectedly missing.");
      return context.redirect(referer);
    }

    // Redirect to the created product page
    const id = actionResult.data?.id;
    if (id) {
      return context.redirect(`/products/${id}`);
    }
    return context.redirect(context.originPathname);
  }

  return next();
});
```

Then, in `src/pages/products/create.astro`, use a form with an action attribute pointing to your action:

```astro
---
import { ActionForm } from 'astro:actions';

const result = Astro.getActionResult();
---

<form method="POST" action="/api/actions/createProduct">
  <ActionForm />
</form>
```

Ensure `createProduct` is defined in `src/actions/index.ts` with `accept: 'form'`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

In Astro 7, you can use the `Astro.session` API in middleware to check for a valid session before allowing client-side RPC actions to proceed. If no session cookie is present, you should return a 403 Forbidden response.

Example:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  // Skip middleware for prerendered pages
  if (context.isPrerendered) return next();

  // Check session validity
  const session = await context.session.get();
  if (!session) {
    return new Response(null, { status: 403 });
  }

  return next();
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

No, you cannot delete the per-action `ActionError` checks, because middleware runs after the action is invoked but before the response is sent. If an RPC call arrives without a session cookie, it will be rejected by the middleware and return 403, preventing execution of the handler entirely.

However, if the same action were called from an HTML form (which bypasses middleware), then the per-action check would still be necessary to ensure authorization — especially since actions can be invoked via both RPC and form submissions. The middleware handles server-side RPC calls, but not all invocation methods are protected by it.

Example:
```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
export const server = {
  likePost: defineAction({
    input: z.object({ postId: z.string() }),
    handler: async (input, ctx) => {
      if (!ctx.cookies.has('user-session')) {
        throw new ActionError({
          code: "UNAUTHORIZED",
          message: "User must be logged in.",
        });
      }
      // Otherwise, like the post
    },
  }),
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

The `server` object must be exported from `src/actions/index.ts`. Use `defineAction()` from `astro:actions` to declare the action’s input schema and handler.

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  getGreeting: defineAction({
    input: z.object({ name: z.string() }),
    handler: async (input) => `Hello, ${input.name}!`,
  }),
};
```

For your `/contact` page, use **Astro Actions** instead of API endpoints because they’re designed for server-side logic triggered by form submissions or direct calls from components, offering built-in features like input validation, type safety, and standardized error handling.

Actions handle:
- Input parsing and validation via Zod schemas.
- Type-safe client-side calling with `Astro.callAction()` or HTML forms.
- Standardized errors (`isInputError()` utility).
- Automatic redirect support on success.
- Zero-JS form submission fallbacks when prerendering is disabled.

Example action definition:

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  contact: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      name: z.string().min(1),
      email: z.string().email(),
      message: z.string().min(10)
    }),
    handler: async (input) => {
      // Send email or save to DB
      return { success: true };
    }
  })
};
```

Use it in your component like this:

```astro
---import { actions } from 'astro:actions';---
<form method="POST" action={actions.contact}>
  <input name="name" required />
  <input type="email" name="email" required />
  <textarea name="message" required></textarea>
  <button>Send</button>
</form>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

The URL path for the `blog.like` action can be retrieved using the `getActionPath()` utility from `astro:actions`. This function returns a string representing the route where the action is exposed on the server, allowing you to call it directly via `fetch()` or other HTTP clients.

Example usage:
```ts
import { actions, getActionPath } from 'astro:actions';

const path = getActionPath(actions.blog.like);
// e.g. "/_actions/blog/like"
```

The path exists because Astro exposes all defined actions at specific routes under `/_actions/` to enable server-side execution through HTTP requests, which is necessary for client-side invocation or integration with tools like `fetch()` and `sendBeacon()`. It ensures that actions are accessible only on the server side when using server output mode.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/#getactionpath

To handle both "create" and "update" submissions in one action using a `type` field to differentiate, use `z.discriminatedUnion()` in your `input` schema. This allows you to define separate validation schemas for each branch based on the value of the `type` field.

In your handler, Astro narrows the input type automatically based on the discriminator field (`type`). If `type` is `'create'`, the input will be typed as `{ type: 'create', name: string, email: string }`. If `type` is `'update'`, it will be typed as `{ type: 'update', id: number, name: string, email: string }`.

Example:

```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  changeUser: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.discriminatedUnion('type', [
      z.object({
        type: z.literal('create'),
        name: z.string(),
        email: z.email(),
      }),
      z.object({
        type: z.literal('update'),
        id: z.number(),
        name: z.string(),
        email: z.email(),
      }),
    ]),
    async handler(input) {
      if (input.type === 'create') {
        // input is narrowed to { type: 'create', name: string, email: string }
      } else {
        // input is narrowed to { type: 'update', id: number, name: string, email: string }
      }
    },
  }),
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Yes, all Zod extension functions including `.refine()`, `.transform()`, and `.pipe()` are supported on the `z.object()` validator when using `accept: 'form'` in an action. You can also use `z.coerce.date()` to validate date fields from form inputs.

Example:
```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  comment: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      name: z.string(),
      email: z.email(),
      date: z.coerce.date(), // Coerces string to Date
    }).refine(data => data.name !== data.email, { // Cross-field validation
      message: "Name and email must be different",
    }),
    handler: async (input) => {
      // ...
    },
  })
};
```

To retain user input after a failed form submission, you must manually populate the form fields with the previously entered data. Astro's actions do not automatically repopulate forms on validation errors.

You can access the `Astro.getActionResult()` within your component to retrieve the action result and extract the original input values. Then, use JavaScript or your UI framework to set those values back into the form fields before re-rendering.

Example using vanilla JS:
```javascript
const result = Astro.getActionResult(actions.newsletter);
if (result?.data?.email) {
  document.querySelector('input[name="email"]').value = result.data.email;
}
```

