The issue occurs because your `signin.ts` endpoint doesn't handle OAuth provider redirection when a `provider` field is present in the form data. When the "Sign in with GitHub" button is clicked, it sends a POST request with `provider=github`, but your current implementation only checks for email and password fields and assumes all requests are for email/password authentication.

To fix this, modify your `/src/pages/api/auth/signin.ts` to check if a provider is specified and redirect accordingly. For example:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { supabase } from "../../../lib/supabase";

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request, cookies, redirect }) => {
  const formData = await request.formData();
  const email = formData.get("email")?.toString();
  const password = formData.get("password")?.toString();
  const provider = formData.get("provider")?.toString();

  if (provider) {
    // Redirect to OAuth provider
    return redirect(supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth({ provider }).url);
  }

  if (!email || !password) {
    return new Response("Email and password are required", { status: 400 });
  }

  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithPassword({
    email,
    password,
  });

  if (error) {
    return new Response(error.message, { status: 500 });
  }

  const { access_token, refresh_token } = data.session;
  cookies.set("sb-access-token", access_token, {
    path: "/",
  });
  cookies.set("sb-refresh-token", refresh_token, {
    path: "/",
  });

  return redirect("/dashboard");
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/supabase/

In Astro, when using Turso (or any database) inside a dynamic route page like `src/pages/posts/[slug].astro`, you can access the `slug` parameter via `Astro.params.slug` and pass it directly into your SQL query.

Here's how to do it:

```ts
// src/pages/posts/[slug].astro
import { db } from '../../db/turso'; // Adjust path as needed

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await db.prepare('SELECT slug FROM posts').all();
  return posts.map((post) => ({
    params: { slug: post.slug }
  }));
}

export async function GET({ params }) {
  const { slug } = params;
  const post = await db.prepare('SELECT * FROM posts WHERE slug = ?').get(slug);

  if (!post) {
    return new Response('Post not found', { status: 404 });
  }

  return new Response(JSON.stringify(post), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  });
}
```

This example uses Turso's `db.prepare()` with a parameterized query to safely fetch data based on the dynamic route slug. The `slug` is extracted from `Astro.params` and passed as a parameter to prevent SQL injection.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

