Astro processes scripts by default, bundling them and potentially inlining or deferring their execution, which can cause timing issues when a script tries to access DOM elements rendered by client-side framework components like React. When using `client:load`, the React component hydrates asynchronously after the initial page load, so accessing its markup immediately from a processed `<script>` may fail because the DOM nodes haven't been rendered yet.

Astro recommends using the `is:inline` directive for scripts that must interact with UI framework components. This prevents Astro from processing or deferring the script execution, ensuring it runs at the right time and can reliably query the DOM elements created by client-side components.

Example:
```astro
<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const reactElement = document.querySelector('.react-component');
    // Safe to interact with React-rendered markup
  });
</script>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/client-side-scripts/

The issue is in the `AstroHeart` class's use of `document.querySelector()` instead of `this.querySelector()`. Since `querySelector()` searches globally, all instances share the same `<span>` element and count variable.

To fix this, replace `this.querySelector("span")` with `this.querySelector("span")` inside each custom element instance. This ensures each component manages its own state independently.

Example fix:
```js
class AstroHeart extends HTMLElement {
  connectedCallback() {
    let count = 0;
    const heartButton = this.querySelector("button");
    const countSpan = this.querySelector("span");

    if (heartButton && countSpan) {
      heartButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
        count++;
        countSpan.textContent = count.toString();
      });
    }
  }
}
customElements.define("astro-heart", AstroHeart);
```

To create an `<astro-heart>` custom element with a button and span that maintains独立 state per instance, define a class extending `HTMLElement` with `connectedCallback()` to initialize the counter and attach event listeners. Register it using `customElements.define()`. The script must be placed in the component template's `<script>` tag.

```astro
---
const { initialCount = 0 } = Astro.props;
---

<astro-heart data-initial-count={initialCount}>
  <button>❤️</button>
  <span>0</span>
</astro-heart>

<script>
  class AstroHeart extends HTMLElement {
    connectedCallback() {
      const button = this.querySelector('button');
      const span = this.querySelector('span');
      let count = Number(this.dataset.initialCount);

      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        count++;
        span.textContent = count;
      });
    }
  }

  customElements.define('astro-heart', AstroHeart);
</script>
```

This component will render five independent counters when used five times on a page, each maintaining its own state. The `data-initial-count` attribute passes the initial value from the component script to the browser.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/client-side-scripts/

No, wrapping the logic in a custom element with `connectedCallback()` is not the right choice for registering a page-wide keyboard shortcut listener in an Astro component.

Astro components render to HTML at build-time or on-demand and do not include client-side runtime. They are server-rendered and do not execute JavaScript in the browser unless explicitly instructed via `<script>` tags or client directives like `client:load`. Using custom elements with lifecycle callbacks such as `connectedCallback()` would require a framework component or explicit hydration, which goes against Astro's design of zero-client-side JavaScript by default.

To register a global keyboard shortcut listener, add a standard HTML `<script>` tag in the component template and use the browser’s DOM API directly:

```astro
<script>
  document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'k' && e.ctrlKey) {
      // Handle keypress
    }
  });
</script>
```

This approach ensures the script runs in the browser without needing a client-side framework or hydration, aligning with Astro’s architecture.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/client-side-scripts/

Astro treats the `<script>` block of `.astro` components as TypeScript automatically, enabling editor features like autocompletion, type hints, and error detection even when writing JavaScript. The `tsconfig.json` file defines how TypeScript interprets your project, including which files to include/exclude and strictness settings. While the dev server doesn't perform type checking, you can run `astro check` or `tsc` to validate types in `.ts`, `.tsx`, and `.astro` files.

Example `tsconfig.json`:
```json
{
  "extends": "astro/tsconfigs/base",
  "include": [".astro/types.d.ts", "**/*"],
  "exclude": ["dist"]
}
```

To catch type errors during build, update your `package.json` scripts:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "astro check && astro build"
  }
}
```

