You can safely leave `base` and `trailingSlash` unset in `astro.config.mjs` if your site deploys to the domain root and your hosting handles trailing slashes consistently.

When `base` is not set, Astro defaults to `/`, and when `trailingSlash` is not set, it defaults to `'ignore'`. This configuration works well for sites deployed at the root of a domain where trailing slash handling is managed by the host.

The only required option is `site`, which should be set to your full deployed URL (e.g., `https://example.com`) to ensure correct canonical URLs and sitemap generation. The RSS feed will automatically align with this setup if you use `trailingSlash: false` in the rss() helper when using `trailingSlash: "never"` globally.

Example minimal config:
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  site: 'https://example.com',
});
```

