Astro supports `.mjs`, `.js`, and `.ts` extensions for its configuration files. If you rename `astro.config.mjs` to `astro.config.js` and switch to CommonJS syntax with `require()`, it will still work, but only if your project is set up to handle CommonJS modules.

Astro recommends using `.mjs` or `.ts` for most projects due to better compatibility with ES Modules and TypeScript support. Using `.js` requires careful handling of module resolution in your environment.

Example configuration in `astro.config.js`:
```javascript
const { defineConfig } = require('astro/config');

module.exports = defineConfig({
  // config options
});
```

