To apply the cache hint from `getLiveCollection()` while capping freshness at 10 minutes, use `Astro.cache.set(cacheHint)` followed by `Astro.cache.set({ maxAge: 600 })` in your `.astro` page’s frontmatter.

```astro
---
import { getLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const { entries, error, cacheHint } = await getLiveCollection('products');
if (error) {
  return new Response('Error loading products', { status: 500 });
}
if (cacheHint) {
  Astro.cache.set(cacheHint);
}
Astro.cache.set({ maxAge: 600 });
---
<ul>
  {entries.map((p) => <li>{p.data.name}</li>)}
</ul>
```

This merges the cache hints from the collection and individual entries, then sets a maximum age of 600 seconds (10 minutes).

In Astro 7, defining a Zod schema for a live collection is optional even if your `loader` returns typed data. However, it's still beneficial to define one for validation, transformation, and consistent typing.

When both the loader’s types and a Zod schema are present, **the Zod schema takes precedence** when querying the collection. This means that any transformations or validations defined in the schema will be applied, overriding the loader’s raw data structure.

Example:
```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: apiLoader({ endpoint: process.env.API_URL }),
  schema: z.object({
    id: z.string(),
    name: z.string(),
    price: z.number().transform(n => `$${n.toFixed(2)}`),
  })
});
```

Here, the `price` field is transformed into a formatted string regardless of how it's returned by the loader.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/#definelivecollection

To filter a content collection query to only include entries from a specific subdirectory like `en/`, use the `id` property in the filter callback of `getCollection()`. Since the `id` includes the full nested path, you can check if it starts with the desired directory prefix.

Example:
```astro
---import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const englishDocsEntries = await getCollection('docs', ({ id }) => {
  return id.startsWith('en/');
});
---
```

This filters entries so only those in the `en/` subdirectory are returned. The filter function receives each entry’s `id`, which contains its full path relative to the collection's root.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#filtering-collection-queries

You can define custom live loaders in your Astro project by creating loader functions in `src/` that return a `LiveLoader` object with `name`, `loadCollection()`, and `loadEntry()` methods. Use `getLiveCollection()` or `getLiveEntry()` in your `.astro` pages to fetch data, passing the loader name and optional filters.

For local Markdown docs, you can use Astro's built-in content collections (`src/content/`) with `getCollection()` instead of live loaders. For CMS data, create a custom loader like `src/product-loader.ts`.

Example product loader:
```ts
// src/product-loader.ts
import type { LiveLoader } from 'astro/loaders';
interface Product {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
}
export function productLoader(config: { apiKey: string }): LiveLoader<Product, { id: string }, { category?: string }> {
  return {
    name: 'product-loader',
    loadCollection: async ({ filter }) => {
      // Fetch products from CMS
      const products = await fetchProducts({ apiKey: config.apiKey, ...filter });
      return {
        entries: products.map((p) => ({
          id: p.id,
          data: p,
        })),
      };
    },
    loadEntry: async ({ filter }) => {
      const product = await fetchProductById({ apiKey: config.apiKey, id: filter.id });
      if (!product) return { error: new Error('Product not found') };
      return {
        id: product.id,
        data: product,
      };
    },
  };
}
```

Then in your page:
```astro
---
import { getLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const products = await getLiveCollection('product-loader', { category: 'electronics' });
---
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

Using `getCollection()` with a filter callback filters entries at build time, before they are returned, while filtering the returned array afterward happens in JavaScript runtime code. The former is more efficient because it reduces the number of entries processed during the build, whereas the latter loads all entries and then discards some in memory.

In Astro 7, `getCollection()` with a filter callback runs during the static site generation phase and does not include filtered-out entries in the final output. Filtering after querying requires loading all collection data into memory first, which can increase build time for large collections.

Example:
```astro
// Filter at query-time (preferred)
const publishedPosts = await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => {
  return data.draft !== true;
});

// Filter post-query (less efficient)
const allPosts = await getCollection('blog');
const publishedPosts = allPosts.filter(post => post.data.draft !== true);
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#filtering-collection-queries

To define a collection schema where `updatedDate` is optional and `pubDate` is required, use `z.optional()` for the optional field and ensure the required field has no `.optional()` wrapper. Use `z.coerce.date()` to coerce strings in frontmatter into Date objects.

Example:
```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { z } from "astro/zod";

const blog = defineCollection({
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    description: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(), // required, coerces string to Date
    updatedDate: z.coerce.date().optional(), // optional field
  }),
});
```

