When using the `glob()` loader with Markdown files in Astro, custom heading IDs are configured via a [Markdown processor plugin](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/markdown-content/#markdown-processor-plugins) in your `astro.config.mjs`. The default behavior uses `github-slugger` to generate IDs from heading content.

To override these IDs, use a rehype plugin like `rehype-slug` or create a custom one that sets the `id` property on heading elements. This configuration is applied globally for all Markdown files processed by Astro's Markdown pipeline:

astro.config.mjs

```
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { rehypeSlug } from 'rehype-slug';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    rehypePlugins: [
      rehypeSlug,
    ],
  },
});
```

The custom plugin must be placed before any other plugins that rely on heading IDs. If you want to preserve existing anchor links, you can create a custom plugin that strips trailing hyphens from generated IDs (as shown in the upgrade guide), or ensure your Markdown headings include explicit `id` attributes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/markdown-content/#heading-ids

To preserve uppercase letters in entry IDs when using the `glob()` loader, pass a custom `generateId` function to the loader options. This function receives an object with `entry`, `base`, and `data` properties; use it to define how IDs are generated.

For a collection loading JSON files from `./src/data/authors`, configure your content collection like this:

```ts
import { glob } from 'astro/loaders';
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';

const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({
    pattern: '**/*.json',
    base: './src/data/authors',
    generateId: ({ entry }) => entry.replace(/\.json$/, ''),
  }),
});

export const collections = {
  authors,
};
```

This removes the `.json` extension from filenames while preserving case.

To load a content collection from a CSV file using the `file()` loader, provide a custom `parser` function in the loader options that converts the CSV text into an array of objects with unique `id` fields. The parser can be async if needed, such as when fetching data from a remote source.

Example:
```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { file } from "astro/loaders";
import { parse as parseCsv } from "csv-parse/sync";

const cats = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/cats.csv", {
    parser: (text) => parseCsv(text, { columns: true, skipEmptyLines: true }),
  }),
});
```

For async parsing, wrap the parser logic in an `async` function and use `await` if fetching from a remote source. The loader will handle the async nature of your parser automatically.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/#parser

Use the `file()` loader with a custom `parser` function in `src/content.config.ts` to extract specific arrays from your JSON file and define separate collections for each.

```ts
import { file } from "astro/loaders";
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";

const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/pets.json", {
    parser: (text) => JSON.parse(text).dogs,
  }),
});

const cats = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/pets.json", {
    parser: (text) => JSON.parse(text).cats,
  }),
});

export const collections = { dogs, cats };
```

This splits the `pets.json` file into two distinct content collections based on the `dogs` and `cats` keys. Each entry must have a unique `id` property.

