You can define references in your blog collection schema using `reference()` to link to entries in another collection or itself. In your case, you need a single author reference and an array of related posts from the same collection.

Here’s how to set up the schema:

```ts
import { defineCollection, reference } from "astro:content";
import { z } from "astro/zod";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    author: reference("authors"),
    relatedPosts: z.array(reference("blog")),
  }),
});

const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.json", base: "./src/data/authors" }),
  schema: z.object({
    name: z.string(),
    portfolio: z.url(),
  }),
});

export const collections = { blog, authors };
```

In your Markdown frontmatter, reference entries by their `id`:

```yaml
---
title: "Welcome to my blog"
author: ben-holmes
relatedPosts:
  - about-me
  - my-year-in-review
---
```

The referenced values in the entry data will be transformed into objects with a `collection` and `id` key, like this:

```ts
{
  author: { collection: "authors", id: "ben-holmes" },
  relatedPosts: [
    { collection: "blog", id: "about-me" },
    { collection: "blog", id: "my-year-in-review" }
  ]
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#defining-collection-references

The ordering of `getCollection("blog")` is unstable because Astro does not guarantee a consistent sort order when no explicit sorting is applied. To ensure posts are consistently sorted by date (newest first), you must explicitly sort the collection.

Use `sort()` on the array returned by `getCollection("blog")`, typically sorting by `pubDate` in descending order:

```astro
---
const allPosts = await getCollection("blog");
const sortedPosts = allPosts.sort((a, b) => {
  return new Date(b.data.pubDate) - new Date(a.data.pubDate);
});
---
```

This ensures consistent ordering across deploys. The `pubDate` field from frontmatter is used for sorting.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/#getcollection

TypeScript infers `price: number` from the schema passed to `defineLiveCollection()`, overriding the loader’s type. At runtime, Astro validates the loader’s response against the schema, not its own types.

Example:
```ts
// In your live loader
const entry = await getLiveEntry('products', '123');
entry.data.price; // TypeScript infers number

// Schema in src/live.config.ts
defineLiveCollection({
  loader: productLoader(...),
  schema: z.object({ price: z.number() }),
});
```

