You have two options for generating routes from your build-time content collection: prerender all pages at build time or render pages dynamically on demand.

Prerendered pages are generated during the build process and served directly from disk. This is ideal when you want fast response times and don't mind longer build times or increased memory usage. It's suitable for smaller sites where build performance isn't a concern.

On-demand rendering generates pages only when first requested, which is useful for large collections like yours (30,000 entries). This approach reduces initial build time and resource consumption but may introduce latency on the first request for each page.

Use on-demand rendering when:
- Build times become prohibitively long
- You're dealing with a very large number of pages (>10k)
- Memory usage during builds is an issue

Example setup using `getEntry()` in a dynamic route:

```astro
---
export const prerender = false;
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';

const { id } = Astro.params;
if (!id) return Astro.redirect('/404');

const entry = await getEntry('docs', id);
if (!entry) return Astro.redirect('/404');
---
<h1>{entry.data.title}</h1>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

To define a build-time `blog` collection using the `glob()` loader that matches `.md` and `.mdx` files in `./src/content/blog`, with required frontmatter fields (`title`, `description`, `pubDate`), you need two files:

1. **`src/content.config.ts`** — defines the collection schema and loader.
2. **A Markdown file like `src/content/blog/my-post.md`** — contains the required frontmatter.

### File: `src/content.config.ts`

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { glob } from 'astro/loaders';

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: '**/*.{md,mdx}', base: './src/content/blog' }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    description: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
  }),
});

export const collections = { blog };
```

### File: `src/content/blog/my-post.md`

```md
---
title: "My Blog Post"
description: "A brief description of the post."
pubDate: 2023-06-01T00:00:00Z
---

Post content here.
```

This setup ensures Astro validates and types your blog posts at build time. The `collections` export makes the collection available in your project.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

To generate a static page for each Markdown post in `src/blog` with routes like `/posts/<id>`, your `src/pages/[id].astro` file needs to use `getStaticPaths()` to map collection entries to routes, and render the content using `render()`.

The frontmatter of `src/pages/[id].astro` should import `getCollection` and `render`, define a `getStaticPaths()` function that fetches posts from the `blog` collection, and pass each post as a prop. The component then uses the `post` prop to render its title and content.

Example:

```astro
---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await getCollection('blog');
  return posts.map((post) => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
    props: { post },
  }));
}

const { post } = Astro.props;
const { Content } = await render(post);
---

<h1>{post.data.title}</h1>
<Content />
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

Your dynamic route filename must use a rest parameter (`[...id]`) to match paths with slashes in the `params` value.

For example, if your blog entries have IDs like `guides/install`, your route file should be named `src/pages/posts/[...id].astro`. This allows the route to capture the full path segment including slashes. Using a regular parameter `[id]` only matches a single path segment and will not match paths with slashes.

Example:
```astro
// src/pages/posts/[...id].astro
---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await getCollection('blog');
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
    props: { post },
  }));
}

const { post } = Astro.props;
---
<h1>{post.data.title}</h1>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/#rest-parameters

You can distinguish between a missing product and other errors by checking the error type using `instanceof`. Import `LiveEntryNotFoundError` from `"astro/content/runtime"` and use it to detect when a product is not found.

```astro
---export const prerender = false;
import { LiveEntryNotFoundError } from "astro/content/runtime";
import { getLiveEntry } from "astro:content";

const { entry, error } = await getLiveEntry("products", Astro.params.id);

if (error) {
  if (error instanceof LiveEntryNotFoundError) {
    console.error(`Product not found: ${error.message}`);
    Astro.response.status = 404;
  } else {
    console.error(`Error loading product: ${error.message}`);
    return Astro.redirect("/500");
  }
}
---
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#error-handling

No, defining `getStaticPaths()` in `src/pages/posts/[id].astro` is not the right approach if you want routes to be resolved at request time. `getStaticPaths()` generates prerendered pages at build time, which contradicts on-demand rendering.

Instead, set `export const prerender = false` and use `getEntry()` or `getLiveEntry()` to fetch content dynamically based on `Astro.params.id`.

Example:
```astro
---
export const prerender = false;
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';

const { id } = Astro.params;
if (id === undefined) {
  return Astro.redirect('/404');
}

const post = await getEntry('blog', id);
if (post === undefined) {
  return Astro.redirect('/404');
}
---

<h1>{post.data.title}</h1>
{post.content}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#generating-routes-from-content

`getLiveCollection()` and `getLiveEntry()` can surface `AstroError` objects for validation failures. When collection data doesn't match the defined Zod schema, you receive a `LiveCollectionValidationError`.

Example:
```ts
import { LiveCollectionValidationError } from 'astro/content/runtime';
const { entry, error } = await getLiveEntry('products', '123');
if (LiveCollectionValidationError.is(error)) {
  console.error(error.message);
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/error-reference/

In Astro, `fetch()` calls in components execute at build time during static generation, unless the component is marked as an island or SSR is enabled. When using `getStaticPaths()` with `cacheKey`, pages are skipped if unchanged, but their `fetch()` calls still run once per build.

With SSR enabled (`ssr: true`), `fetch()` calls execute on the server during request time, not at build time. This allows dynamic data fetching for each visitor.

Example:
```astro
---
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts').then(r => r.json());
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
    props: { post },
    cacheKey: post.updatedAt,
  }));
}
---
```
This runs at build time for static builds. With SSR, the same code would run on each request.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/experimental-flags/incremental-build/

Astro components using `fetch()` in prerendered pages will return stale data because the fetch occurs at build time, not runtime. The fetched data is cached and does not update on subsequent requests unless you use on-demand rendering or implement a custom solution like a server endpoint that refreshes data dynamically.

To avoid this issue, consider:

1. Using `client:visible` directive to defer fetching until client-side
2. Implementing an API route that fetches fresh data each time it's called
3. Setting up a static site generator with incremental builds or using Astro’s on-demand rendering

Example of a server endpoint for dynamic data:
```ts
// src/pages/api/data.ts
export async function GET() {
  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
  return new Response(await response.text(), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/data-fetching/

To generate prerendered tag pages at build time in Astro 7, use `getStaticPaths()` to dynamically compute the unique set of tags from your blog posts.

In `src/pages/tags/[tag].astro`, import all Markdown posts using `import.meta.glob` with `{ eager: true }`. Map over these posts to extract and flatten their `tags` arrays into a single list. Use `new Set()` to deduplicate the tags, then convert back to an array. Return this array as `params` in `getStaticPaths()`, where each object contains a `tag` param.

Example:
```astro
---
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const allPosts = Object.values(import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', { eager: true }));
  const uniqueTags = [...new Set(allPosts.map((post) => post.frontmatter.tags).flat())];
  return uniqueTags.map((tag) => ({
    params: { tag },
  }));
}
const { tag } = Astro.params;
---
<p>Posts tagged with {tag}</p>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/tutorial/5-astro-api/2/

You should derive tags from your existing blog collection rather than creating a separate "tags" content collection.

In Astro 7, content collections are designed to represent distinct logical entities with their own data structures and use cases. A tag is not an independent entity but rather a property of a blog post entry. Using `getCollection("blog")` allows you to access all tags through the `post.data.tags` array and generate dynamic routes for each unique tag.

This approach avoids redundancy, keeps your content consistent, and leverages Astro's built-in capabilities for handling collections efficiently.

Example:
```astro
--- 
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const allPosts = await getCollection("blog");
  const uniqueTags = [...new Set(allPosts.map((post) => post.data.tags).flat())];

  return uniqueTags.map((tag) => ({
    params: { tag },
    props: { posts: allPosts.filter(post => post.data.tags.includes(tag)) }
  }));
}

const { tag } = Astro.params;
const { posts } = Astro.props;
---
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

To serve the first page of your paginated blog from `/blog/` instead of `/blog/1/`, rename your route file from `src/pages/blog/[page].astro` to `src/pages/blog.astro`.

This change makes Astro treat `/blog/` as the canonical path for the first page, while still allowing pagination links to generate subsequent pages like `/blog/2/`, `/blog/3/`, etc.

Example:
```astro
// src/pages/blog.astro
---
const { page } = Astro.params;
const postsPerPage = 5;
const allPosts = await import.meta.glob('../../content/posts/*.md');
const postCount = Object.keys(allPosts).length;
const totalPages = Math.ceil(postCount / postsPerPage);
const currentPage = parseInt(page) || 1;
// ... pagination logic
---
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/tutorial/2-pages/2/

To ensure consistent tag handling, normalize all tags to lowercase when generating the `uniqueTags` array and when filtering posts. Update your `getStaticPaths` function like this:

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const allPosts = Object.values(import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', { eager: true }));
  const uniqueTags = [...new Set(allPosts.flatMap((post) => post.frontmatter.tags.map(tag => tag.toLowerCase())))];

  return uniqueTags.map((tag) => {
    const filteredPosts = allPosts.filter((post) => 
      post.frontmatter.tags.some(t => t.toLowerCase() === tag)
    );
    return {
      params: { tag },
      props: { posts: filteredPosts },
    };
  });
}
```

Update your layout to also normalize tags:

```astro
<div class="tags">
  {frontmatter.tags.map((tag: string) => (
    <p class="tag"><a href={`/tags/${tag.toLowerCase()}`}>{tag}</a></p>
  ))}
</div>
```

This ensures all tag pages are generated with lowercase names and links point to the correct normalized tag.

A framework component’s `fetch()` call logs to both the CLI and browser console because the component renders on the server first (during SSR) and then again in the browser. During server rendering, `fetch()` executes normally, logging to the CLI. When the component hydrates in the browser, any client-side `fetch()` calls will log to the browser console.

This behavior is consistent with how Astro handles framework components: they render on the server for static HTML generation and then re-render on the client when hydrated, triggering both server and client logs.

Example:
```jsx
// MyComponent.jsx
export default function MyComponent() {
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/data').then(res => res.json()).then(data => console.log(data));
  }, []);
  return <div>Hello</div>;
}
```
When used with `client:visible`, the component logs once on the server and again in the browser.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/framework-components/

To run `fetch()` calls at request time on the server, you need to enable **on-demand rendering** by setting `export const prerender = false` on the page or endpoint where the fetch occurs. This can be combined with using an adapter for server-side execution.

Alternatively, configure your project’s output mode to `'server'` in `astro.config.mjs`, which makes all pages render on demand by default. Then use `export const prerender = true` only on pages that should remain static.

Example:
```ts
// src/pages/api/data.ts
export const prerender = false;
export async function GET() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(await res.json()));
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/on-demand-rendering/

When you add a `client:*` directive like `client:load` to a Preact component, Astro renders that component on the server at build time (so the CLI log appears), but it also hydrates the component client-side. The JavaScript code inside the component runs again in the browser when the component is hydrated.

If you're not seeing the console log in the browser, ensure your `client:*` directive is correctly applied to a framework component and that the component actually gets hydrated (i.e., it's rendered on the page). Also confirm that the fetch call happens inside the component's script block or function, so it executes during hydration.

Example:
```tsx
// src/components/MyComponent.tsx
import type { FunctionalComponent } from 'preact';

const data = await fetch('https://example.com/data.json').then(res => res.json());
console.log(data); // This logs in the browser when hydrated

const MyComponent: FunctionalComponent = () => {
  return <div>Content</div>;
};

export default MyComponent;
```

Then use it with `client:load`:
```astro
<MyComponent client:load />
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/data-fetching/

For a widget that fetches a remote JSON feed periodically, Astro recommends using [client-side JavaScript](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/client-side-scripts/) within an `.astro` file to handle the `fetch()` logic. You can use the `<script>` tag with `type="module"` and run the polling logic inside a `useEffect`-like pattern via `onMount`.

Example:

```astro
---
// src/pages/feed.astro
let data: any = null;
const fetchData = async () => {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data.json');
  data = await res.json();
};
fetchData(); // Initial load
setInterval(fetchData, 5000); // Poll every 5 seconds
---

<script type="module">
  import { onMount } from 'astro:client';
  onMount(() => {
    const interval = setInterval(async () => {
      const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data.json');
      data = await response.json();
      // Update DOM here if needed
    }, 5000);
    return () => clearInterval(interval);
  });
</script>

{data ? <pre>{JSON.stringify(data, null, 2)}</pre> : <p>Loading...</p>}
```

Astro does not recommend using server-side rendering or static generation for such dynamic polling behavior. Instead, keep the logic client-side to avoid unnecessary server load and ensure real-time updates.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/client-side-scripts/

When using `fetch()` to make a GraphQL query with variables in Astro, the request body must be a JSON object containing a `query` string and a `variables` object. The `query` should include the full GraphQL operation (e.g., a query or mutation), and `variables` should hold the data needed for that operation.

Example:

```ts
const response = await fetch("https://example.com/graphql", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    query: `
      query GetFilm($id: ID!) {
        film(id: $id) {
          title
          releaseDate
        }
      }
    `,
    variables: {
      id: "ZmlsbXM6MQ=="
    }
  })
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/data-fetching/

This behavior occurs because Astro prerenders pages at build time, not runtime. If your page fetches data from an external API like `randomuser.me` during prerendering, it will only execute once and cache that single result. Visitors see the same cached response instead of fresh data on each visit.

To fix this, use server-side rendering (SSR) or client-side fetching to ensure dynamic data is fetched per request:

```astro
---
// src/pages/index.astro
const response = await fetch('https://randomuser.me/api/');
const { results } = await response.json();
const user = results[0];
---
<h1>{user.name.first}</h1>
```

Set `output: 'server'` in your Astro config to enable SSR for dynamic pages.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/concepts/why-astro/#developer-focused

To generate a static page for each article from your headless CMS, use `getStaticPaths()` in the dynamic route file `src/pages/blog/[slug].astro`. This function fetches the list of articles and maps them to routes using their slugs.

Example:
```astro
---
import fetchApi from '../../lib/strapi';
import type Article from '../../interfaces/article';

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const articles = await fetchApi<Article[]>({
    endpoint: 'articles',
    wrappedByKey: 'data',
  });
  return articles.map((article) => ({
    params: { slug: article.slug },
    props: article,
  }));
}

type Props = Article;
const article = Astro.props;
---
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/#getstaticpaths

No, you do not need to install or configure a GraphQL client library like Apollo to query a GraphQL API from an Astro component. Astro provides built-in data fetching capabilities using standard JavaScript and HTTP requests.

You can use `fetch()` or other HTTP clients directly within your Astro components to interact with GraphQL APIs. For example:

```astro
---
const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/graphql', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    query: '{ posts { title } }'
  })
});
const data = await response.json();
---
<h1>{data.posts[0].title}</h1>
```

This approach leverages native browser APIs and does not require additional GraphQL libraries. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/data-fetching/

JavaScript written in a component’s frontmatter runs at build time, while JavaScript inside a `<script>` tag runs in the browser at runtime.

When you place `fetch()` in the frontmatter, it executes during site generation and is used to fetch data for static rendering. The fetched data becomes part of the pre-rendered HTML sent to the browser.

In contrast, placing `fetch()` inside a `<script>` tag means the code runs client-side after the page loads. This approach allows dynamic behavior like fetching data based on user actions or environment-specific conditions that aren't known at build time.

Example:
```astro
---
// frontmatter - runs at build-time
const data = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data').then(res => res.json());
---

<script>
  // runs client-side
  window.addEventListener('load', async () => {
    const response = await fetch('/api/data');
    const result = await response.json();
  });
</script>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/tutorial/3-components/4/
